window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["mphil-cp-2021"] = {"id": "mphil-cp-2021", "title": "M.Phil CP Entrance 2021 (college not printed)", "fullTitle": "M.Phil Clinical Psychology Entrance Examination 2021, booklet A (real paper, the college is not printed on it)", "sub": "70 real questions · 1 mark each, no negative marking · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social and Health Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "The tendency to attribute one's own feelings and thoughts to an external object is known as", "options": {"a": "Personification", "b": "Displacement", "c": "Cathexis", "d": "Projection"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the tendency to attribute one's own feelings and thoughts to an outside person or object is projection. Projection is one of the ego defence mechanisms described by Sigmund Freud and later catalogued by his daughter Anna Freud in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936). A defence mechanism is an unconscious strategy the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety. In projection, a wish, impulse or feeling that a person cannot accept in himself or herself is pushed outward and seen as belonging to someone else; the person then reacts to it 'out there' instead of facing it inside. In its extreme form it is seen in paranoid thinking, where one's own hostility is experienced as persecution by others. A broader sense of the word underlies projective tests: Lawrence K. Frank (1939) coined 'projective methods' for tests in which people read their inner needs into ambiguous material. For example, Arjun secretly resents a colleague's promotion, but he tells everyone that the colleague is jealous of him and is trying to undermine him. Option d is right because attributing one's own feelings and thoughts to something external is the textbook definition of projection. Exam trap: displacement also shifts an impulse onto another target, but it changes the target, not the owner; if Arjun displaced, he would shout at his younger brother instead of the colleague, while the anger would still be felt as his own.", "topic": "Projection as a defence mechanism", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Impaired language comprehension following a stroke would most probably be diagnosed as", "options": {"a": "Dyslexia", "b": "Aphagia", "c": "Wernicke's aphasia", "d": "Broca's aphasia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: impaired language comprehension after a stroke points to Wernicke's aphasia. Aphasia is a loss of language caused by brain damage, most often a stroke in the left hemisphere, where language is lateralised in most people. Carl Wernicke (1874) described patients with damage to the back of the left superior temporal gyrus (Wernicke's area) who could not understand speech. Their own speech stays fluent, with normal rhythm and largely intact grammar, but it is empty and full of wrong or invented words (paraphasias and neologisms; severe cases are called jargon aphasia), and they are often unaware of their errors. Paul Broca (1861) had earlier described the opposite pattern after damage to the left inferior frontal gyrus (Broca's area): slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with comprehension largely spared. A simple rule: Broca's is a problem of producing language (front, motor), Wernicke's is a problem of understanding it (back, sensory). For example, after a stroke Meera's grandfather talks quickly and easily, but his sentences make no sense and he cannot follow a simple request such as 'close your eyes'. Option c is right because poor comprehension with fluent speech is the hallmark of Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia. Exam trap: Broca's aphasia is the best known aphasia, so students pick it for any language loss; but Broca's patients understand well and struggle to speak. The printed 'Aphagia' is also a lure: it looks like aphasia but means a swallowing problem.", "topic": "Wernicke's versus Broca's aphasia", "fig": "Carl Wernicke"}, {"q": "Which of the following stages postulated by Erikson corresponds most closely to Freud's oral stage of development?", "options": {"a": "Trust vs mistrust", "b": "Initiative vs guilt", "c": "Integrity vs despair", "d": "Autonomy vs shame"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: trust versus mistrust is the Erikson stage that matches Sigmund Freud's oral stage. Erik Erikson, in Childhood and Society (1950), recast Freud's psychosexual stages as eight psychosocial stages covering the whole life span. Each stage centres on a crisis between the person's needs and the demands of the social world, and a good resolution leaves a lasting strength or virtue. The first stage, trust versus mistrust, runs from birth to about 1 year (some texts say 18 months), the same period as Freud's oral stage, when pleasure centres on the mouth and the baby depends completely on a caregiver for feeding. Erikson even labelled this stage 'oral-sensory'. Where Freud stressed oral gratification, Erikson stressed the relationship: reliable feeding, comfort and contact teach the infant that the world is dependable, and the virtue of hope emerges; neglect or inconsistency breeds mistrust. For example, baby Anaya, whose mother comes promptly when she cries, learns to expect that her needs will be met. The first four stages map onto Freud's: trust (oral), autonomy versus shame and doubt (anal), initiative versus guilt (phallic) and industry versus inferiority (latency). Option a is right because trust versus mistrust covers the same first year of feeding and dependence as the oral stage. Exam trap: autonomy versus shame and doubt is the second stage and matches Freud's anal stage (toilet training), so a one-step slip is the usual error.", "topic": "Erikson's stages and Freud's", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "What three styles of parenting did Baumrind identify?", "options": {"a": "Autocratic, democratic, laissez-faire", "b": "Authoritarian, authoritative, permissive", "c": "Preconventional, conventional, postconventional", "d": "Moving against, moving toward, moving away from"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Diana Baumrind identified authoritarian, authoritative and permissive parenting. Diana Baumrind (1966, 1967) observed preschool children and their parents at home and in the laboratory, and sorted parents on two broad dimensions: how demanding and controlling they are, and how warm and responsive they are. Authoritarian parents are high in control and low in warmth: strict rules, obedience demanded, little explanation ('because I said so'). Authoritative parents are high in both: firm, clear limits together with warmth, reasons and give-and-take; their children were the most self-reliant, self-controlled and content. Permissive parents are warm but make few demands and set few limits. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) later split low-control parenting into indulgent (permissive) and neglectful or uninvolved, giving the four-style model taught today. For example, when 12-year-old Kabir wants to stay out late, an authoritarian parent says 'no, and don't argue', an authoritative parent explains why 9 pm is the limit and listens to his case, and a permissive parent says 'come home whenever you like'. Option b is right because these three are exactly the styles in Baumrind's original work. Exam trap: autocratic, democratic and laissez-faire sound like parenting styles, and authoritative parenting is often explained as 'democratic', but that trio is Kurt Lewin's leadership styles. Also, authoritarian and authoritative are easy to swap: authoritative is the warm one.", "topic": "Baumrind's parenting styles", "fig": "Diana Baumrind"}, {"q": "A person sees an accident, runs away, and feels afraid. This sequence is explicitly predicted by which of the following theories?", "options": {"a": "Sheldon's theory", "b": "Cannon-Bard theory", "c": "Signal detection theory", "d": "James-Lange theory"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: seeing an accident, running, and then feeling afraid is the sequence predicted by the James-Lange theory. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) independently proposed that emotion follows the body, not the other way round. First we perceive an exciting event, then the body responds automatically (running, a racing heart, trembling), and the felt emotion is our perception of those bodily changes. In James's own words, we are 'afraid because we tremble', not the reverse; textbooks often put it as 'we are afraid because we run'. Common sense says the opposite: see the accident, feel fear, then run. The theory implies that each emotion has its own pattern of bodily change. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked it: the viscera respond too slowly and too similarly across emotions to tell them apart, and animals whose viscera were surgically cut off from the brain still showed emotional behaviour. Cannon and Philip Bard proposed instead that the thalamus sends signals at the same time to the cortex (the feeling) and to the body (the arousal). Option d is right because the stem places running before fear, which is exactly the body-first order of the James-Lange theory. Exam trap: the Cannon-Bard theory also involves bodily arousal, but it says fear and running happen together and independently; any stem where the bodily reaction comes first and the feeling follows is James-Lange.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion", "fig": "William James and Carl Lange"}, {"q": "A young child who does not smile at people and who is indifferent to cuddling exhibits behavior typical of", "options": {"a": "Enuresis", "b": "Separation anxiety disorder", "c": "Autistic disorder", "d": "Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: a young child who does not smile at people and is indifferent to cuddling shows behaviour typical of autistic disorder. Leo Kanner (1943) first described 11 children with 'autistic disturbances of affective contact': from early life they showed little interest in people, poor eye contact, no social smile, no reaching out to be picked up, and an anxious insistence on sameness. Parents often reported that such babies did not mould to the body when held, or stiffened and seemed indifferent to being cuddled. DSM-IV (1994) called the condition autistic disorder, one of the pervasive developmental disorders, with three groups of features: impaired social interaction, impaired communication, and restricted, repetitive behaviour and interests. DSM-5 (2013) merged it with Asperger's disorder and related conditions into autism spectrum disorder, with two domains: social communication and interaction, and restricted, repetitive behaviour, both present from early childhood. For example, two-year-old Vihaan does not smile back when his grandmother smiles, does not look up when called, wriggles away from hugs and spends hours lining up toy cars. Option c is right because a lack of social smiling and indifference to physical affection are core signs of impaired social reciprocity in autism. Exam trap: separation anxiety disorder also concerns the child's bond with caregivers, but it is the reverse picture: the child is strongly attached and becomes distressed when parted, rather than indifferent to closeness.", "topic": "Early social signs of autism", "fig": "Leo Kanner"}, {"q": "The primary feature of catatonic schizophrenia is", "options": {"a": "Motor abnormalities", "b": "Auditory hallucinations", "c": "Inappropriate affect", "d": "Paranoia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the primary feature of catatonic schizophrenia is motor abnormality. Catatonia is a marked disturbance of psychomotor behaviour. Karl Kahlbaum (1874) first described it as a separate illness, and Emil Kraepelin (1899) later folded it into dementia praecox, so catatonic schizophrenia became one of the classic subtypes in DSM-IV and ICD-10. The signs sit at two extremes. At one end is stupor: the person is mute, motionless and unresponsive, may hold odd postures for hours (posturing, catalepsy), and may let the examiner mould the limbs into positions that are then kept, like wax (waxy flexibility). Negativism, resisting or ignoring every instruction, belongs here too. At the other end is catatonic excitement: purposeless, frenzied overactivity. Other signs include stereotypies, mannerisms, grimacing, echolalia (repeating others' words) and echopraxia (copying others' movements). DSM-5 (2013) dropped the schizophrenia subtypes; catatonia is now a specifier that can attach to schizophrenia, mood disorders or medical conditions, and it needs at least three of twelve signs. For example, Ravi stands frozen in one corner of the ward for hours, does not speak, and keeps his arm raised in the air after the doctor lifts it. Option a is right because catatonia is defined by disturbed movement and posture, not by the content of thought or perception. Exam trap: hallucinations and paranoia were the hallmark of the old paranoid subtype, and inappropriate affect belonged to the old disorganised (hebephrenic) subtype; examiners mix up the signature features of the subtypes.", "topic": "Catatonia: motor signs", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The technique used to extinguish fear by forcing the patient to remain in the anxiety-arousing situation is called", "options": {"a": "Inhibition", "b": "Flooding", "c": "Desensitization", "d": "Implosion"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the technique that extinguishes fear by making the patient remain in the anxiety-arousing situation is flooding. Flooding is a form of exposure therapy. The client is exposed, usually in real life (in vivo), to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, all at once, and is prevented from escaping. It rests on classical extinction: when the conditioned stimulus (the feared object) occurs again and again without the feared outcome, the conditioned fear response weakens. Escape normally keeps fear alive. In the two-factor theory of Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947), fears are learnt by classical conditioning and maintained by operant avoidance, because each escape is rewarded by relief, so the person never learns that the danger is not real. When escape is blocked, anxiety rises to a peak and then falls through habituation, and the client learns that nothing terrible happens. Sessions are long, because stopping while anxiety is still high could strengthen the fear. For example, Ishaan, who is terrified of lifts, rides a lift up and down a tall building with his therapist for two hours; his panic peaks in the first 20 minutes and then slowly fades. Option b is right because keeping the client in the feared situation, with no escape, until the fear extinguishes is the definition of flooding. Exam trap: systematic desensitization is also exposure-based, but it is gradual and paired with relaxation, whereas flooding starts at the top of the fear hierarchy with no relaxation. Key note: implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl and Donald Levis, 1967) also keeps the client in intense fear, but in imagination, with exaggerated scenes; because the stem speaks of remaining in the anxiety-arousing situation itself, it describes flooding, and the answer b stands.", "topic": "Flooding versus implosion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Clinical Psychology deals with the practical aspect of", "options": {"a": "Abnormal Psychology", "b": "Educational Psychology", "c": "Child Psychology", "d": "Experimental Psychology"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: clinical psychology deals with the practical, applied side of abnormal psychology. Abnormal psychology (psychopathology) is the scientific study of disordered behaviour and experience: what the disorders are, how they are classified and what causes them. Clinical psychology takes that knowledge into practice: the assessment, diagnosis, treatment and prevention of psychological disorders and distress, together with research on these. Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896, to help children with learning and behaviour problems, and he first used the term 'clinical psychology' in print in 1907, in his new journal The Psychological Clinic. Put simply, abnormal psychology asks 'what is wrong, and why?', while clinical psychology asks 'how do we assess and help this person?'. For example, Riya learns the symptoms and causes of obsessive-compulsive disorder in her abnormal psychology course; in her clinical training she learns to rate the symptoms on the Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale, make a diagnosis and carry out exposure and response prevention with a patient. Option a is right because clinical psychology is the applied branch that puts the knowledge of abnormal psychology to work with real patients. Exam trap: child psychology and educational psychology also have applied wings, and Witmer's first clients were children with school problems, which tempts students; but clinical psychology as a whole is defined by its link to psychopathology, not by an age group or a setting.", "topic": "Clinical and abnormal psychology", "fig": "Lightner Witmer"}, {"q": "Confabulation is often a symptom of", "options": {"a": "Obsessive neurosis", "b": "Korsakoff's syndrome", "c": "Schizophrenia", "d": "Bipolar disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: confabulation is a classic symptom of Korsakoff's syndrome. Confabulation is the honest production of false memories: the person fills gaps in memory with invented or misplaced stories and believes them, with no intent to deceive, which separates it from lying or malingering. Sergei Korsakoff (1887) described the syndrome in people with long-term heavy drinking. The cause is a deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1), from poor diet and poor absorption in chronic alcohol use, which damages the mammillary bodies and the mediodorsal (dorsomedial) thalamus. It often follows Wernicke's encephalopathy (Carl Wernicke, 1881), an acute state of confusion, ataxia and eye movement problems such as nystagmus and ophthalmoplegia; together they are called Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. Korsakoff's syndrome brings severe anterograde amnesia (new events cannot be stored), some retrograde amnesia, and confabulation, while attention and general intelligence are relatively spared. For example, Ramesh, a long-term drinker who has been on the ward for weeks, tells the doctor warmly that he went to the market this morning and met his brother there; he is not lying, he simply cannot remember and fills the gap. Option b is right because confabulation, together with anterograde amnesia, is the hallmark of Korsakoff's syndrome. Exam trap: schizophrenia also produces false beliefs and odd stories, but those are delusions, fixed false beliefs held against evidence, whereas confabulation is a memory symptom that fills gaps and often changes from one telling to the next.", "topic": "Confabulation in Korsakoff's syndrome", "fig": "Sergei Korsakoff"}, {"q": "Which of these illnesses is related to abnormality in the dopaminergic system?", "options": {"a": "Schizophrenia only", "b": "Parkinson's disease only", "c": "Alzheimer's disease only", "d": "Parkinson's disease and schizophrenia"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: both Parkinson's disease and schizophrenia are linked to abnormality in the dopaminergic system. Dopamine is a catecholamine neurotransmitter that travels along several major pathways. In Parkinson's disease, the dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra die, so the nigrostriatal pathway to the striatum (caudate and putamen) loses its dopamine. The result is tremor at rest, rigidity, slowed movement (bradykinesia) and unstable posture. Treatment replaces the missing transmitter with L-dopa, a precursor that crosses the blood-brain barrier. Arvid Carlsson (Nobel Prize 2000) showed that dopamine is a neurotransmitter, and in 1963, with Margit Lindqvist, proposed that antipsychotic drugs work by blocking catecholamine (monoamine) receptors, dopamine among them. This grew into the dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia: overactivity of dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway produces positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. The evidence is that antipsychotics block D2 receptors, their clinical potency tracks how strongly they bind D2, and amphetamine, which releases dopamine, can cause a paranoid psychosis. The two illnesses sit at opposite ends of dopamine activity. For example, when the L-dopa dose of Kabir's grandfather, who has Parkinson's disease, is raised, he begins to see people who are not there; patients given older antipsychotics, in turn, can develop parkinsonian (extrapyramidal) side effects. Option d is right because Parkinson's disease involves too little dopamine and schizophrenia too much, so both are dopaminergic disorders. Exam trap: options a and b are each true but incomplete, and the word 'only' makes them wrong; Alzheimer's disease is the lure for neurodegeneration, but its core transmitter loss is acetylcholine.", "topic": "Dopamine in Parkinson's and schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A child with a mental age of 10.7 and a chronological age of 9.5 would have a ratio IQ of about", "options": {"a": "132", "b": "125", "c": "113", "d": "103"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: a child with a mental age of 10.7 and a chronological age of 9.5 has a ratio IQ of about 113. The formula is ratio IQ = (mental age / chronological age) x 100. Here 10.7 / 9.5 = 1.126, and 1.126 x 100 = 112.6, which rounds to 113. A quick check: 9.5 x 1.13 is about 10.7. Mental age (MA) is the age level at which a child performs on a test. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905, revised 1908) arranged tasks by the age at which typical children pass them, so a child who passes the tasks of typical ten-year-olds has an MA of 10. William Stern (1912) divided MA by chronological age (CA) to get a quotient, and Lewis Terman multiplied it by 100 in the 1916 Stanford-Binet, giving the intelligence quotient. An IQ of 100 means MA equals CA; above 100 the child is ahead of age, below 100 behind. This child is about 1.2 years ahead, a lead of about 13 percent, so the IQ is a little above 100. The ratio IQ fails for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15), which compares a person with others of the same age. Option c is right because 10.7 divided by 9.5, times 100, gives 112.6, about 113. Exam trap: dividing the wrong way round (9.5 / 10.7 x 100, about 89) is the classic slip; it gives an IQ below 100 for a child who is actually ahead of age.", "topic": "Calculating the ratio IQ", "fig": "William Stern"}, {"q": "Which archetype represents the feminine side of men?", "options": {"a": "Persona", "b": "Anima", "c": "Animus", "d": "Shadow"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the anima is the archetype that represents the feminine side of men. Carl Jung, who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913, divided the unconscious into a personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes) and a deeper collective unconscious shared by all humans and inherited from our ancestral past. Its contents are archetypes: universal, inborn tendencies to form certain images, seen in the recurring figures of myths, dreams and religions across cultures. Jung held that every person carries qualities of the other sex. The anima is the unconscious feminine image in a man, built from humanity's long experience of women; the animus is the unconscious masculine image in a woman. Both shape the choice of partner and are often projected onto a beloved. Other major archetypes are the persona (the social mask we show others), the shadow (the dark, primitive side we refuse to own) and the self (the archetype of wholeness, often symbolised by the mandala, and the goal of individuation). For example, Rahul, a tough and practical engineer, keeps dreaming of a wise, gentle woman who guides him; a Jungian analyst would read her as his anima, pointing to tenderness he has not yet developed. Option b is right because anima is Jung's name for the feminine archetype in men. Exam trap: anima and animus are easy to swap; remember that anima, ending in 'a' like many feminine Latin words, is the feminine side, found in men, and animus is the masculine side, found in women.", "topic": "Jung's archetypes: anima", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "The concepts like \"Introspection\" and \"Conscious Experience\" are associated with", "options": {"a": "Functionalism", "b": "Behaviourism", "c": "Structuralism", "d": "Gestalt Psychology"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: introspection and conscious experience are the hallmark concepts of structuralism. Wilhelm Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879 and defined psychology as the study of immediate conscious experience. His English student Edward Titchener took this approach to Cornell University in 1892 and named it structuralism (1898). The aim was to analyse consciousness into its basic elements, as a chemist analyses a compound, and to find the laws by which those elements combine. The method was trained introspection: observers, drilled through thousands of trials, reported the raw sensations, images and feelings of an experience, not its meaning. Describing the object ('I see an apple') instead of the raw qualities (red, round, shiny) was called the stimulus error. Titchener found elements of three kinds: sensations, images and affections (feelings). Structuralism faded after Titchener's death in 1927, because introspective reports could not be checked or agreed between laboratories. For example, Meera, as a trained observer, listens to a tuning fork and reports only pitch, loudness, duration and clearness, never 'a tuning fork'. Option c is right because structuralism is defined by the analysis of conscious experience through introspection. Exam trap: functionalism also studied consciousness and also used introspection, so it is the strongest lure; but functionalists asked what consciousness is for, how it helps us adapt, not what it is made of.", "topic": "Structuralism and introspection", "fig": "Edward Titchener"}, {"q": "Which of the following is based on the principle of classical conditioning?", "options": {"a": "Token economies", "b": "Differential reinforcement", "c": "Systematic desensitization", "d": "Contingency management"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: systematic desensitization is based on the principle of classical conditioning. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, treated a phobia as a conditioned emotional response and argued that a response incompatible with anxiety can replace it: a person cannot be deeply relaxed and anxious at the same moment (reciprocal inhibition). This is counterconditioning, a classical (respondent) procedure, because a new response is attached to the old stimulus by pairing. There are three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually Edmund Jacobson's progressive relaxation (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, from the least to the most frightening situation, rated on Wolpe's Subjective Units of Disturbance scale. Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each step in turn, moving up only when a step no longer causes anxiety. Earlier, Mary Cover Jones (1924) had removed little Peter's fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit gradually closer while he ate, an early counterconditioning case. For example, Arjun, who panics about exams, relaxes and pictures first hearing the exam date, then studying the night before, then entering the hall, and finally opening the paper. Option c is right because desensitization works by pairing feared stimuli with a new, incompatible response (relaxation), not by reinforcing behaviour after it occurs. Exam trap: the other three options are all operant techniques that change behaviour through its consequences, so the item simply tests the classical versus operant split.", "topic": "Systematic desensitization's principle", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "The sound of a sizzling frying pan causes John to salivate. The sound of the sizzling frying pan is", "options": {"a": "Unconditioned stimulus", "b": "Conditioned stimulus", "c": "Unconditioned response", "d": "Conditioned response"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the sound of the sizzling frying pan is a conditioned stimulus. Classical conditioning was discovered by Ivan Pavlov during his studies of digestion in dogs (Nobel Prize 1904 for the physiology of digestion): a neutral stimulus comes to trigger a response after it has been paired repeatedly with a stimulus that triggers the response naturally. Four terms describe the process. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS) produces a response without any learning; here, the food in the mouth. The unconditioned response (UCR) is that unlearned reaction: salivating to food. The conditioned stimulus (CS) starts out neutral and produces no salivation on its own, but after pairing with the UCS it comes to produce the response; here, the sizzle, which John has heard again and again just before eating. The conditioned response (CR) is the learned reaction to the CS: salivating to the sizzle. A newborn would not salivate at a frying pan; John learned to because the sound reliably predicts food. Two quick tests help. First, ask 'would this work without any experience?'; if not, it is conditioned. Second, stimuli are things that happen to the person (sounds, sights, smells), while responses are what the person does (salivating). Option b is right because the sizzle is a once-neutral sound that now triggers salivation only through its learned link with food. Exam trap: the CR and the UCR are both salivation, so students fix on the response; but the question asks about the sound, which is a stimulus, and the only learned stimulus here is the CS.", "topic": "Classical conditioning terms", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "_________ approach is popularly rooted in Gestalt psychology.", "options": {"a": "Wholistic Approach", "b": "Stimulus-Response-Behaviouristic Approach", "c": "Dynamic and Psychoanalytic Approach", "d": "Cognitive Approach"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the holistic approach (printed 'Wholistic') is the one rooted in Gestalt psychology. Gestalt psychology began with Max Wertheimer's (1912) paper on the phi phenomenon, the apparent movement seen when two lights flash one after the other, with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka as co-founders in Germany. Gestalt is German for form, shape or configuration. Its central claim is that the whole is different from the sum of its parts: experience comes organised into meaningful wholes, and breaking it into elements, as the structuralists did, destroys what matters. A melody stays the same melody when played in another key, though every note has changed. From this came the laws of perceptual organisation (proximity, similarity, closure, continuity, figure and ground), insight learning (Kohler's chimpanzee studies, The Mentality of Apes, German edition 1917) and, through Kurt Lewin's work in the 1930s, field theory. 'Holistic' is the general label for any approach that studies experience or the person as an organised whole, an idea later carried into Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman, 1951) and humanistic psychology. For example, Anika sees a ring of dots as a circle, not as twelve separate dots, because her mind organises them into a whole. Option a is right because the whole-before-parts view is exactly what Gestalt psychology stands for. Exam trap: the cognitive approach is tempting because Gestalt work on perception and insight did feed into cognitive psychology, but it grew mainly from information processing and the computer analogy. Key note: some textbooks call Gestalt psychology a forerunner of the cognitive approach, so d is not absurd; but the approach defined by Gestalt's whole-before-parts principle is the holistic one, so the answer a stands.", "topic": "Gestalt and the holistic approach", "fig": "Max Wertheimer"}, {"q": "A subject takes longer naming the color of the ink used when the word green is written in red than when the word button is written in red. This is an example of", "options": {"a": "The Stroop effect", "b": "Automatic processing", "c": "Metacognition", "d": "The opponent-process theory"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: taking longer to name red ink when the word is 'green' than when the word is 'button' is the Stroop effect. John Ridley Stroop (1935) showed that naming the ink colour of a colour word is slower, and more error-prone, when the word names a different colour (the word GREEN printed in red) than when the stimulus is neutral, such as a row of coloured patches or a non-colour word. The effect arises because reading is so practised in literate adults that it happens automatically: the meaning of the word is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, deliberate task of naming the colour. The person must suppress the automatic reading response, and that costs time. Here 'button' carries no colour meaning, so it causes little conflict, while 'green' sets up a clash with red. The task is now a standard measure of selective attention, response inhibition and executive control, sensitive to frontal (anterior cingulate and prefrontal) dysfunction, and an 'emotional Stroop' with threat words is used to study attentional bias in anxiety. Option a is right because the stem describes exactly Stroop's interference between a colour word and a mismatched ink colour. Exam trap: automatic processing is the near-miss, because it is the mechanism that causes the effect; but the question asks what the observation is an example of, and the named phenomenon is the Stroop effect.", "topic": "The Stroop effect", "fig": "John Ridley Stroop"}, {"q": "Schizophrenia was formerly known as", "options": {"a": "Huntington disease", "b": "Dementia praecox", "c": "Pick's disease", "d": "Cretinism"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: schizophrenia was formerly known as dementia praecox. The French psychiatrist Benedict Morel used 'demence precoce' for a mental deterioration beginning in young people. The German psychiatrist Emil Kraepelin took up the Latin form, dementia praecox ('premature dementia'), in the 1893 edition of his textbook. In the sixth edition (1899) he drew a line through the psychoses by their course: dementia praecox began in youth and ran a downhill course towards deterioration, whereas manic-depressive insanity came in episodes with recovery between them. He grouped catatonic, hebephrenic and paranoid forms under dementia praecox. Eugen Bleuler (1908 lecture; 1911 book) renamed it schizophrenia, a splitting of mental functions such as thought, emotion and will (not a split personality), because he saw that the illness did not always start young or end in dementia. Option b is right because dementia praecox is Kraepelin's name for the illness that Bleuler renamed schizophrenia. Exam trap: the other options are also old disease names, and Huntington's and Pick's disease do cause dementia; but they are neurological dementias with known brain pathology, not the early name of a psychotic disorder.", "topic": "Dementia praecox and schizophrenia", "fig": "Emil Kraepelin"}, {"q": "The method of loci is an example of", "options": {"a": "The primacy effect", "b": "The recency effect", "c": "A mnemonic", "d": "A heuristic"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the method of loci is an example of a mnemonic. A mnemonic is any technique that improves memory by adding organisation, meaning or vivid imagery to new material and linking it to something already well known. In the method of loci (Latin locus, 'place'), you first picture a familiar route, such as the rooms of your home, and then 'place' each item to be remembered at a spot along it, ideally as a striking image. To recall, you walk the route in your mind and 'see' each item where you left it. The method is credited to the Greek poet Simonides of Ceos (about 556 to 468 BCE) who, the story goes, identified the guests crushed when a banquet hall collapsed by remembering where each one had been sitting. It works because spatial memory and imagery give strong retrieval cues, and the route keeps the items in order. Other mnemonics include the peg-word system ('one is a bun'), acronyms such as OCEAN for the Big Five, and the keyword method. For example, Kabir must remember milk, eggs, bread and mangoes; he imagines milk pouring over his front door, eggs cracking on the sofa, bread piled on the stairs and mangoes in the bathtub, then walks through his flat in his mind at the shop. Option c is right because the method of loci is a deliberate memory aid, which is what a mnemonic is. Exam trap: primacy and recency are also about remembering lists in order, but they are serial position effects that happen by themselves, not techniques that a person chooses to use.", "topic": "Method of loci as a mnemonic", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Piaget the ability to \"think like a scientist\" is characterized of which of the following developmental stages?", "options": {"a": "Preoperational", "b": "Sensorimotor", "c": "Concrete operational", "d": "Formal operational"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: according to Jean Piaget, the ability to 'think like a scientist' marks the formal operational stage. Piaget described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to about 2), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11 or 12 onwards). In the formal operational stage, thought is freed from concrete reality. The adolescent can reason about abstract ideas and hypothetical possibilities, and can use hypothetico-deductive reasoning: form a hypothesis, deduce what should follow, and test it systematically while holding other factors constant, just as a scientist does. Barbel Inhelder and Jean Piaget (1958), in The Growth of Logical Thinking from Childhood to Adolescence, used the pendulum problem: children had to find what controls how fast a pendulum swings (the length of the string, the weight, the height of release or the force of the push). Concrete operational children changed several factors at once and drew muddled conclusions; formal operational adolescents varied one factor at a time and found that only the length matters. For example, 14-year-old Riya, testing why her plants wilt, waters two identical pots differently while keeping light and soil the same. Not everyone reaches this stage, and many adults use it only in familiar fields. Option d is right because systematic hypothesis testing and abstract reasoning define formal operations. Exam trap: concrete operational children are logical too (they master conservation, classification and seriation), which makes c tempting; but their logic works only on concrete, present objects, not on hypotheses.", "topic": "Piaget's formal operational stage", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "A graphical representation of correlational data is called a", "options": {"a": "Bell curve", "b": "Chi square", "c": "Scatterplot", "d": "Skewed distribution"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a graphical representation of correlational data is called a scatterplot. A scatterplot, or scatter diagram, shows the relationship between two variables measured on the same people. Each person is one dot: the score on one variable (X) sets its horizontal position and the score on the other (Y) sets its vertical position. The pattern of the whole cloud shows the correlation at a glance. If the dots rise from lower left to upper right, the correlation is positive; if they fall, it is negative. The more tightly the dots cluster around a straight line, the stronger the relationship; a round, shapeless cloud means little or no linear relationship. The correlation coefficient r, Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment correlation, puts a number on this, from minus 1 to plus 1. A scatterplot also reveals what r can hide: curved (nonlinear) relationships, outliers and a restricted range. For example, Meera plots hours of study (X) against exam marks (Y) for 30 classmates; the dots slope upwards with moderate spread, suggesting a moderate positive correlation. Even so, correlation never proves causation: more study may raise marks, or keen students may simply do both. Option c is right because the scatterplot is the standard graph for showing the relationship between two sets of paired scores. Exam trap: the bell curve and the skewed distribution are also graphs, but each shows how a single variable is distributed, not how two variables relate.", "topic": "Scatterplot for correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Name the test which measures set shifting?", "options": {"a": "Bender Gestalt test", "b": "Nahor - Benson test", "c": "Wisconsin card sorting test", "d": "Digit span test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test measures set shifting. Set shifting, or cognitive flexibility, is the executive ability to drop a rule or mental set that no longer works and switch to a new one. David Grant and Esta Berg (1948) built the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) around it. Four stimulus cards are laid out (one red triangle, two green stars, three yellow crosses, four blue circles). The person sorts a pack of response cards under them, matching by colour, form or number, but is never told the rule, only 'right' or 'wrong' after each card. After a run of correct sorts (ten in the standard version), the examiner changes the rule without warning. A flexible person notices the feedback and shifts; a person with poor set shifting keeps sorting by the old rule, making perseverative errors. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal lobe excisions made many such errors, so the WCST became the classic test of frontal executive function, widely used in schizophrenia, head injury and frontal lobe damage. For example, after a road accident Aman keeps sorting by colour for twenty cards after the rule has changed to number, even though he hears 'wrong' each time. Option c is right because the WCST's unannounced rule changes measure set shifting directly. Exam trap: all four options are neuropsychological tests, and digit span is linked to attention, so students may pick it; but digit span measures attention span and working memory, not switching between rules.", "topic": "WCST and set shifting", "fig": "David Grant and Esta Berg"}, {"q": "Which of the following type of hallucination is most common in schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "Tactile", "b": "Olfactory", "c": "Auditory", "d": "Visual"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: auditory hallucinations are the most common type of hallucination in schizophrenia. A hallucination is a perception without any external stimulus, experienced as real and as coming from outside (in outer, objective space), unlike an illusion, which is a misreading of a real stimulus. In schizophrenia, hearing voices is by far the most frequent kind, reported by well over half of patients in most studies. The voices may speak to the person (second person: 'you are useless'), talk about the person (third person: 'he is going out now'), comment on what the person is doing, or speak the person's thoughts aloud (thought echo). Kurt Schneider (Clinical Psychopathology, English edition 1959) counted three kinds of voices among his first-rank symptoms: audible thoughts, voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and a running commentary. Visual hallucinations occur in schizophrenia too, but much less often; when they are prominent, clinicians first look for an organic cause such as delirium, drug intoxication or Lewy body dementia. Tactile hallucinations such as formication (insects crawling under the skin) suggest cocaine use or alcohol withdrawal, and olfactory ones suggest temporal lobe epilepsy. For example, Sunil, aged 23, hears two men behind the wall discussing him: 'he is useless, he should leave'; no one is there. Option c is right because voices are the commonest and most characteristic hallucinations of schizophrenia. Exam trap: visual hallucinations are the commonest type in organic states such as delirium, so a question about delirium has a different answer; for schizophrenia the answer is auditory.", "topic": "Hallucinations in schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The act in which people help others without any personal interest, motive, reason is called:", "options": {"a": "Altruism", "b": "Prosocial", "c": "Nurturance", "d": "Social Obligation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: helping others without any personal interest, motive or expected reward is called altruism. The word was coined by the French philosopher Auguste Comte (1798 to 1857) to mean living for others. In social psychology, altruism is helping driven purely by concern for the other person's welfare, with no expectation of reward and often at some cost to the helper. It sits inside a wider category, prosocial behaviour, which covers any voluntary act that benefits others, such as sharing, donating, comforting or rescuing, whatever the motive behind it. So all altruism is prosocial, but much prosocial behaviour is not altruistic: helping to win praise, to repay a favour or to ease one's own guilt is prosocial but self-serving. Whether pure altruism exists is a long debate. C. Daniel Batson (1981) proposed the empathy-altruism hypothesis: when we feel empathy for someone in need, we help for their sake, even when escaping the situation is easy. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1987) replied with the negative-state relief model: we help mainly to lift our own sadness. Example: Meera sees an elderly stranger fall on a crowded Kolkata road, takes her to a clinic and leaves without giving her name; she gains nothing and loses time, so her act is altruism. Option a is right because the stem stresses the absence of any personal interest, motive or reason, which is the defining feature of altruism. Exam trap: 'prosocial' is the near-miss, because altruism is one kind of prosocial behaviour; but prosocial is the broad umbrella that also includes helping for selfish reasons, so it does not capture 'without any motive'.", "topic": "Altruism versus prosocial behaviour", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Emphasizing what comes to mind first or most readily/quickly is known as", "options": {"a": "Heuristic", "b": "Critical Thinking", "c": "Intuitive Thought", "d": "Confirmation Bias"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: judging by what comes to mind first or most readily is a heuristic, specifically the availability heuristic. A heuristic is a mental shortcut, a rule of thumb that gives a quick, usually good enough judgement without weighing all the evidence. Herbert Simon (1955) argued that people with limited time, information and mental capacity cannot weigh every option and settle for choices that are good enough, an idea he later called bounded rationality. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1973) described the availability heuristic: we judge how frequent or likely something is by how easily examples come to mind. In their classic demonstration, people judged that more English words begin with K than have K as the third letter, although the reverse is true, because words starting with K are easier to retrieve. Vivid, recent or heavily reported events are the most available, so people overrate the risk of plane crashes and underrate quieter risks such as diabetes. Example: after watching news of two train accidents, Kabir decides trains are more dangerous than cars and drives to Pune instead, although road travel is far riskier. Option a is right because 'emphasising what comes to mind first or most readily' is the core of heuristic judgement, the availability heuristic. Exam trap: 'intuitive thought' sounds like fast, first-thought judgement, but as a textbook term it is the intuitive substage of preoperational thought described by Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980), at about 4 to 7 years, not the name of this shortcut. Key note: 'intuitive thought' also suggests the fast, automatic System 1 thinking that Daniel Kahneman (2011) describes, and heuristics do belong to System 1; but the stem describes the availability heuristic itself, so a stands.", "topic": "Availability heuristic", "fig": "Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman"}, {"q": "Sex chromosome anomaly (XXY) results into the development of:", "options": {"a": "Klinefelter's Syndrome", "b": "Turner Syndrome", "c": "Down Syndrome", "d": "Phenylketonuria"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: an XXY sex chromosome pattern produces Klinefelter's syndrome. Humans normally have 46 chromosomes: 22 pairs of autosomes and one pair of sex chromosomes, XX in females and XY in males. Klinefelter syndrome, described by the American physician Harry Klinefelter (1942), is a 47,XXY karyotype: a male with an extra X chromosome, usually from nondisjunction (chromosomes failing to separate) when the egg or sperm is formed. Because a Y chromosome is present, the person develops as male, but the extra X lowers testosterone. Typical features are tall stature with long legs, small testes, infertility, sparse body hair, sometimes breast enlargement (gynaecomastia), and often language, reading and learning difficulties, although intelligence is usually in the average range. It is one of the commonest sex chromosome conditions, roughly 1 in 500 to 1,000 male births, and is often found only when an adult is investigated for infertility. Testosterone treatment from puberty helps physical development. Option a is right because 47,XXY is the defining karyotype of Klinefelter syndrome. Exam trap: Turner syndrome is the other famous sex chromosome anomaly and students mix the two up; Turner is 45,X (often written XO), a female with one X missing, the mirror image of an extra X, not XXY.", "topic": "Klinefelter syndrome (XXY)", "fig": "Harry Klinefelter"}, {"q": "Who has explained intelligence in terms of Psychometric Approach?", "options": {"a": "Jensen", "b": "Cattell", "c": "Sternberg", "d": "Binet"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: Alfred Binet is the theorist credited with explaining intelligence through the psychometric approach. The psychometric approach treats intelligence as abilities measured with standardised tests, scored against norms and analysed statistically, especially by factor analysis. Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon (1905), built the first practical intelligence test for Paris schools, to pick out children who needed special teaching. Their 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children pass them, giving the idea of mental age: a child who passes the items most 8-year-olds pass has a mental age of 8. William Stern (1912) turned this into a quotient, and Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet (1916) made the IQ famous. Earlier sensory and reaction-time tests in the style of Francis Galton (1880s) had failed to predict school grades, so Binet's scale was the first useful measure of intelligence. Factor-analytic theories, which study the structure of test scores, belong to the same tradition: Charles Spearman's g (1904), Louis Thurstone's primary mental abilities (1938), Raymond Cattell's fluid and crystallised intelligence (1963) and Arthur Jensen's Level I and Level II abilities (1969). By contrast, Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory (1985) explains intelligence through mental processes, an information-processing approach. Example: when Riya's school psychologist reports her mental age and IQ from a standardised test, she is using the approach that began with Binet. Option d is right because Binet built the first working intelligence test and the idea of mental age, the starting point of the psychometric approach to intelligence. Exam trap: Jensen and Cattell are factor-analytic theorists and look like the 'real' psychometricians, which makes the test-builder Binet seem the odd one out; in fact Binet's test is where the measurement of intelligence began. Key note: Jensen and Cattell are psychometric theorists too, so only Sternberg is clearly outside the approach; Binet is keyed as the one who first explained intelligence through measurement (mental age), and d stands.", "topic": "Psychometric approach to intelligence", "fig": "Alfred Binet"}, {"q": "Paradoxical Intention is based on which of the following technique?", "options": {"a": "CBT", "b": "Logo Therapy", "c": "Existential Therapy", "d": "Gestalt Therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: paradoxical intention is a technique of logotherapy. Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997), a Viennese psychiatrist, built logotherapy (therapy through meaning) on the idea that the deepest human drive is the will to meaning; his book Man's Search for Meaning (1946) drew on his survival of the Nazi concentration camps. He described paradoxical intention in 1939. Many symptoms are kept alive by anticipatory anxiety: the person fears a symptom, the fear brings it on, and the cycle repeats. In paradoxical intention the client is asked to deliberately wish for, or try to produce, the very thing he fears, often with humour. A man who dreads sweating in public is told to try to sweat as much as he can; trying to produce the symptom removes the anxious struggle against it, and the symptom often fades. Frankl's second technique, dereflection, turns attention away from the symptom and towards meaningful goals. Example: Arjun lies awake every night worrying that he will not sleep; his therapist asks him to try as hard as he can to stay awake, and once the pressure to fall asleep is gone, he drifts off. Paradoxical methods were later borrowed by strategic family therapy and by CBT for insomnia, but they began in logotherapy. Option b is right because paradoxical intention is Frankl's own logotherapeutic technique. Exam trap: logotherapy is a form of existential therapy, so 'existential therapy' is the near-miss; the technique belongs specifically to Frankl's logotherapy, which is the more precise answer.", "topic": "Paradoxical intention and logotherapy", "fig": "Viktor Frankl"}, {"q": "Hope, happiness, optimism and flow takes together as", "options": {"a": "Cognitive psychology", "b": "Positive psychology", "c": "Humanistic psychology", "d": "Evolutionary psychology"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: hope, happiness, optimism and flow are studied together as positive psychology. Positive psychology is the scientific study of what makes life go well: positive emotions, strengths of character, and the conditions that let people and communities flourish. Martin Seligman launched it in his 1998 presidential address to the American Psychological Association, arguing that psychology had spent decades on illness and damage and too little on well-being; with Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi he set out its aims in a special issue of the American Psychologist (2000). Each word in the stem is one of its signature topics. Hope theory (C. R. Snyder, 1991) sees hope as goal-directed willpower plus waypower, the ability to find paths to goals. Happiness, studied as subjective well-being by Ed Diener (1984), is life satisfaction with frequent positive and rare negative feelings. Learned optimism (Martin Seligman, 1990) is a habit of explaining bad events as temporary and specific rather than permanent and pervasive. Flow (Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi, 1990) is total absorption in a task whose challenge matches one's skill. Example: when Meera loses all sense of time while practising the sitar, she is in flow, a state that positive psychologists measure and try to increase. Option b is right because hope, happiness, optimism and flow form the core research cluster of positive psychology. Exam trap: humanistic psychology (Abraham Maslow, 1954; Carl Rogers, 1951) also stresses growth and the good life and is a forerunner, but it relied mostly on theory and case study; the research movement that grouped these four topics is positive psychology.", "topic": "Positive psychology", "fig": "Martin Seligman"}, {"q": "In treating which of the following conditions 'mand' is used as the central principle of behavior modification?", "options": {"a": "Anxiety", "b": "Autism", "c": "Anhedonia", "d": "ADHD"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the mand is the central principle of language training for children with autism. B. F. Skinner, in Verbal Behavior (1957), treated language as operant behaviour shaped by its consequences and named several verbal operants. The mand (from 'command' and 'demand') is a request controlled by what the speaker wants: the child says 'juice' and is reinforced by getting juice. The tact is a label controlled by something seen or heard ('car' on seeing a car), the echoic repeats another person's words, and the intraverbal answers someone else's words ('What do you drink?' 'Juice'). Many children with autism have little functional speech and communicate through tantrums or by pulling an adult's hand. Applied behaviour analysis (ABA), the best-known behavioural treatment for autism since Ole Ivar Lovaas (1987), includes the verbal behaviour approach of Mark Sundberg and James Partington (1998), which starts with mand training because a request brings an immediate, personally valued reward, so the child learns that words work. Mands can be spoken words, signs or picture cards. Example: Kabir, a four-year-old with autism, is taught to say or sign 'ball' and is handed the ball at once; as his mands grow, his tantrums fall. Option b is right because mand training is the core of language teaching in ABA programmes for autism. Exam trap: ADHD is also treated with behaviour modification (token economies, daily report cards, parent training), so it tempts; but those programmes target attention and conduct, not teaching a child to make requests, which is what the mand does.", "topic": "Mand training in autism", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Bowlby's major contribution to theories of child development grew out of studies of", "options": {"a": "Fraternal and identical twins", "b": "Children separated from their parents", "c": "Recessive gene inheritance", "d": "Imitation and modeling as causes of aggression"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: John Bowlby's theory grew out of studies of children separated from their parents. John Bowlby (1907 to 1990), a British psychiatrist and psychoanalyst, first saw the link in Forty-Four Juvenile Thieves (1944): of 44 young thieves at his London clinic, 14 showed 'affectionless psychopathy' (no guilt, no warmth towards others), and 12 of those 14 had suffered long early separations from their mothers. His World Health Organization report Maternal Care and Mental Health (1951) set out the maternal deprivation hypothesis: an infant needs a warm, continuous relationship with a mother or permanent mother substitute, and prolonged breaks in the early years do lasting harm. James Robertson's film A Two-Year-Old Goes to Hospital (1952) showed the sequence of protest, despair and detachment in a separated child. Bowlby then drew on ethology, Konrad Lorenz's imprinting (1935) and Harry Harlow's monkeys (1958), to build attachment theory in Attachment (1969), Separation (1973) and Loss (1980): attachment is an evolved system that keeps the infant close to a caregiver, who becomes a secure base for exploration. Example: when two-year-old Ananya spends a week in hospital without her mother, she cries and searches (protest), then turns listless (despair), then seems indifferent when her mother returns (detachment), just as Bowlby and Robertson described. Option b is right because separation and deprivation studies were the root of Bowlby's work. Exam trap: option d also names a famous child study, but imitation and modelling of aggression is Albert Bandura's Bobo doll research (1961), the basis of social learning theory, not of attachment theory.", "topic": "Bowlby and maternal separation", "fig": "John Bowlby"}, {"q": "A school psychologist wishes to test a second-grade student for possible mental retardation. The most appropriate test to use would probably be the", "options": {"a": "Stanford-Binet intelligence Test", "b": "MMPI", "c": "WAIS", "d": "Thematic Apperception Test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: for a second grader being assessed for intellectual disability, the Stanford-Binet is the most suitable of the tests offered. 'Mental retardation' was the DSM-IV name; the current term is intellectual disability: significantly below-average intellectual functioning (an IQ about two standard deviations below the mean, roughly 70 or less), together with deficits in adaptive behaviour, beginning in the developmental period. Diagnosing it needs an individually given, standardised test of general intelligence normed for the child's age, plus an adaptive behaviour measure such as Edgar Doll's Vineland Social Maturity Scale (1935). A second grader is about 7 to 8 years old. The Stanford-Binet, Lewis Terman's (1916) American revision of the Binet-Simon scale, was built for children; its fifth edition (Gale Roid, 2003) covers ages 2 to 85 and over, gives a full-scale IQ with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, and has many easy items at the low end, so it measures low ability well in young children. Option a is right because the Stanford-Binet is an individual intelligence test suited to a young child and widely used to diagnose intellectual disability. Exam trap: the WAIS is also an individual IQ test and looks right, but it is the adult Wechsler scale (16 years and over); the child version, the WISC, is not offered here. Key note: today the WISC (ages 6 to 16) would be just as usual for a second grader, but it is not offered; among these options a stands.", "topic": "Testing for intellectual disability", "fig": "Lewis Terman"}, {"q": "Which of the following models accepts that the more deeply information is processed; the more likely it is to be retained?", "options": {"a": "Level of processing model", "b": "Retrieval cues model", "c": "Atkinson and Shiffrin model", "d": "Trace model"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the levels of processing model holds that the more deeply information is processed, the better it is retained. Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972) argued against the idea that memory depends on which store an item sits in. Instead, retention depends on how deeply the item is analysed when it is encoded. Shallow (structural) processing attends to appearance ('Is the word in capital letters?'); intermediate (phonemic) processing attends to sound ('Does it rhyme with weight?'); deep (semantic) processing attends to meaning ('Does it fit the sentence: He met a ___ in the street?'). Fergus Craik and Endel Tulving (1975) showed that after such questions people remembered far more of the words they had judged for meaning than those judged for letter case, even though they had not been told to memorise anything. Elaboration, linking new material to what you already know or to yourself, deepens processing further. Critics noted that 'depth' is hard to measure except by the recall it is meant to explain, a circularity problem. Example: Riya reads a chapter on reinforcement twice and forgets it, while Arjun explains each idea in his own words with examples from his life; Arjun processes semantically and recalls more. Option a is right because 'deeper processing, better retention' is the central claim of Craik and Lockhart's model. Exam trap: the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) is the most famous memory model and tempts by familiarity, but it explains retention by transfer through stores and rehearsal, not by depth of processing.", "topic": "Levels of processing", "fig": "Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart"}, {"q": "The fact that a nearer object may obscure a more distant object from the view is due to:", "options": {"a": "Figure and ground", "b": "Proximity", "c": "Interposition", "d": "Closure"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: when a nearer object hides part of a more distant one, the depth cue is interposition. Interposition (also called overlap or occlusion) is a monocular depth cue: if one object blocks part of another, the visual system treats the blocking object as nearer. It is one of the pictorial cues that painters have used for centuries to put depth on a flat canvas, along with linear perspective, relative size, texture gradient, aerial perspective, shading and height in the visual field. Because it needs only one eye, it works in photographs and paintings. It gives only relative order (which object is in front), not distance in metres. Binocular cues, by contrast, need both eyes: retinal disparity (each eye receives a slightly different image) and convergence (the eyes turn inwards to fixate a near object). Example: at a cricket match, a spectator's head blocks part of the scoreboard for Arjun, and he knows at once that the head is closer, without judging any distance. Option c is right because the stem describes a nearer object obscuring a farther one, which is the definition of interposition. Exam trap: figure and ground, proximity and closure are also perception terms from the same chapter, but they are Gestalt principles for organising shapes into objects and groups, not cues for depth.", "topic": "Interposition as a depth cue", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A child's parents only allow her to play video games after she has completed all of her homework for that night. This reward system is consistent with", "options": {"a": "The Premack principle", "b": "The Garcia effect", "c": "Observational learning", "d": "Systematic desensitization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: allowing video games only after the homework is done is the Premack principle. David Premack (1959, 1965) proposed that reinforcement is relative: a behaviour a person prefers, that is, one they do often when free to choose (a high-probability behaviour), can reinforce a less preferred, low-probability behaviour. The reinforcer need not be food or praise; it can be an activity. In everyday terms this is 'grandma's rule': first eat your vegetables, then you can have dessert. In the stem, video games are the high-probability behaviour and homework the low-probability one, and access to the first is made contingent on finishing the second, so homework should increase. The order matters: the preferred activity must follow the less preferred one. Premack showed this with children who could either eat sweets or play a pinball machine: for children who preferred pinball, the chance to play reinforced eating sweets, and for those who preferred sweets, the reverse held. Teachers, parents and behaviour therapists use it widely because it needs no tangible reward. Option a is right because a preferred activity (gaming) is used to reinforce a less preferred one (homework), which is exactly the Premack principle. Exam trap: students sometimes reverse the rule; it is the high-probability behaviour that reinforces the low-probability behaviour, not the other way round, so 'homework first, then games' fits, while 'games first, then homework' would not.", "topic": "Premack principle", "fig": "David Premack"}, {"q": "The main difference between the Minnesota Multiphasic personality inventory (MMPI) and the California personality inventory (CPI) is that", "options": {"a": "The CPI is more applicable to normal groups", "b": "The MMPI is more applicable to normal groups", "c": "The CPI is a projective test", "d": "The MMPI is a projective test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the CPI is more applicable to normal groups, while the MMPI targets psychopathology. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) was built by empirical criterion keying: a large pool of true-false items was given to psychiatric patient groups (such as depressed, schizophrenic or hypochondriacal patients) and to normal controls, and only items that separated a patient group from the controls were kept for its scale. Its clinical scales therefore carry diagnostic names (Depression, Hysteria, Paranoia, Schizophrenia), and it is used mainly in clinical settings. The California Psychological Inventory (Harrison Gough, 1956) used the same empirical method and borrowed many MMPI items, but it was keyed against normal criteria, such as people rated high or low in leadership or responsibility, to measure 'folk concepts', everyday traits recognised in all cultures: dominance, sociability, responsibility, self-control, tolerance and achievement. It is used in schools, counselling, personnel selection and leadership research, and is often called 'the normal person's MMPI'. Both are objective, self-report inventories with fixed answers scored by key, so neither is projective. Example: a hospital would give the MMPI to Rahul to check for depressive or psychotic features, whereas a company choosing managers would give Sneha the CPI to describe her dominance and sociability. Option a is right because the CPI was designed to describe normal personality. Exam trap: option b simply reverses the two tests; the MMPI is the one keyed against psychiatric patients, so it is the more clinical tool.", "topic": "MMPI versus CPI", "fig": "Harrison Gough"}, {"q": "People are likely to invest less effort in a task when they are working with others. What is this phenomenon called?", "options": {"a": "Social Facilitation", "b": "Social Loafing", "c": "Deindividuation", "d": "Sleeper Effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: investing less effort when working with others on a shared task is social loafing. Social loafing is the tendency of individuals to exert less effort when their contributions are pooled into a group product than when they work alone. Its forerunner was the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913), who found that each person pulled a rope less hard as the team grew. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: students asked to clap or shout as loudly as they could made less noise per person in groups, even when they only believed that others were shouting with them, which showed that motivation was lost, not just coordination. Loafing happens because individual effort cannot be identified or evaluated, responsibility is spread across members, and people feel their own effort makes little difference. It shrinks when each person's output is identifiable, the task matters to them, the group is cohesive or each member has a distinct role. Example: in a group project, Kabir does very little because he knows the teacher will mark only the final report, not each member's part. Option b is right because the stem describes reduced individual effort in a group, the definition of social loafing. Exam trap: social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965) is the look-alike, but it runs the other way: the presence of others improves performance on simple, well-learned tasks, especially when each person's effort is visible.", "topic": "Social loafing", "fig": "Bibb Latane"}, {"q": "Which of the following was most closely associated with the structuralist school of psychology?", "options": {"a": "Titchener", "b": "James", "c": "Descartes", "d": "Watson"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Edward Titchener is the name most closely tied to the structuralist school. Edward Bradford Titchener (1867 to 1927), an Englishman, trained with Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig and then led the psychology laboratory at Cornell University from 1892. He named the school structural psychology in his paper 'The postulates of a structural psychology' (1898), contrasting it with functional psychology. Structuralism aimed to break conscious experience into its basic elements, sensations, images and feelings (affections), describe their attributes (quality, intensity, duration, clearness) and find the laws by which they combine, much as a chemist analyses compounds into elements. Its method was trained introspection: observers were drilled to report raw sensations and to avoid the 'stimulus error' of naming the object instead. Titchener's laboratory manuals, Experimental Psychology (1901 to 1905), became standard guides. The school faded after his death, because introspective reports could not be checked and laboratories disagreed, and because it ignored children, animals, abnormal behaviour and practical use. Option a is right because Titchener founded, named and led structuralism. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (who opened the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879) is often called the founder of structuralism, but Wundt called his own system voluntarism; the label 'structuralism' and the school itself belong to his student Titchener.", "topic": "Titchener and structuralism", "fig": "Edward Titchener"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT categorized as a personality disorder according to the DSM IV?", "options": {"a": "Narcissistic", "b": "Antisocial", "c": "Impulsive", "d": "Borderline"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: 'impulsive' is not one of the DSM-IV personality disorders. A personality disorder is an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from cultural expectations, is pervasive and inflexible, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, is stable over time and causes distress or impairment. DSM-IV (American Psychiatric Association, 1994) listed ten personality disorders on Axis II in three clusters. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive. A memory aid is 'weird, wild, worried'. Narcissistic, antisocial and borderline are therefore all genuine Cluster B disorders. Impulsivity is a trait and a criterion within borderline and antisocial personality disorder, not a disorder of its own. A separate DSM-IV chapter covered impulse-control disorders (intermittent explosive disorder, kleptomania, pyromania, pathological gambling, trichotillomania), which are not personality disorders. Example: Neha has had unstable relationships, self-harm and sudden mood swings since her teens; her clinician diagnoses borderline personality disorder and records impulsivity as one of its features, never 'impulsive personality disorder'. Option c is right because no DSM-IV personality disorder is called 'impulsive'. Exam trap: the word feels familiar because ICD-10 (World Health Organization, 1992) splits emotionally unstable personality disorder into an impulsive type and a borderline type; that is an ICD-10 subtype, not a DSM-IV disorder. Key note: ICD-10 does have an 'emotionally unstable personality disorder, impulsive type' (F60.30), but DSM-IV has no impulsive personality disorder, so c stands.", "topic": "DSM-IV personality disorders", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The ________ complex is to girls as the ________ complex is to boys.", "options": {"a": "Electra, Oedipus", "b": "Oedipus, Electra", "c": "Oral, Phallic", "d": "Phallic, Oral"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Electra complex is to girls as the Oedipus complex is to boys. The stem pairs each blank with a sex: the first blank needs the complex named for girls, the second the complex named for boys. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) placed both in the phallic stage of psychosexual development (about 3 to 6 years), when pleasure centres on the genitals. He introduced the Oedipal idea in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900), after the Greek king Oedipus, who unknowingly killed his father and married his mother. In the Oedipus complex the boy unconsciously desires his mother and sees his father as a rival; fearing punishment (castration anxiety), he resolves the conflict by identifying with his father, which builds his superego and gender role. Carl Jung (1913) proposed the name Electra complex for the girl's version, after Electra, who helped avenge her father's murder on her mother. In this account the girl turns towards her father, partly through penis envy, sees her mother as a rival, and resolves the conflict by identifying with her mother. Freud himself rejected Jung's term and spoke of the female Oedipus attitude, but the Electra label stuck in textbooks. Both ideas are now criticised for lack of evidence and for their view of women; Karen Horney (1926) answered penis envy by pointing to men's envy of motherhood. Option a is right because it places Electra with girls and Oedipus with boys, in that order. Exam trap: option b uses the right two words in the wrong order, and the analogy form makes the reversal easy to miss, so check which blank goes with girls.", "topic": "Oedipus and Electra complexes", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Allport believed that traits could be organized into three levels:", "options": {"a": "Primary, Secondary, Tertiary", "b": "Cognitive, Emotional, Physiological", "c": "Id, Ego, Superego", "d": "Cardinal, Central, Secondary"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Gordon Allport organised traits into cardinal, central and secondary traits. Gordon Allport (1937), in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, treated traits as real neuropsychic structures inside the person that make behaviour consistent across situations. He ranked a person's traits by how pervasive they are, and in Pattern and Growth in Personality (1961) he renamed these individual traits 'personal dispositions'. A cardinal trait dominates almost everything a person does, so much that the person becomes known by it, as in Machiavellian, narcissistic or Christlike; few people have one. Central traits are the 5 to 10 broad qualities that describe most people well, the words you would use in a reference letter: honest, warm, ambitious, anxious. Secondary traits are narrower, less consistent dispositions that appear only in particular situations, such as a food preference or irritability in traffic. For example, Neha's central traits might be friendly, hard-working and talkative, while a secondary trait is that she becomes nervous only when speaking on stage; she has no single cardinal trait. Allport studied individuals in depth (the idiographic approach), for instance in Letters from Jenny (1965). Option d is right because cardinal, central and secondary are Allport's own three levels. Exam trap: 'secondary' also appears in option a, which makes 'primary, secondary, tertiary' look familiar; but Allport's three levels do not include primary or tertiary, and 'primary' belongs to Raymond Cattell's (1949) 16 primary (first-order) factors, a different model.", "topic": "Allport's levels of traits", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five traits?", "options": {"a": "Sense of humour", "b": "Openness to experience", "c": "Conscientiousness", "d": "Extraversion"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: sense of humour is not one of the Big Five traits. The Big Five, or five-factor model, holds that most differences in personality can be summed up by five broad dimensions, remembered as OCEAN: openness to experience (imaginative and curious versus conventional), conscientiousness (organised and dependable versus careless), extraversion (sociable and energetic versus reserved), agreeableness (warm and cooperative versus antagonistic) and neuroticism (anxious and moody versus emotionally stable). The model grew from the lexical hypothesis, the idea that important traits become words in every language. Factor analyses of trait ratings by Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) and Warren Norman (1963) kept finding five factors, and Lewis Goldberg (1981) named them the Big Five, 'big' meaning broad. Paul Costa and Robert McCrae reached the same five through questionnaires and built the NEO Personality Inventory (1985) and its revision, the NEO-PI-R (1992). The five appear across many cultures and are partly heritable. Sense of humour is a real and valued quality, but it is a narrow characteristic that relates partly to extraversion and openness rather than a separate dimension. Example: Kavya is talkative and outgoing (high extraversion), punctual and tidy (high conscientiousness) and very funny; her humour shows up within her extraversion and openness scores, not on a scale of its own. Option a is right because humour is not one of the OCEAN dimensions. Exam trap: examiners slip in a pleasant everyday trait word and rely on students who know only some of the five; a near-miss such as 'emotional stability' is genuine (it is the low pole of neuroticism), but humour is not a factor at all.", "topic": "Big Five factors", "fig": "Paul Costa and Robert McCrae"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT an example of primary prevention of mental illness?", "options": {"a": "Drug abuse resistance education (DARE)", "b": "Prenatal health care", "c": "Narcotics Anonymous", "d": "Genetic screening"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Narcotics Anonymous is not primary prevention, because it serves people who are already addicted. Gerald Caplan (1964), in Principles of Preventive Psychiatry, applied the public health levels of prevention to mental illness. Primary prevention stops a disorder from starting, lowering its incidence (new cases) in the whole population or in at-risk groups, through education, prenatal care, nutrition, stress reduction and genetic counselling. Secondary prevention detects and treats cases early, shortening them and lowering prevalence, as in screening schoolchildren for depression. Tertiary prevention reduces disability and relapse in people who are already ill, through rehabilitation, aftercare and self-help groups. Narcotics Anonymous, founded in 1953 on the twelve-step model of Alcoholics Anonymous, is a self-help fellowship for people already dependent on drugs, helping them stay abstinent and rebuild their lives: relapse prevention, which is tertiary. The other three options act before any disorder exists. DARE (Drug Abuse Resistance Education), begun in Los Angeles schools in 1983, teaches children to resist drugs before they start using them. Prenatal health care protects the foetus from infection, malnutrition, alcohol and birth complications that can cause intellectual disability. Genetic screening with counselling identifies carriers and risks so that a disorder can be avoided. Example: Rohan, recovering from heroin dependence and attending weekly NA meetings, is receiving tertiary prevention; his younger sister, in a DARE class at school, is receiving primary prevention. Option c is right because NA begins only after addiction exists. Exam trap: DARE tempts as the 'not primary' answer because evaluations found it had little effect on later drug use, but effectiveness does not change its level: it still targets children who are not yet using.", "topic": "Levels of prevention", "fig": "Gerald Caplan"}, {"q": "Which of the following hormone is being secreted by adrenal gland:", "options": {"a": "Cortisol", "b": "Epinephrine", "c": "A and B", "d": "Melatonin"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the adrenal gland secretes both cortisol and epinephrine. Each adrenal gland sits on top of a kidney and is really two glands in one. The outer adrenal cortex makes steroid hormones: aldosterone (a mineralocorticoid that keeps salt and water balance), cortisol (a glucocorticoid) and small amounts of sex hormones (androgens). Cortisol is the main hormone of the slower stress system, the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: the hypothalamus releases CRH, the pituitary releases ACTH, and ACTH makes the adrenal cortex release cortisol, which raises blood sugar, dampens inflammation and helps the body sustain a long stress response; its level peaks in the morning. So option a qualifies. The inner adrenal medulla is in effect part of the sympathetic nervous system: when sympathetic nerves fire, it releases epinephrine (adrenaline) and some norepinephrine into the blood within seconds, raising heart rate, blood pressure and blood sugar for the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915). So option b qualifies too. Example: when a dog rushes at Meera, epinephrine from her adrenal medulla sets her heart pounding at once; if her exam stress lasts for weeks, cortisol from her adrenal cortex stays raised. Option c is right because cortisol comes from the adrenal cortex and epinephrine from the adrenal medulla, so both are adrenal hormones. Exam trap: picking a or b alone feels safe because each is correct, but each is incomplete; when both listed hormones are adrenal, the combined option is the answer, and melatonin, the odd one out, is made by the pineal gland.", "topic": "Adrenal gland hormones", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Despite working fewer hours than her coworkers, Melanie receives a promotion and a raise. Instead of feeling happy, Melanie feels guilty. This is best explained by", "options": {"a": "Social-exchange theory", "b": "Gain-loss theory", "c": "Equity theory", "d": "Foot-in-the-door phenomenon"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: Melanie's guilt at being over-rewarded is best explained by equity theory. J. Stacy Adams (1963, 1965) proposed that people judge fairness by comparing the ratio of their outcomes (pay, promotion, praise) to their inputs (hours, effort, skill) with the same ratio for others. When the ratios match, the situation feels fair. When they do not, people feel distress: the under-benefited, who get less than they deserve, feel anger or resentment, and the over-benefited, who get more than they deserve, feel guilt. Elaine Walster (later Hatfield), Ellen Berscheid and G. William Walster (1973, 1978) extended the theory to close relationships and stressed that both kinds of inequity push people to restore equity, either actually (working harder, giving something back) or psychologically (persuading themselves that they really did contribute more). In the stem, Melanie's inputs (fewer hours) are lower than her coworkers', yet her outcomes (a promotion and a raise) are higher, so she is over-benefited, and the predicted feeling is guilt rather than pleasure. She might now stay late every evening to bring her inputs up to her outcomes. Option c is right because only equity theory predicts guilt from being over-rewarded. Exam trap: social exchange theory sounds right because it is also about rewards and costs, but it assumes people simply try to maximise rewards and minimise costs, so it would predict that Melanie feels happy; only equity theory says that too much reward is itself distressing.", "topic": "Equity theory", "fig": "J. Stacy Adams"}, {"q": "Which type of memory is being tested when you take a multiple-choice test?", "options": {"a": "Method of Loci", "b": "Recognition", "c": "Savings method", "d": "Free recall"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: a multiple-choice test measures recognition memory. Memory researchers measure retention in three main ways. In recall you must retrieve the information yourself, with no options in front of you: free recall (in any order), serial recall (in the original order) or cued recall (from a hint). In recognition you only have to identify the correct item among alternatives you are shown, as in a multiple-choice or true-false test, or a witness picking out a suspect in an identity parade. In the savings (relearning) method, developed by Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885), you relearn material learned earlier and measure how much less time, or how many fewer trials, it takes the second time. Recognition is usually easier than recall, because the correct answer is presented as a ready-made retrieval cue: you need only judge whether it feels familiar, rather than search memory and produce the item. That is why people often recognise the name of a schoolmate they could not have recalled unaided. Example: in a history exam, Meera cannot write the year of the Battle of Plassey in the essay section, but when the multiple-choice section shows 1757 among four years, she picks it at once. Option b is right because choosing the correct answer from given options is recognition. Exam trap: free recall is the near-miss because it is also a memory test, but recall requires producing the answer with no options, as in an essay or fill-in-the-blank question; once options are shown, the task becomes recognition.", "topic": "Recognition versus recall", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A high school teacher walks around the room more and speaks more clearly when being observed by her principal. This is an example of", "options": {"a": "The Placebo effect", "b": "The Hawthorne effect", "c": "The Availability heuristic", "d": "A Double-blind experiment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the teacher who works harder only while her principal watches is showing the Hawthorne effect. The Hawthorne effect is a change in behaviour that happens because people know they are being observed or studied, whatever the actual intervention. It takes its name from the Western Electric Company's Hawthorne Works near Chicago, where studies from 1924 to 1932 examined worker productivity. In the illumination experiments, output rose when the lighting was brightened, but it also rose in the control group, and sometimes even when the lights were dimmed, suggesting that the researchers' attention mattered more than the lighting. The later studies were associated with Elton Mayo (1880 to 1949) and reported by Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson in Management and the Worker (1939); Henry Landsberger coined the term 'Hawthorne effect' in 1958. Later reanalyses found the original data weaker than the legend, but the lesson remains central to research design: being observed is itself a variable. Researchers reduce it with unobtrusive measures, longer observation so that people get used to the observer, and control groups that receive the same attention. In the stem, the teacher walks around more and speaks more clearly only when observed, so the change is caused by being watched, not by any training. Option b is right because behaviour that changes through awareness of being observed is the Hawthorne effect. Exam trap: the placebo effect is the closest look-alike because both are expectation effects, but placebo improvement comes from believing one has received a treatment, while the Hawthorne effect comes from knowing one is being watched.", "topic": "Hawthorne effect", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A false belief maintained despite contradictory evidence or experience is an", "options": {"a": "Compulsion", "b": "Delusion", "c": "Hallucination", "d": "Obsession"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a false belief that is held firmly despite contradictory evidence or experience is a delusion. A delusion is a disorder of thought content, not of perception. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, gave three classic marks: the belief is held with extraordinary conviction (subjective certainty), it is impervious to counter-argument and to contrary experience (incorrigibility), and its content is false or impossible. Modern definitions add that the belief is not explained by the person's culture or religion, so a belief shared by one's community, such as belief in the evil eye, is not a delusion. Common types are persecutory, referential, grandiose, nihilistic, jealous, erotomanic and somatic delusions; they are typical of schizophrenia, delusional disorder and psychotic mood episodes. For example, Arjun is convinced that his neighbours have hidden a camera in his ceiling fan to read his thoughts. An electrician opens the fan in front of him and finds nothing, yet Arjun says the camera was removed the night before. The evidence does not shake the belief, which is exactly what makes it delusional. Option b is right because the stem gives the core definition of a delusion: false, fixed and unaffected by evidence. Exam trap: hallucination is the commonest distractor. A hallucination is a perception without an adequate external stimulus (a voice in an empty room), whereas a delusion is a belief. A patient may have both, and may build a delusion to explain a hallucination, but they are different phenomena. Obsessions are also thoughts, but they are intrusive, and the person recognises them as their own and usually as senseless, so they are not held as fixed truths.", "topic": "Delusion: definition", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Single form of the test is administered twice on the same sample with a reasonable time gap is called as?", "options": {"a": "Validity", "b": "Test-retest reliability", "c": "Internal consistency reliability", "d": "Alternate form reliability"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: giving a single form of a test twice to the same sample, with a reasonable time gap, measures test-retest reliability. Reliability is the consistency of a test's scores. In classical test theory an observed score is a true score plus random error, and reliability tells us how much of the score variation is true variation. In the test-retest method the same people take the same test on two occasions and the two sets of scores are correlated; the correlation is called the coefficient of stability. The time gap matters. If it is too short, people remember their answers and gain from practice, so the coefficient is inflated. If it is too long, the trait itself may change (children's abilities grow, moods shift), so the coefficient falls for reasons that are not measurement error. A gap of a few weeks is common for ability tests. The method suits stable traits such as intelligence, not passing states such as today's anxiety. For example, Meera gives a vocabulary test to 60 college students in January and the same test to the same students in February; a correlation of 0.88 between the two sessions shows the scores are stable over time. Option b is right because the stem names all three defining features: one form, the same sample and two administrations separated in time. Exam trap: alternate (parallel) form reliability also tests the same people twice, but with two equivalent forms of the test, giving a coefficient of equivalence; the words 'single form' rule it out.", "topic": "Test-retest reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The therapies that use behaviour modification techniques but also incorporate procedure designed to change maladaptive beliefs are called", "options": {"a": "Behavioural therapies", "b": "Cognitive behaviour therapies", "c": "Psychoanalytic therapies", "d": "Dynamic therapies"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: therapies that use behaviour modification techniques and also add procedures to change maladaptive beliefs are cognitive behaviour therapies. Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) grew from two streams. Behaviour therapy, built on conditioning research and on Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation, changes behaviour directly through exposure, reinforcement, modelling and skills training. The cognitive stream holds that the way we interpret events shapes how we feel and act. Albert Ellis (1955) founded rational therapy, later renamed rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), which disputes irrational beliefs such as 'I must be approved of by everyone'. Aaron Beck developed cognitive therapy in the early 1960s, targeting automatic thoughts and distortions such as catastrophising (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, 1979). Donald Meichenbaum (1977) joined self-instruction training to behavioural practice in cognitive behaviour modification. CBT combines the two streams: thought records and Socratic questioning challenge the beliefs, while behavioural experiments, homework, activity scheduling and graded exposure change what the person does. For example, Kabir avoids speaking in class because he believes 'everyone will think I am stupid'. With his therapist he rates and questions this thought, then gives a two-minute talk to test what really happens. Option b is right because the stem names both ingredients, behaviour techniques plus belief change, which is the definition of CBT. Exam trap: option a is the near-miss. Pure behaviour therapy treats observable behaviour and its learning history and does not set out to change beliefs; once cognitive restructuring is added, the therapy is called cognitive behavioural.", "topic": "Cognitive behaviour therapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Sympathetic nerves are projected from……", "options": {"a": "Brain", "b": "Cervical- Sacral sections of spinal cord", "c": "Thoracic-lumbar sections of spinal cord", "d": "All the sections of the spinal cord"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: sympathetic nerves arise from the thoracic and lumbar sections of the spinal cord. The autonomic nervous system has two main divisions. The sympathetic division prepares the body for action; Walter Cannon (1915) called its emergency role the fight-or-flight response: the heart races, pupils widen, airways open and blood shifts to the muscles. Its preganglionic cell bodies lie in the lateral grey horn of spinal segments T1 to L2 (some books say L3), so it is called the thoracolumbar outflow. Their short axons leave through the ventral roots and synapse mostly in the sympathetic chain, a row of ganglia beside the spine, from which long postganglionic fibres run to the organs. Some fibres go straight to the adrenal medulla, which releases adrenaline into the blood. The parasympathetic division (rest and digest) is the craniosacral outflow: cranial nerves III, VII, IX and X (the vagus carries most of it) plus sacral segments S2 to S4, with ganglia near or inside the organs. For example, when Rohan steps off a kerb and a bus horn blasts beside him, his pounding heart and dry mouth come from sympathetic fibres that leave the upper thoracic segments of his spinal cord. Option c is right because thoracic-lumbar is the textbook location of the sympathetic preganglionic neurons. Exam trap: option b sounds like the parasympathetic pattern but is garbled; the parasympathetic outflow is cranial-sacral (brainstem plus sacral cord), and 'cervical-sacral' matches neither division.", "topic": "Sympathetic thoracolumbar outflow", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a standard physiological measure of sleep?", "options": {"a": "Electrooculogram", "b": "Electromyogram", "c": "Electrocardiogram", "d": "Electroencephalogram"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the electrocardiogram is not one of the standard physiological measures used to define sleep. Sleep is studied in a laboratory by polysomnography, the overnight recording of several body signals at once. Three signals are needed to score the stages of sleep, as set out in the scoring manual of Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968) and kept in the American Academy of Sleep Medicine rules (2007). The electroencephalogram (EEG) records brain waves from the scalp: alpha in relaxed waking, theta in stage N1, sleep spindles and K-complexes in N2, slow delta waves in N3, and a fast, desynchronised pattern in REM sleep. The electrooculogram (EOG) records eye movements; this is how Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered rapid eye movement sleep. The electromyogram (EMG), usually from the chin, records muscle tone, which falls to near zero during REM (REM atonia). Together they separate the stages: REM, for instance, shows a waking-like EEG, rapid eye movements and a silent EMG. When Ananya spends a night in a sleep clinic, the technician fixes electrodes on her scalp, beside her eyes and under her chin before she sleeps. Option c is right because the ECG measures the heart's electrical activity, which is not used to stage sleep. Exam trap: clinical polysomnography often adds an ECG channel, along with breathing, blood oxygen and leg movements, to detect problems such as sleep apnoea. That extra channel is a monitoring add-on; the three standard measures of sleep itself remain EEG, EOG and EMG.", "topic": "Polysomnography measures", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Explicit or Declarative memory consist of which of the following types of memories?", "options": {"a": "Episodic memory", "b": "Semantic memory", "c": "Autobiographical memory", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: explicit (declarative) memory includes episodic, semantic and autobiographical memory. Long-term memory is commonly divided into two systems. Neal Cohen and Larry Squire (1980) contrasted 'knowing that' (declarative) with 'knowing how' (procedural), and Squire later built the standard taxonomy. Declarative or explicit memory holds information that can be consciously recalled and put into words; nondeclarative or implicit memory shows itself in performance without conscious recollection (procedural skills, priming, classical conditioning). Endel Tulving (1972) had already divided this kind of memory into two parts. Episodic memory stores personal events tied to a time and place, such as what you ate at your cousin's wedding last May. Semantic memory stores facts and general knowledge, free of the context in which they were learned: that Delhi is the capital of India, or what 'neuron' means. Autobiographical memory is memory for one's own life story. It is built mostly from episodic memories (specific remembered events) plus personal semantic facts (one's birthplace, the name of one's school), and both of these are declarative, so it belongs to explicit memory as well. For example, Riya can describe her first day at college (episodic), state that her college is in Pune (semantic) and narrate how her interest in psychology grew over the years (autobiographical); she can put all three into words. Option d is right because each listed type can be consciously retrieved and stated, which is what defines declarative memory. Exam trap: procedural memory, such as riding a bicycle, is the classic implicit memory; had it appeared in the list, 'all of the above' would fail.", "topic": "Declarative memory systems", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychotherapy is the treatment, by psychological means, of problems of an emotional nature in which a trained person deliberately establishes a professional relationship with the patient. Definition given by?", "options": {"a": "David Wechsler", "b": "Wolberg", "c": "Carl Rogers", "d": "Aaron Beck"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: this definition of psychotherapy was given by Lewis Wolberg. Lewis Wolberg (1954), an American psychiatrist, wrote The Technique of Psychotherapy, a standard training text. His full definition says that psychotherapy is 'the treatment, by psychological means, of problems of an emotional nature in which a trained person deliberately establishes a professional relationship with the patient' with the object of removing, modifying or retarding existing symptoms, mediating disturbed patterns of behaviour and promoting positive personality growth and development. Each phrase does work. 'By psychological means' separates psychotherapy from drugs, surgery or electroconvulsive therapy. 'Problems of an emotional nature' sets the target. 'A trained person' who 'deliberately establishes a professional relationship' separates therapy from friendly advice. The three aims run from relieving symptoms, through changing behaviour patterns, to growth of the whole personality. For example, when Sneha's friend comforts her after a failed exam, that is support, not psychotherapy; when a trained psychologist meets her weekly with agreed goals to reduce her exam panic, Wolberg's definition fits. Option b is right because the quoted words are the opening of Wolberg's well-known definition, which is widely quoted in textbooks. Exam trap: Carl Rogers (1951) is tempting because he placed the therapeutic relationship at the centre of change, but his famous statement is the necessary and sufficient conditions for change (1957), not this definition. David Wechsler (1939) is a test author whose well-known definition is of intelligence, not psychotherapy.", "topic": "Wolberg's definition of psychotherapy", "fig": "Lewis Wolberg"}, {"q": "Self-Efficacy is", "options": {"a": "A person's mental model of his or her ability and attitudes.", "b": "A generalised evaluative attitude toward the self that influences both moods and behaviour", "c": "The process by which a person comes to know or perceive the personal attributes", "d": "A set of beliefs that one can perform adequately in a particular situation."}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: self-efficacy is a set of beliefs that one can perform adequately in a particular situation. Albert Bandura (1977) introduced the idea in the Psychological Review paper 'Self-efficacy: toward a unifying theory of behavioral change', as part of what became his social cognitive theory. Self-efficacy is not a general opinion of oneself; it is a task-specific expectation: 'Can I do this, here?' A student may have high self-efficacy for mathematics and low self-efficacy for public speaking. Bandura separated efficacy expectations (I can carry out the behaviour) from outcome expectations (the behaviour will bring the result). He named four sources of efficacy beliefs: mastery experiences (past successes, the strongest source), vicarious experience (seeing similar others succeed), verbal persuasion (encouragement) and physiological and emotional states (reading a racing heart as a sign of unreadiness). High self-efficacy predicts more effort, persistence after failure and better progress in therapy, and it is a key ingredient in health behaviour change. For example, Arjun is sure he will pass his driving test because he has driven safely with his father for months, yet he doubts he could cook a meal for ten guests; his self-efficacy differs by task. Option d is right because it captures both features of Bandura's definition: a belief about one's capability, tied to a particular situation. Exam trap: option b describes self-esteem, a global evaluation of one's worth, which is often confused with self-efficacy; a person can have high self-esteem yet low self-efficacy for a specific task.", "topic": "Bandura's self-efficacy", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "A forensic psychologist would be most likely to study:", "options": {"a": "The accuracy of eyewitness memory", "b": "The impact of advertising on shopping behaviour", "c": "The effect of hormones on decision making", "d": "Gender differences in learning styles"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: a forensic psychologist would be most likely to study the accuracy of eyewitness memory. Forensic psychology is the application of psychology to the law and the legal system. Its work includes assessing competence to stand trial and criminal responsibility (the insanity defence), assessing the risk of violence and reoffending, child custody evaluations, police interviewing, jury decision-making and the reliability of witnesses. Hugo Munsterberg (1908), in On the Witness Stand, argued early that memory and testimony should be tested scientifically. The best-known modern work is by Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer (1974). Students watched films of car accidents and were asked how fast the cars were going when they 'smashed', 'collided', 'bumped', 'hit' or 'contacted' each other. The stronger the verb, the higher the speed estimate, and a week later people asked with 'smashed' were more likely to falsely remember broken glass. Leading questions can reshape memory, which matters in every courtroom. For example, Vikram, a witness to a chain-snatching, is asked by police, 'Was the thief wearing the red jacket?', and later 'remembers' a red jacket that was never there. Option a is right because eyewitness accuracy is a question that directly serves the legal system, the defining territory of forensic psychology. Exam trap: options b, c and d are all real research topics, but each belongs to another applied or basic field (consumer, biological and educational psychology); only option a connects to the law.", "topic": "Scope of forensic psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a defence mechanism?", "options": {"a": "Projection", "b": "Regression", "c": "Ingratiation", "d": "Sublimation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: ingratiation is not a defence mechanism. Defence mechanisms, described by Sigmund Freud and systematised by Anna Freud (1936) in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, are unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety by distorting or blocking awareness of unacceptable impulses or painful facts. Projection attributes one's own unacceptable feelings to someone else, as when a man who resents his colleague believes the colleague hates him. Regression is a retreat under stress to behaviour typical of an earlier stage, such as a seven-year-old who starts thumb-sucking and bedwetting after a sibling is born. Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially valued activity, as when aggressive urges are poured into competitive sport; it is usually seen as the healthiest defence, and George Vaillant (1977) ranked it among the mature defences. Ingratiation, by contrast, is a social psychology term. Edward E. Jones (1964) analysed it as a deliberate impression-management tactic for winning someone's liking: flattery, agreeing with their opinions, doing favours. For example, Rahul praises his manager's dull presentation and agrees with every view she states because appraisals are next week. He knows exactly what he is doing; nothing is unconscious and no inner anxiety is being warded off. Option c is right because ingratiation is a conscious influence strategy aimed at other people, while the other three are unconscious ego defences. Exam trap: ingratiation sounds like introjection, which is a genuine defence (taking in another person's values or traits as one's own), so read the word carefully.", "topic": "Defences versus ingratiation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is the third stage of Selye's general adaptation syndrome?", "options": {"a": "Alarm", "b": "Exhaustion", "c": "Resistance", "d": "Mobilization"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: exhaustion is the third stage of Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome. Hans Selye (1936), an endocrinologist working at McGill University in Montreal, exposed rats to many different noxious agents (cold, injury, toxic drugs, tissue extracts) and found the same triad every time: enlarged adrenal glands, shrunken immune tissue (thymus and lymph nodes) and stomach ulcers. He concluded that the body makes a nonspecific response to any demand, which he later called stress (The Stress of Life, 1956). The general adaptation syndrome (GAS) has three stages in a fixed order. In the alarm reaction the body first meets the stressor (a brief shock phase) and then mobilises: sympathetic nerves and the adrenal medulla release adrenaline, and the adrenal cortex begins to pour out cortisol. In the stage of resistance the body adapts to the continuing stressor; cortisol stays high and the person seems to cope, but resources are being spent. In the stage of exhaustion, if the stressor goes on, those resources run out, resistance collapses and illness follows, which Selye called diseases of adaptation (ulcers, high blood pressure, lowered immunity). For example, Pooja copes with three months of night shifts at a call centre (resistance), but in the fourth month she falls ill with one infection after another (exhaustion). Option b is right because exhaustion comes after alarm and resistance. Exam trap: resistance is the second stage, and the usual slip is swapping its order with exhaustion. 'Mobilization' is a plausible word because mobilising energy is part of the alarm reaction, but it is not a separate stage.", "topic": "Selye's general adaptation syndrome", "fig": "Hans Selye"}, {"q": "Visual cliff experiment demonstrated that", "options": {"a": "Depth perception is most likely acquired", "b": "Depth perception is most likely innate", "c": "Shape perception is most likely acquired", "d": "Size perception is most likely acquired"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the visual cliff experiment was taken to show that depth perception is most likely innate. Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960) built a visual cliff: a sheet of thick glass over a table. On one side a checkerboard pattern lay just under the glass (the shallow side); on the other the same pattern lay far below (the deep side), so the glass looked like a drop. A centre board ran between them. They tested 36 infants aged 6 to 14 months. Of the 27 who moved off the centre board, all crawled onto the shallow side when their mothers called, but only three crept onto the deep side; many peered down, cried or backed away even though they could feel the solid glass. Young animals that move about at or soon after birth, such as chicks, lambs and kid goats, also refused the deep side. Gibson and Walk therefore concluded that depth perception is present as soon as a creature can move about and is largely inborn. For example, baby Aarav, newly crawling and never having fallen off anything, stops at the edge of the deep side while his mother calls. Option b is right because the classic conclusion of the study is that depth perception is innate rather than learned from falls. Exam trap: option a reverses the conclusion, and options c and d swap in shape and size perception, which the cliff did not test. Key note: Joseph Campos and colleagues (1970) found that 2-month-olds placed on the deep side showed a slowed heart rate (interest, not fear), and later work tied fear of heights to crawling experience; since crawlers already have months of visual experience, the cliff cannot fully separate nature from learning. The standard conclusion of the original study is still b, so b stands.", "topic": "Visual cliff and depth perception", "fig": "Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk"}, {"q": "The structuralist intended to identify the building blocks of", "options": {"a": "Consciousness", "b": "Subconsciousness", "c": "Unconsciousness", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the structuralists aimed to identify the building blocks of consciousness. Structuralism was named by Edward Titchener (1898), an Englishman who trained with Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig and taught at Cornell University from 1892. He wanted to do for the mind what chemistry had done for matter: break conscious experience down into its basic elements, discover how they combine, and describe the structure that results. He named three elements: sensations (the elements of perception), images (the elements of ideas) and affections (feelings of pleasantness or unpleasantness). Each element had attributes such as quality, intensity, duration and clearness. The method was trained introspection: observers, after long practice, reported the raw elements of an experience while avoiding the 'stimulus error', that is, naming the object ('an apple') instead of describing the sensations ('red, round, smooth'). For example, asked to introspect on biting a mango, Neha would have to report sweetness, softness, coldness and a mild pleasantness, not simply 'a mango'. Because the method needed a trained, conscious observer, structuralism ignored the unconscious, children, animals and disturbed minds, and it faded after Titchener's death in 1927. Option a is right because consciousness, analysed into its elements, was the whole subject matter of structuralism. Exam trap: 'subconscious' and 'unconscious' belong to Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (from about 1900), which studied exactly what introspection could not reach; structuralism stands at the opposite pole.", "topic": "Structuralism's subject matter", "fig": "Edward Titchener"}, {"q": "The smallest change in the stimulus that a person is able to detect:", "options": {"a": "DL", "b": "AL", "c": "Weber's Law", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the smallest change in a stimulus that a person can detect is the DL, the difference limen. Psychophysics, founded by Gustav Fechner (1860) in Elements of Psychophysics, studies how physical stimuli relate to sensations, using thresholds (limens). The absolute limen (AL, absolute threshold) is the smallest intensity of a stimulus that can be detected at all, defined as detection on 50 percent of trials. The difference limen (DL, difference threshold) is the smallest change in intensity that can be detected, again on 50 percent of trials; the change itself is called the just noticeable difference (JND). Ernst Weber (1834) found that the DL is not a fixed amount but a constant fraction of the starting stimulus: delta I / I = k, now called Weber's law. For lifted weights the fraction is roughly 2 percent, so a 100 g weight must change by about 2 g to be noticed, but a 1,000 g weight by about 20 g. For example, Sana can feel the difference between a 100 g and a 102 g packet in her hand, but holding a 5 kg bag of rice she cannot feel an extra 2 g; it would take about 100 g. Option a is right because the DL is defined as the smallest detectable change in a stimulus. Exam trap: AL is the near-miss; it is the smallest detectable stimulus, not the smallest detectable change. Key note: Weber's law is about the DL (it states how the DL grows with the size of the stimulus), so it is related, but a law is not a threshold, and the AL is not about change at all; 'All of the above' therefore fails, and a stands.", "topic": "Difference threshold (DL)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Rorschach ink blot test is based on which types of projective technique:", "options": {"a": "Constructive", "b": "Completion", "c": "Associative", "d": "Expressive"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Rorschach inkblot test is an associative projective technique. Projective techniques present an ambiguous stimulus and assume that people project their own needs, conflicts and ways of thinking onto it. Gardner Lindzey (1959) sorted them by the kind of response they demand into five families. In association techniques the person reports the first word, image or percept the stimulus calls up: the inkblots of Hermann Rorschach (1921, Psychodiagnostik) and word association tests such as the Kent-Rosanoff list (Grace Kent and Aaron Rosanoff, 1910). In construction techniques the person builds a product, usually a story: the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935). In completion techniques the person finishes an incomplete stimulus: sentence completion blanks and the Rosenzweig Picture-Frustration Study. In choice or ordering techniques the person picks or arranges items, as in the Szondi test. In expressive techniques the person creates freely: Draw-a-Person, House-Tree-Person, finger painting, play and psychodrama. In the Rorschach the examiner shows ten symmetrical blots one by one and asks, 'What might this be?', and the person says what the blot brings to mind. For example, Tara looks at Card I and says 'a bat', then 'two dancers holding hands': she is associating to the blot, not building a story or finishing a sentence. Option c is right because the Rorschach response is an immediate association to an ambiguous blot, the defining feature of Lindzey's association category. Exam trap: construction (option a) is the home of the TAT, the other famous projective test, and examiners swap the two; remember 'Rorschach associates, TAT constructs'.", "topic": "Lindzey's projective classes", "fig": "Gardner Lindzey"}, {"q": "In Rorschach, Card no. 1,4,5,6,7 are representative of:", "options": {"a": "Chromatic", "b": "Achromatic", "c": "Semi Chromatic", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Rorschach cards I, IV, V, VI and VII are achromatic. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published his inkblot test in Psychodiagnostik and died the following year, aged 37. The test has ten bilaterally symmetrical inkblots, always shown in the same order from Card I to Card X, and they fall into three colour groups. Five are achromatic, printed only in black and shades of grey: Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII. Two are black with bright red areas: Cards II and III, called partly chromatic or semi-chromatic in some Indian books. Three are fully chromatic, in pastel colours: Cards VIII, IX and X. So the deck splits into five achromatic and five coloured cards. Colour matters in scoring: responses that use colour (FC, CF, C) are read as signs of how a person handles emotion, responses that use the grey shading are linked to anxiety and the need for affection, and a sudden delay or rejection on the coloured cards was traditionally called colour shock. Card V is the one most often seen as a bat or butterfly. As a memory aid, Rahul remembers the grey cards as 'Card I, then the run from IV to VII', with the red cards sitting between them. Option b is right because Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII contain no colour, only black and grey. Exam trap: semi-chromatic (option c) is the label for Cards II and III, which carry red patches, and chromatic (option a) fits Cards VIII to X; mixing up the three groups is the usual error.", "topic": "Rorschach card colours", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "Computer provided a new way to conceptualise mental processes and to develop detail theories which is known as", "options": {"a": "Cognitive Approach", "b": "Behavioural Approach", "c": "Gestalt Approach", "d": "Information Processing Approach"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the approach in which the computer gave a new way to conceptualise mental processes and to build detailed theories is the information-processing approach. During the cognitive revolution of the 1950s psychologists saw that a computer takes in input, codes it, stores it, transforms it by rules and produces output, and that the mind might be described in the same terms. George Miller (1956) showed that short-term memory holds about seven, plus or minus two, chunks. Donald Broadbent (1958) drew one of the first flowcharts of the mind, with attention as a limited-capacity channel guarded by a filter. Allen Newell and Herbert Simon wrote computer programs, the Logic Theorist (1956) and the General Problem Solver (from 1957), that solved problems in humanlike steps. Ulric Neisser (1967) gathered the field in Cognitive Psychology. The approach yields box-and-arrow models with stages and capacity limits, such as the multi-store model of memory of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968): sensory register, short-term store, long-term store. For example, when Isha looks up a phone number and repeats it until she dials it, an information-processing account says the digits enter a sensory register, are encoded into short-term memory and are kept alive by rehearsal. Option d is right because the stem names the computer analogy itself, the defining idea of information processing. Exam trap: behaviourism (option b) refused to theorise about inner mental processes at all. Key note: information processing is the core framework of the wider cognitive approach, so option a is close; but the stem describes the computer as the model of the mind, which is the information-processing approach in particular, so d stands.", "topic": "Information-processing approach", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A person who is impatient, compulsive, and competitive is likely to be diagnosed as having", "options": {"a": "Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder", "b": "Type A personality", "c": "Type B personality", "d": "Machiavellian personality"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: a person who is impatient, compulsive and competitive is best described as having a Type A personality. The idea came not from psychologists but from two San Francisco cardiologists, Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman (1959), who noticed that many of their heart patients were driven, hurried people. They described the Type A behaviour pattern: intense competitive achievement striving, a chronic sense of time urgency (doing several things at once, impatience with any delay), easily roused hostility and aggressiveness, and over-commitment to work. Type B people are relaxed, patient and easy-going. In their Western Collaborative Group Study of about 3,000 working men, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men; later research found hostility, especially cynical hostility, to be the most harmful component. They popularised the idea in Type A Behavior and Your Heart (1974). For example, Vikram honks the moment a light turns green, eats lunch while answering emails, and turns a friendly game of carrom into a contest he must win; he fits the Type A pattern. Option b is right because impatience (time urgency), competitiveness and a driven, compulsive work style are the textbook marks of Type A. Exam trap: the word 'compulsive' points towards obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, but OCPD is about perfectionism, orderliness and control, not time urgency and competitiveness. Key note: Type A is a behaviour pattern, not a DSM or ICD diagnosis, so 'diagnosed' is loose wording; among these options it fits the description best, so b stands.", "topic": "Type A behaviour pattern", "fig": "Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT true about Ex Post Facto Research?", "options": {"a": "The dependent variable have already occurred", "b": "The independent variables have already occurred", "c": "The investigator have not control over independent variable", "d": "Researcher will conduct the study after the event has occurred"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: of these statements, 'the dependent variable has already occurred' is the one that does not define ex post facto research. Ex post facto means 'after the fact'. Fred Kerlinger, in Foundations of Behavioral Research (first edition 1964), defined it as systematic empirical inquiry in which the scientist does not have direct control of independent variables because their manifestations have already occurred or because they are inherently not manipulable. So the defining features all concern the independent variable: it has already happened (b), the investigator cannot control or manipulate it (c), and the study is carried out after the event (d). The researcher compares groups that already differ on the IV, or traces an outcome back to possible causes, which is why the design is also called causal-comparative research. Smokers versus non-smokers for lung disease is the classic example. With no manipulation or random assignment, causal claims are weak; self-selection and the post hoc fallacy ('after this, therefore because of this') are its risks. For example, Neha compares the exam anxiety of students who did and did not attend coaching classes last year; she cannot assign anyone to coaching, so attendance is an ex post facto IV. Option a is right because the defining clause of ex post facto research is about the independent variable, not the dependent variable. Exam trap: b, c and d all sound like 'after the fact' and are true; the item tests which variable the 'already occurred' clause belongs to. Key note: Kerlinger notes that the researcher often starts from an observed dependent variable and looks back for causes, so a is not flatly false; but the defining feature is an IV that has already occurred and cannot be manipulated, and a is the statement the paper treats as not defining, so a stands.", "topic": "Ex post facto research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "How many clinical scales are there in Luria?", "options": {"a": "10", "b": "11", "c": "12", "d": "13"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Luria-Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery has 11 clinical scales (Form I). Alexander Luria (1902 to 1977), the Russian neuropsychologist, assessed brain-injured patients qualitatively, watching exactly where and how a function broke down rather than using norms. Anne-Lise Christensen (1975) compiled his procedures into a set of test items, and Charles Golden, Thomas Hammeke and Arnold Purisch (1978; manual 1980) standardised them at the University of Nebraska into a scored battery with American norms. Form I has 269 items, each scored 0, 1 or 2, grouped into 11 clinical scales: motor functions, rhythm, tactile functions, visual functions, receptive speech, expressive speech, writing, reading, arithmetic, memory and intellectual processes. Scores are converted to T scores, and higher scores mean more impairment. Beyond the clinical scales there are summary scales (such as the pathognomonic, left hemisphere and right hemisphere scales) and localisation scales. Like the Halstead-Reitan, it is a fixed battery: every patient gets the same tests. A simple way to remember the eleven: Farhan groups them as body (motor, rhythm, tactile, visual), language (receptive speech, expressive speech, writing, reading) and thinking (arithmetic, memory, intellectual processes), 4 + 4 + 3 = 11. Option b is right because Form I, the standard version taught in textbooks, has 11 clinical scales. Exam trap: the MMPI has 10 clinical scales, which makes option a tempting; do not carry that number across to the Luria-Nebraska. Key note: Form II adds a twelfth clinical scale, intermediate memory, so 12 is defensible for that form, but the standard answer for 'the Luria' is Form I's 11, so b stands.", "topic": "Luria-Nebraska clinical scales", "fig": "Charles Golden"}, {"q": "Which of the following methods is generally concerned with causal relationship?", "options": {"a": "Field Experiment", "b": "Field Study", "c": "Field Survey", "d": "Laboratory Experiment"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the laboratory experiment is the method generally concerned with causal relationships. To show that X causes Y, a researcher must show that X comes before Y, that the two vary together, and that nothing else explains the link. An experiment does this by manipulating the independent variable, randomly assigning participants to conditions and holding other variables constant, then measuring the dependent variable. The laboratory gives the tightest control over extraneous variables, so it has the highest internal validity, the confidence that the IV really caused the change in the DV. Fred Kerlinger (1964) set out four kinds of research: laboratory experiments, field experiments, field studies and survey research. A field experiment manipulates an IV in a real-life setting, as when Irving Piliavin and colleagues (1969) staged a collapse on New York subway trains to see who helped; it gains realism but loses control. A field study manipulates nothing and studies relations as they exist in a factory, school or community. A field survey uses questionnaires or interviews to describe how variables are distributed in a population. For example, Aditi tests whether caffeine speeds reaction time: she randomly gives half her participants coffee and half decaffeinated coffee in a quiet laboratory, keeps the room, time and instructions identical, and measures reaction time, so any difference can be traced to the caffeine. Option d is right because the laboratory experiment combines manipulation with maximum control, the conditions needed to infer cause. Exam trap: the word 'field' in three options hides the real distinction, which is manipulation plus control. Key note: a field experiment also manipulates the IV and can test causal relations, but with weaker control over extraneous variables, so the method generally concerned with causation is the laboratory experiment, and d stands.", "topic": "Laboratory experiment and causation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which One of the following scales takes measure from true zero?", "options": {"a": "Nominal", "b": "Ordinal", "c": "Interval", "d": "Ratio"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the ratio scale is the scale whose measurements start from a true zero. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), in 'On the theory of scales of measurement' in Science, described four levels of measurement, each adding one property. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, blood group, jersey numbers); the numbers carry no order. An ordinal scale adds order (class rank, ratings of agreement), but the distances between ranks are not equal. An interval scale adds equal intervals, so differences are meaningful, but its zero is arbitrary: 0 degrees Celsius is just the freezing point of water, not an absence of temperature, so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20 degrees. IQ and most psychological test scores are treated as interval. A ratio scale adds a true (absolute) zero, where zero means none of the quantity: height, weight, reaction time, number of errors, temperature in kelvin. Only here do ratios make sense. For example, if Kiran answers in 400 milliseconds and Dev in 800 milliseconds, Dev took twice as long, a statement that is valid because 0 milliseconds means no time at all. The memory hook is NOIR: nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio, in rising order of information. Option d is right because a true zero is the defining feature of the ratio scale. Exam trap: the interval scale (option c) also has equal units and is often confused with ratio; the difference is that the interval zero is arbitrary, while the ratio zero is real.", "topic": "Ratio scale and true zero", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"mphil-cp-2021": [{"q": "The tendency to attribute one's own feelings and thoughts to an external object is known as", "concept": "The answer is d: the tendency to attribute one's own feelings and thoughts to an outside person or object is projection. Projection is one of the ego defence mechanisms described by Sigmund Freud and later catalogued by his daughter Anna Freud in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936). A defence mechanism is an unconscious strategy the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety. In projection, a wish, impulse or feeling that a person cannot accept in himself or herself is pushed outward and seen as belonging to someone else; the person then reacts to it 'out there' instead of facing it inside. In its extreme form it is seen in paranoid thinking, where one's own hostility is experienced as persecution by others. A broader sense of the word underlies projective tests: Lawrence K. Frank (1939) coined 'projective methods' for tests in which people read their inner needs into ambiguous material. For example, Arjun secretly resents a colleague's promotion, but he tells everyone that the colleague is jealous of him and is trying to undermine him. Option d is right because attributing one's own feelings and thoughts to something external is the textbook definition of projection. Exam trap: displacement also shifts an impulse onto another target, but it changes the target, not the owner; if Arjun displaced, he would shout at his younger brother instead of the colleague, while the anger would still be felt as his own.", "traps": {"a": "Personification is Harry Stack Sullivan's term for the mental image a person builds of self or others (the good-me, the bad-me, the good mother); it is a picture formed from experience, not a defence that pushes one's own feelings outward.", "b": "Displacement is a Freudian defence that redirects an impulse from a threatening target to a safer one (anger at the boss taken out on the family); the feeling stays one's own, only its target changes.", "c": "Cathexis is Freud's term for the investment of psychic energy (libido) in a person, object or idea; it describes where energy is attached, not attributing one's own thoughts to someone else."}}, {"q": "Impaired language comprehension following a stroke would most probably be diagnosed as", "concept": "The answer is c: impaired language comprehension after a stroke points to Wernicke's aphasia. Aphasia is a loss of language caused by brain damage, most often a stroke in the left hemisphere, where language is lateralised in most people. Carl Wernicke (1874) described patients with damage to the back of the left superior temporal gyrus (Wernicke's area) who could not understand speech. Their own speech stays fluent, with normal rhythm and largely intact grammar, but it is empty and full of wrong or invented words (paraphasias and neologisms; severe cases are called jargon aphasia), and they are often unaware of their errors. Paul Broca (1861) had earlier described the opposite pattern after damage to the left inferior frontal gyrus (Broca's area): slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with comprehension largely spared. A simple rule: Broca's is a problem of producing language (front, motor), Wernicke's is a problem of understanding it (back, sensory). For example, after a stroke Meera's grandfather talks quickly and easily, but his sentences make no sense and he cannot follow a simple request such as 'close your eyes'. Option c is right because poor comprehension with fluent speech is the hallmark of Wernicke's (receptive, fluent) aphasia. Exam trap: Broca's aphasia is the best known aphasia, so students pick it for any language loss; but Broca's patients understand well and struggle to speak. The printed 'Aphagia' is also a lure: it looks like aphasia but means a swallowing problem.", "traps": {"a": "Dyslexia is a developmental difficulty in learning to read (a specific learning disorder); reading loss acquired after a stroke is called alexia, and neither is a general loss of language comprehension.", "b": "Aphagia means inability to swallow (dysphagia is difficulty swallowing); it is a feeding problem, not a language disorder. It may be a misprint of aphasia, but as printed it does not fit.", "d": "Broca's aphasia (Paul Broca, 1861) follows damage to the left inferior frontal gyrus and causes non-fluent, effortful speech with comprehension largely preserved, the opposite of what the stem describes."}}, {"q": "Which of the following stages postulated by Erikson corresponds most closely to Freud's oral stage of development?", "concept": "The answer is a: trust versus mistrust is the Erikson stage that matches Sigmund Freud's oral stage. Erik Erikson, in Childhood and Society (1950), recast Freud's psychosexual stages as eight psychosocial stages covering the whole life span. Each stage centres on a crisis between the person's needs and the demands of the social world, and a good resolution leaves a lasting strength or virtue. The first stage, trust versus mistrust, runs from birth to about 1 year (some texts say 18 months), the same period as Freud's oral stage, when pleasure centres on the mouth and the baby depends completely on a caregiver for feeding. Erikson even labelled this stage 'oral-sensory'. Where Freud stressed oral gratification, Erikson stressed the relationship: reliable feeding, comfort and contact teach the infant that the world is dependable, and the virtue of hope emerges; neglect or inconsistency breeds mistrust. For example, baby Anaya, whose mother comes promptly when she cries, learns to expect that her needs will be met. The first four stages map onto Freud's: trust (oral), autonomy versus shame and doubt (anal), initiative versus guilt (phallic) and industry versus inferiority (latency). Option a is right because trust versus mistrust covers the same first year of feeding and dependence as the oral stage. Exam trap: autonomy versus shame and doubt is the second stage and matches Freud's anal stage (toilet training), so a one-step slip is the usual error.", "traps": {"b": "Initiative versus guilt is Erikson's third stage (about 3 to 6 years), the counterpart of Freud's phallic stage, when children plan and start activities of their own.", "c": "Integrity versus despair is Erikson's eighth and last stage (late adulthood), when people review their lives; Freud's stages end with the genital stage at puberty, so it has no Freudian counterpart.", "d": "Autonomy versus shame and doubt is Erikson's second stage (about 1 to 3 years), which matches Freud's anal stage and toilet training, not the oral stage."}}, {"q": "What three styles of parenting did Baumrind identify?", "concept": "The answer is b: Diana Baumrind identified authoritarian, authoritative and permissive parenting. Diana Baumrind (1966, 1967) observed preschool children and their parents at home and in the laboratory, and sorted parents on two broad dimensions: how demanding and controlling they are, and how warm and responsive they are. Authoritarian parents are high in control and low in warmth: strict rules, obedience demanded, little explanation ('because I said so'). Authoritative parents are high in both: firm, clear limits together with warmth, reasons and give-and-take; their children were the most self-reliant, self-controlled and content. Permissive parents are warm but make few demands and set few limits. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) later split low-control parenting into indulgent (permissive) and neglectful or uninvolved, giving the four-style model taught today. For example, when 12-year-old Kabir wants to stay out late, an authoritarian parent says 'no, and don't argue', an authoritative parent explains why 9 pm is the limit and listens to his case, and a permissive parent says 'come home whenever you like'. Option b is right because these three are exactly the styles in Baumrind's original work. Exam trap: autocratic, democratic and laissez-faire sound like parenting styles, and authoritative parenting is often explained as 'democratic', but that trio is Kurt Lewin's leadership styles. Also, authoritarian and authoritative are easy to swap: authoritative is the warm one.", "traps": {"a": "Autocratic, democratic and laissez-faire are the leadership climates studied by Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939) in boys' clubs; they describe group leaders, not Baumrind's parenting styles.", "c": "Preconventional, conventional and postconventional are Lawrence Kohlberg's three levels of moral reasoning, not styles of parenting.", "d": "Moving against, moving toward and moving away from people are Karen Horney's three neurotic trends, ways of coping with basic anxiety, not parenting styles."}}, {"q": "A person sees an accident, runs away, and feels afraid. This sequence is explicitly predicted by which of the following theories?", "concept": "The answer is d: seeing an accident, running, and then feeling afraid is the sequence predicted by the James-Lange theory. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) independently proposed that emotion follows the body, not the other way round. First we perceive an exciting event, then the body responds automatically (running, a racing heart, trembling), and the felt emotion is our perception of those bodily changes. In James's own words, we are 'afraid because we tremble', not the reverse; textbooks often put it as 'we are afraid because we run'. Common sense says the opposite: see the accident, feel fear, then run. The theory implies that each emotion has its own pattern of bodily change. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked it: the viscera respond too slowly and too similarly across emotions to tell them apart, and animals whose viscera were surgically cut off from the brain still showed emotional behaviour. Cannon and Philip Bard proposed instead that the thalamus sends signals at the same time to the cortex (the feeling) and to the body (the arousal). Option d is right because the stem places running before fear, which is exactly the body-first order of the James-Lange theory. Exam trap: the Cannon-Bard theory also involves bodily arousal, but it says fear and running happen together and independently; any stem where the bodily reaction comes first and the feeling follows is James-Lange.", "traps": {"a": "Sheldon's theory is William Sheldon's somatotype theory (1940s), which linked body build (endomorph, mesomorph, ectomorph) to temperament; it is a personality theory, not a theory of emotion.", "b": "The Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon, 1927, with Philip Bard) holds that the thalamus triggers bodily arousal and felt emotion at the same time, so it does not predict running first and fear afterwards.", "c": "Signal detection theory (David Green and John Swets, 1966) explains how people detect faint stimuli under uncertainty by separating sensitivity from response bias; it says nothing about the order of arousal and emotion."}}, {"q": "A young child who does not smile at people and who is indifferent to cuddling exhibits behavior typical of", "concept": "The answer is c: a young child who does not smile at people and is indifferent to cuddling shows behaviour typical of autistic disorder. Leo Kanner (1943) first described 11 children with 'autistic disturbances of affective contact': from early life they showed little interest in people, poor eye contact, no social smile, no reaching out to be picked up, and an anxious insistence on sameness. Parents often reported that such babies did not mould to the body when held, or stiffened and seemed indifferent to being cuddled. DSM-IV (1994) called the condition autistic disorder, one of the pervasive developmental disorders, with three groups of features: impaired social interaction, impaired communication, and restricted, repetitive behaviour and interests. DSM-5 (2013) merged it with Asperger's disorder and related conditions into autism spectrum disorder, with two domains: social communication and interaction, and restricted, repetitive behaviour, both present from early childhood. For example, two-year-old Vihaan does not smile back when his grandmother smiles, does not look up when called, wriggles away from hugs and spends hours lining up toy cars. Option c is right because a lack of social smiling and indifference to physical affection are core signs of impaired social reciprocity in autism. Exam trap: separation anxiety disorder also concerns the child's bond with caregivers, but it is the reverse picture: the child is strongly attached and becomes distressed when parted, rather than indifferent to closeness.", "traps": {"a": "Enuresis is repeated wetting of the bed or clothes after about age 5, an elimination disorder; it says nothing about social smiling or response to affection.", "b": "Separation anxiety disorder is excessive fear and distress on separation from attachment figures; the child clings to caregivers rather than being indifferent to closeness.", "d": "ADHD is a persistent pattern of inattention and/or hyperactivity and impulsivity; these children usually seek and enjoy social contact, so a missing social smile and indifference to cuddling do not define it."}}, {"q": "The primary feature of catatonic schizophrenia is", "concept": "The answer is a: the primary feature of catatonic schizophrenia is motor abnormality. Catatonia is a marked disturbance of psychomotor behaviour. Karl Kahlbaum (1874) first described it as a separate illness, and Emil Kraepelin (1899) later folded it into dementia praecox, so catatonic schizophrenia became one of the classic subtypes in DSM-IV and ICD-10. The signs sit at two extremes. At one end is stupor: the person is mute, motionless and unresponsive, may hold odd postures for hours (posturing, catalepsy), and may let the examiner mould the limbs into positions that are then kept, like wax (waxy flexibility). Negativism, resisting or ignoring every instruction, belongs here too. At the other end is catatonic excitement: purposeless, frenzied overactivity. Other signs include stereotypies, mannerisms, grimacing, echolalia (repeating others' words) and echopraxia (copying others' movements). DSM-5 (2013) dropped the schizophrenia subtypes; catatonia is now a specifier that can attach to schizophrenia, mood disorders or medical conditions, and it needs at least three of twelve signs. For example, Ravi stands frozen in one corner of the ward for hours, does not speak, and keeps his arm raised in the air after the doctor lifts it. Option a is right because catatonia is defined by disturbed movement and posture, not by the content of thought or perception. Exam trap: hallucinations and paranoia were the hallmark of the old paranoid subtype, and inappropriate affect belonged to the old disorganised (hebephrenic) subtype; examiners mix up the signature features of the subtypes.", "traps": {"b": "Auditory hallucinations, especially voices, are the most common hallucinations in schizophrenia and were prominent in the old paranoid subtype, but they are not what defines catatonia.", "c": "Inappropriate affect (laughing at sad news) was the signature of the old disorganised or hebephrenic subtype, together with disorganised speech and behaviour, not of the catatonic subtype.", "d": "Paranoia, with prominent delusions of persecution or grandeur, defined the old paranoid subtype; catatonia is a disturbance of movement, not of belief."}}, {"q": "The technique used to extinguish fear by forcing the patient to remain in the anxiety-arousing situation is called", "concept": "The answer is b: the technique that extinguishes fear by making the patient remain in the anxiety-arousing situation is flooding. Flooding is a form of exposure therapy. The client is exposed, usually in real life (in vivo), to the most feared stimulus at full intensity, all at once, and is prevented from escaping. It rests on classical extinction: when the conditioned stimulus (the feared object) occurs again and again without the feared outcome, the conditioned fear response weakens. Escape normally keeps fear alive. In the two-factor theory of Orval Hobart Mowrer (1947), fears are learnt by classical conditioning and maintained by operant avoidance, because each escape is rewarded by relief, so the person never learns that the danger is not real. When escape is blocked, anxiety rises to a peak and then falls through habituation, and the client learns that nothing terrible happens. Sessions are long, because stopping while anxiety is still high could strengthen the fear. For example, Ishaan, who is terrified of lifts, rides a lift up and down a tall building with his therapist for two hours; his panic peaks in the first 20 minutes and then slowly fades. Option b is right because keeping the client in the feared situation, with no escape, until the fear extinguishes is the definition of flooding. Exam trap: systematic desensitization is also exposure-based, but it is gradual and paired with relaxation, whereas flooding starts at the top of the fear hierarchy with no relaxation. Key note: implosive therapy (Thomas Stampfl and Donald Levis, 1967) also keeps the client in intense fear, but in imagination, with exaggerated scenes; because the stem speaks of remaining in the anxiety-arousing situation itself, it describes flooding, and the answer b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Inhibition points to Joseph Wolpe's (1958) reciprocal inhibition, the principle behind systematic desensitization in which relaxation blocks anxiety; it is a counterconditioning mechanism, not a technique of keeping the client in high anxiety.", "c": "Systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) exposes the client gradually, step by step up a fear hierarchy, while deeply relaxed; it avoids, rather than forces, prolonged high anxiety.", "d": "Implosion (implosive therapy, Thomas Stampfl and Donald Levis, 1967) is the imaginal cousin of flooding: the client imagines exaggerated, often psychodynamically themed scenes rather than remaining in the real feared situation."}}, {"q": "Clinical Psychology deals with the practical aspect of", "concept": "The answer is a: clinical psychology deals with the practical, applied side of abnormal psychology. Abnormal psychology (psychopathology) is the scientific study of disordered behaviour and experience: what the disorders are, how they are classified and what causes them. Clinical psychology takes that knowledge into practice: the assessment, diagnosis, treatment and prevention of psychological disorders and distress, together with research on these. Lightner Witmer opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896, to help children with learning and behaviour problems, and he first used the term 'clinical psychology' in print in 1907, in his new journal The Psychological Clinic. Put simply, abnormal psychology asks 'what is wrong, and why?', while clinical psychology asks 'how do we assess and help this person?'. For example, Riya learns the symptoms and causes of obsessive-compulsive disorder in her abnormal psychology course; in her clinical training she learns to rate the symptoms on the Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale, make a diagnosis and carry out exposure and response prevention with a patient. Option a is right because clinical psychology is the applied branch that puts the knowledge of abnormal psychology to work with real patients. Exam trap: child psychology and educational psychology also have applied wings, and Witmer's first clients were children with school problems, which tempts students; but clinical psychology as a whole is defined by its link to psychopathology, not by an age group or a setting.", "traps": {"b": "Educational psychology applies psychology to teaching and learning (Edward Thorndike's Educational Psychology, 1903, is often taken as its starting point); its focus is instruction, not psychological disorder.", "c": "Child psychology studies normal development in childhood; clinical work with children is one application, but clinical psychology as a whole is not the practical side of child psychology.", "d": "Experimental psychology studies basic processes such as perception, learning and memory by controlled laboratory methods; clinical psychology borrows its methods but is not its practical aspect."}}, {"q": "Confabulation is often a symptom of", "concept": "The answer is b: confabulation is a classic symptom of Korsakoff's syndrome. Confabulation is the honest production of false memories: the person fills gaps in memory with invented or misplaced stories and believes them, with no intent to deceive, which separates it from lying or malingering. Sergei Korsakoff (1887) described the syndrome in people with long-term heavy drinking. The cause is a deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1), from poor diet and poor absorption in chronic alcohol use, which damages the mammillary bodies and the mediodorsal (dorsomedial) thalamus. It often follows Wernicke's encephalopathy (Carl Wernicke, 1881), an acute state of confusion, ataxia and eye movement problems such as nystagmus and ophthalmoplegia; together they are called Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. Korsakoff's syndrome brings severe anterograde amnesia (new events cannot be stored), some retrograde amnesia, and confabulation, while attention and general intelligence are relatively spared. For example, Ramesh, a long-term drinker who has been on the ward for weeks, tells the doctor warmly that he went to the market this morning and met his brother there; he is not lying, he simply cannot remember and fills the gap. Option b is right because confabulation, together with anterograde amnesia, is the hallmark of Korsakoff's syndrome. Exam trap: schizophrenia also produces false beliefs and odd stories, but those are delusions, fixed false beliefs held against evidence, whereas confabulation is a memory symptom that fills gaps and often changes from one telling to the next.", "traps": {"a": "Obsessive neurosis (now obsessive-compulsive disorder) involves intrusive, unwanted thoughts that the person recognises as his or her own; memory is intact and there is no confabulation.", "c": "Schizophrenia produces delusions and hallucinations, which are disorders of belief and perception; memory gaps filled with honest false stories are not its core feature.", "d": "Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder with episodes of mania and depression; grandiose talk in mania is not confabulation, because it does not arise from amnesia."}}, {"q": "Which of these illnesses is related to abnormality in the dopaminergic system?", "concept": "The answer is d: both Parkinson's disease and schizophrenia are linked to abnormality in the dopaminergic system. Dopamine is a catecholamine neurotransmitter that travels along several major pathways. In Parkinson's disease, the dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra die, so the nigrostriatal pathway to the striatum (caudate and putamen) loses its dopamine. The result is tremor at rest, rigidity, slowed movement (bradykinesia) and unstable posture. Treatment replaces the missing transmitter with L-dopa, a precursor that crosses the blood-brain barrier. Arvid Carlsson (Nobel Prize 2000) showed that dopamine is a neurotransmitter, and in 1963, with Margit Lindqvist, proposed that antipsychotic drugs work by blocking catecholamine (monoamine) receptors, dopamine among them. This grew into the dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia: overactivity of dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway produces positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. The evidence is that antipsychotics block D2 receptors, their clinical potency tracks how strongly they bind D2, and amphetamine, which releases dopamine, can cause a paranoid psychosis. The two illnesses sit at opposite ends of dopamine activity. For example, when the L-dopa dose of Kabir's grandfather, who has Parkinson's disease, is raised, he begins to see people who are not there; patients given older antipsychotics, in turn, can develop parkinsonian (extrapyramidal) side effects. Option d is right because Parkinson's disease involves too little dopamine and schizophrenia too much, so both are dopaminergic disorders. Exam trap: options a and b are each true but incomplete, and the word 'only' makes them wrong; Alzheimer's disease is the lure for neurodegeneration, but its core transmitter loss is acetylcholine.", "traps": {"a": "Schizophrenia is linked to dopamine through the dopamine hypothesis, but 'only' is wrong: Parkinson's disease is the classic dopamine-loss disorder, so this option is incomplete.", "b": "Parkinson's disease is indeed a loss of substantia nigra dopamine neurons, but 'only' leaves out schizophrenia, the other classic dopamine disorder.", "c": "Alzheimer's disease is mainly a loss of acetylcholine neurons (nucleus basalis of Meynert), with amyloid plaques and tau tangles, and is treated with cholinesterase inhibitors; it is a cholinergic, not a dopaminergic, disorder."}}, {"q": "A child with a mental age of 10.7 and a chronological age of 9.5 would have a ratio IQ of about", "concept": "The answer is c: a child with a mental age of 10.7 and a chronological age of 9.5 has a ratio IQ of about 113. The formula is ratio IQ = (mental age / chronological age) x 100. Here 10.7 / 9.5 = 1.126, and 1.126 x 100 = 112.6, which rounds to 113. A quick check: 9.5 x 1.13 is about 10.7. Mental age (MA) is the age level at which a child performs on a test. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905, revised 1908) arranged tasks by the age at which typical children pass them, so a child who passes the tasks of typical ten-year-olds has an MA of 10. William Stern (1912) divided MA by chronological age (CA) to get a quotient, and Lewis Terman multiplied it by 100 in the 1916 Stanford-Binet, giving the intelligence quotient. An IQ of 100 means MA equals CA; above 100 the child is ahead of age, below 100 behind. This child is about 1.2 years ahead, a lead of about 13 percent, so the IQ is a little above 100. The ratio IQ fails for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15), which compares a person with others of the same age. Option c is right because 10.7 divided by 9.5, times 100, gives 112.6, about 113. Exam trap: dividing the wrong way round (9.5 / 10.7 x 100, about 89) is the classic slip; it gives an IQ below 100 for a child who is actually ahead of age.", "traps": {"a": "132 would need a mental age of about 12.5 at a chronological age of 9.5 (12.5 / 9.5 x 100 is about 132); it overstates the child's lead.", "b": "125 would need a mental age of about 11.9 (9.5 x 1.25); a lead of only 1.2 years gives a smaller ratio.", "d": "103 would need a mental age of only about 9.8 (9.5 x 1.03), almost equal to chronological age; it understates the lead."}}, {"q": "Which archetype represents the feminine side of men?", "concept": "The answer is b: the anima is the archetype that represents the feminine side of men. Carl Jung, who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913, divided the unconscious into a personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed experiences, organised into complexes) and a deeper collective unconscious shared by all humans and inherited from our ancestral past. Its contents are archetypes: universal, inborn tendencies to form certain images, seen in the recurring figures of myths, dreams and religions across cultures. Jung held that every person carries qualities of the other sex. The anima is the unconscious feminine image in a man, built from humanity's long experience of women; the animus is the unconscious masculine image in a woman. Both shape the choice of partner and are often projected onto a beloved. Other major archetypes are the persona (the social mask we show others), the shadow (the dark, primitive side we refuse to own) and the self (the archetype of wholeness, often symbolised by the mandala, and the goal of individuation). For example, Rahul, a tough and practical engineer, keeps dreaming of a wise, gentle woman who guides him; a Jungian analyst would read her as his anima, pointing to tenderness he has not yet developed. Option b is right because anima is Jung's name for the feminine archetype in men. Exam trap: anima and animus are easy to swap; remember that anima, ending in 'a' like many feminine Latin words, is the feminine side, found in men, and animus is the masculine side, found in women.", "traps": {"a": "The persona is Jung's archetype of the social mask, the public face a person presents to fit social roles; it is not tied to either sex.", "c": "The animus is the reverse archetype: the masculine side in a woman's unconscious, not the feminine side of men.", "d": "The shadow is the dark, primitive side of the personality that a person refuses to acknowledge and often projects onto others; it is not the feminine side."}}, {"q": "The concepts like \"Introspection\" and \"Conscious Experience\" are associated with", "concept": "The answer is c: introspection and conscious experience are the hallmark concepts of structuralism. Wilhelm Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879 and defined psychology as the study of immediate conscious experience. His English student Edward Titchener took this approach to Cornell University in 1892 and named it structuralism (1898). The aim was to analyse consciousness into its basic elements, as a chemist analyses a compound, and to find the laws by which those elements combine. The method was trained introspection: observers, drilled through thousands of trials, reported the raw sensations, images and feelings of an experience, not its meaning. Describing the object ('I see an apple') instead of the raw qualities (red, round, shiny) was called the stimulus error. Titchener found elements of three kinds: sensations, images and affections (feelings). Structuralism faded after Titchener's death in 1927, because introspective reports could not be checked or agreed between laboratories. For example, Meera, as a trained observer, listens to a tuning fork and reports only pitch, loudness, duration and clearness, never 'a tuning fork'. Option c is right because structuralism is defined by the analysis of conscious experience through introspection. Exam trap: functionalism also studied consciousness and also used introspection, so it is the strongest lure; but functionalists asked what consciousness is for, how it helps us adapt, not what it is made of.", "traps": {"a": "Functionalism (William James, John Dewey, James Angell) studied the purpose of consciousness, how it helps the organism adapt, rather than its elements; it used introspection but is not defined by it.", "b": "Behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913) rejected introspection and consciousness altogether and studied only observable behaviour.", "d": "Gestalt psychology (Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Kohler, Kurt Koffka, 1912) studied conscious experience as organised wholes and attacked the structuralist breakdown into elements."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is based on the principle of classical conditioning?", "concept": "The answer is c: systematic desensitization is based on the principle of classical conditioning. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, treated a phobia as a conditioned emotional response and argued that a response incompatible with anxiety can replace it: a person cannot be deeply relaxed and anxious at the same moment (reciprocal inhibition). This is counterconditioning, a classical (respondent) procedure, because a new response is attached to the old stimulus by pairing. There are three steps. First, the client learns deep muscle relaxation, usually Edmund Jacobson's progressive relaxation (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, from the least to the most frightening situation, rated on Wolpe's Subjective Units of Disturbance scale. Third, while relaxed, the client imagines each step in turn, moving up only when a step no longer causes anxiety. Earlier, Mary Cover Jones (1924) had removed little Peter's fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit gradually closer while he ate, an early counterconditioning case. For example, Arjun, who panics about exams, relaxes and pictures first hearing the exam date, then studying the night before, then entering the hall, and finally opening the paper. Option c is right because desensitization works by pairing feared stimuli with a new, incompatible response (relaxation), not by reinforcing behaviour after it occurs. Exam trap: the other three options are all operant techniques that change behaviour through its consequences, so the item simply tests the classical versus operant split.", "traps": {"a": "A token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) gives tokens, later exchanged for rewards, after desired behaviour; it is operant conditioning through secondary reinforcement.", "b": "Differential reinforcement (for example, reinforcing other or incompatible behaviour) rewards some responses and withholds reward from others; it is an operant technique.", "d": "Contingency management makes rewards or penalties depend on a target behaviour, for example vouchers for drug-free urine samples; it is operant, not classical."}}, {"q": "The sound of a sizzling frying pan causes John to salivate. The sound of the sizzling frying pan is", "concept": "The answer is b: the sound of the sizzling frying pan is a conditioned stimulus. Classical conditioning was discovered by Ivan Pavlov during his studies of digestion in dogs (Nobel Prize 1904 for the physiology of digestion): a neutral stimulus comes to trigger a response after it has been paired repeatedly with a stimulus that triggers the response naturally. Four terms describe the process. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS) produces a response without any learning; here, the food in the mouth. The unconditioned response (UCR) is that unlearned reaction: salivating to food. The conditioned stimulus (CS) starts out neutral and produces no salivation on its own, but after pairing with the UCS it comes to produce the response; here, the sizzle, which John has heard again and again just before eating. The conditioned response (CR) is the learned reaction to the CS: salivating to the sizzle. A newborn would not salivate at a frying pan; John learned to because the sound reliably predicts food. Two quick tests help. First, ask 'would this work without any experience?'; if not, it is conditioned. Second, stimuli are things that happen to the person (sounds, sights, smells), while responses are what the person does (salivating). Option b is right because the sizzle is a once-neutral sound that now triggers salivation only through its learned link with food. Exam trap: the CR and the UCR are both salivation, so students fix on the response; but the question asks about the sound, which is a stimulus, and the only learned stimulus here is the CS.", "traps": {"a": "The unconditioned stimulus is the one that produces salivation without learning, here the food itself; the sizzle works only because it has been paired with food.", "c": "The unconditioned response is the unlearned salivation to food in the mouth; the sizzle is a sound, a stimulus, not a response.", "d": "The conditioned response is John's learned salivation to the sizzle; the sizzle is the stimulus that triggers it, not the response itself."}}, {"q": "_________ approach is popularly rooted in Gestalt psychology.", "concept": "The answer is a: the holistic approach (printed 'Wholistic') is the one rooted in Gestalt psychology. Gestalt psychology began with Max Wertheimer's (1912) paper on the phi phenomenon, the apparent movement seen when two lights flash one after the other, with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka as co-founders in Germany. Gestalt is German for form, shape or configuration. Its central claim is that the whole is different from the sum of its parts: experience comes organised into meaningful wholes, and breaking it into elements, as the structuralists did, destroys what matters. A melody stays the same melody when played in another key, though every note has changed. From this came the laws of perceptual organisation (proximity, similarity, closure, continuity, figure and ground), insight learning (Kohler's chimpanzee studies, The Mentality of Apes, German edition 1917) and, through Kurt Lewin's work in the 1930s, field theory. 'Holistic' is the general label for any approach that studies experience or the person as an organised whole, an idea later carried into Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman, 1951) and humanistic psychology. For example, Anika sees a ring of dots as a circle, not as twelve separate dots, because her mind organises them into a whole. Option a is right because the whole-before-parts view is exactly what Gestalt psychology stands for. Exam trap: the cognitive approach is tempting because Gestalt work on perception and insight did feed into cognitive psychology, but it grew mainly from information processing and the computer analogy. Key note: some textbooks call Gestalt psychology a forerunner of the cognitive approach, so d is not absurd; but the approach defined by Gestalt's whole-before-parts principle is the holistic one, so the answer a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The stimulus-response approach is behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913, and later B. F. Skinner), which breaks behaviour into S-R units, the kind of element analysis Gestalt psychologists rejected.", "c": "The dynamic and psychoanalytic approach comes from Sigmund Freud and stresses unconscious drives and conflict; it is not rooted in Gestalt psychology.", "d": "The cognitive approach borrowed some Gestalt ideas (insight, perceptual organisation), but its main roots are the information-processing model and the computer analogy of the 1950s and 1960s."}}, {"q": "A subject takes longer naming the color of the ink used when the word green is written in red than when the word button is written in red. This is an example of", "concept": "The answer is a: taking longer to name red ink when the word is 'green' than when the word is 'button' is the Stroop effect. John Ridley Stroop (1935) showed that naming the ink colour of a colour word is slower, and more error-prone, when the word names a different colour (the word GREEN printed in red) than when the stimulus is neutral, such as a row of coloured patches or a non-colour word. The effect arises because reading is so practised in literate adults that it happens automatically: the meaning of the word is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, deliberate task of naming the colour. The person must suppress the automatic reading response, and that costs time. Here 'button' carries no colour meaning, so it causes little conflict, while 'green' sets up a clash with red. The task is now a standard measure of selective attention, response inhibition and executive control, sensitive to frontal (anterior cingulate and prefrontal) dysfunction, and an 'emotional Stroop' with threat words is used to study attentional bias in anxiety. Option a is right because the stem describes exactly Stroop's interference between a colour word and a mismatched ink colour. Exam trap: automatic processing is the near-miss, because it is the mechanism that causes the effect; but the question asks what the observation is an example of, and the named phenomenon is the Stroop effect.", "traps": {"b": "Automatic processing (Walter Schneider and Richard Shiffrin, 1977) is fast, effortless processing that runs without intention; it explains why the Stroop effect happens, but it is the mechanism, not the name of this phenomenon.", "c": "Metacognition (John Flavell, 1979) is thinking about one's own thinking, such as judging how well you know a topic; it has nothing to do with colour-word interference.", "d": "Opponent-process theory is Ewald Hering's account of colour vision in red-green, blue-yellow and black-white pairs (or Richard Solomon's theory of emotion); it explains afterimages, not slower colour naming."}}, {"q": "Schizophrenia was formerly known as", "concept": "The answer is b: schizophrenia was formerly known as dementia praecox. The French psychiatrist Benedict Morel used 'demence precoce' for a mental deterioration beginning in young people. The German psychiatrist Emil Kraepelin took up the Latin form, dementia praecox ('premature dementia'), in the 1893 edition of his textbook. In the sixth edition (1899) he drew a line through the psychoses by their course: dementia praecox began in youth and ran a downhill course towards deterioration, whereas manic-depressive insanity came in episodes with recovery between them. He grouped catatonic, hebephrenic and paranoid forms under dementia praecox. Eugen Bleuler (1908 lecture; 1911 book) renamed it schizophrenia, a splitting of mental functions such as thought, emotion and will (not a split personality), because he saw that the illness did not always start young or end in dementia. Option b is right because dementia praecox is Kraepelin's name for the illness that Bleuler renamed schizophrenia. Exam trap: the other options are also old disease names, and Huntington's and Pick's disease do cause dementia; but they are neurological dementias with known brain pathology, not the early name of a psychotic disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Huntington's disease (George Huntington, 1872) is an inherited, autosomal dominant disorder of the basal ganglia causing chorea and dementia; it was never a name for schizophrenia.", "c": "Pick's disease (Arnold Pick, 1892) is a frontotemporal dementia with personality change and language loss; it is a neurodegenerative disease, not the old term for schizophrenia.", "d": "Cretinism is the old term for untreated congenital hypothyroidism, which causes stunted growth and intellectual disability; it is unrelated to schizophrenia."}}, {"q": "The method of loci is an example of", "concept": "The answer is c: the method of loci is an example of a mnemonic. A mnemonic is any technique that improves memory by adding organisation, meaning or vivid imagery to new material and linking it to something already well known. In the method of loci (Latin locus, 'place'), you first picture a familiar route, such as the rooms of your home, and then 'place' each item to be remembered at a spot along it, ideally as a striking image. To recall, you walk the route in your mind and 'see' each item where you left it. The method is credited to the Greek poet Simonides of Ceos (about 556 to 468 BCE) who, the story goes, identified the guests crushed when a banquet hall collapsed by remembering where each one had been sitting. It works because spatial memory and imagery give strong retrieval cues, and the route keeps the items in order. Other mnemonics include the peg-word system ('one is a bun'), acronyms such as OCEAN for the Big Five, and the keyword method. For example, Kabir must remember milk, eggs, bread and mangoes; he imagines milk pouring over his front door, eggs cracking on the sofa, bread piled on the stairs and mangoes in the bathtub, then walks through his flat in his mind at the shop. Option c is right because the method of loci is a deliberate memory aid, which is what a mnemonic is. Exam trap: primacy and recency are also about remembering lists in order, but they are serial position effects that happen by themselves, not techniques that a person chooses to use.", "traps": {"a": "The primacy effect is better recall of the first items in a list, thought to reflect extra rehearsal into long-term memory; it is a serial position effect, not a memory technique.", "b": "The recency effect is better recall of the last items in a list, because they are still in short-term memory at test; it is also a serial position effect, not a technique.", "d": "A heuristic (Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, 1974) is a mental shortcut for making judgements and solving problems, such as the availability heuristic; it concerns reasoning, not storing information."}}, {"q": "According to Piaget the ability to \"think like a scientist\" is characterized of which of the following developmental stages?", "concept": "The answer is d: according to Jean Piaget, the ability to 'think like a scientist' marks the formal operational stage. Piaget described four stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (birth to about 2), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11 or 12 onwards). In the formal operational stage, thought is freed from concrete reality. The adolescent can reason about abstract ideas and hypothetical possibilities, and can use hypothetico-deductive reasoning: form a hypothesis, deduce what should follow, and test it systematically while holding other factors constant, just as a scientist does. Barbel Inhelder and Jean Piaget (1958), in The Growth of Logical Thinking from Childhood to Adolescence, used the pendulum problem: children had to find what controls how fast a pendulum swings (the length of the string, the weight, the height of release or the force of the push). Concrete operational children changed several factors at once and drew muddled conclusions; formal operational adolescents varied one factor at a time and found that only the length matters. For example, 14-year-old Riya, testing why her plants wilt, waters two identical pots differently while keeping light and soil the same. Not everyone reaches this stage, and many adults use it only in familiar fields. Option d is right because systematic hypothesis testing and abstract reasoning define formal operations. Exam trap: concrete operational children are logical too (they master conservation, classification and seriation), which makes c tempting; but their logic works only on concrete, present objects, not on hypotheses.", "traps": {"a": "The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) brings symbolic thought and language but is egocentric and fails conservation, centring on one feature at a time; it is far from scientific reasoning.", "b": "The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) is thinking through the senses and actions, with object permanence as its key achievement; there are no mental operations yet.", "c": "The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) brings logical operations such as conservation and classification, but only for concrete, present objects, not for abstract hypotheses."}}, {"q": "A graphical representation of correlational data is called a", "concept": "The answer is c: a graphical representation of correlational data is called a scatterplot. A scatterplot, or scatter diagram, shows the relationship between two variables measured on the same people. Each person is one dot: the score on one variable (X) sets its horizontal position and the score on the other (Y) sets its vertical position. The pattern of the whole cloud shows the correlation at a glance. If the dots rise from lower left to upper right, the correlation is positive; if they fall, it is negative. The more tightly the dots cluster around a straight line, the stronger the relationship; a round, shapeless cloud means little or no linear relationship. The correlation coefficient r, Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment correlation, puts a number on this, from minus 1 to plus 1. A scatterplot also reveals what r can hide: curved (nonlinear) relationships, outliers and a restricted range. For example, Meera plots hours of study (X) against exam marks (Y) for 30 classmates; the dots slope upwards with moderate spread, suggesting a moderate positive correlation. Even so, correlation never proves causation: more study may raise marks, or keen students may simply do both. Option c is right because the scatterplot is the standard graph for showing the relationship between two sets of paired scores. Exam trap: the bell curve and the skewed distribution are also graphs, but each shows how a single variable is distributed, not how two variables relate.", "traps": {"a": "A bell curve is the normal distribution, the symmetrical shape of how a single variable's scores spread around the mean; it does not show the relationship between two variables.", "b": "Chi square (Karl Pearson, 1900) is a statistical test for frequency or categorical data, for example whether two categorical variables are associated; it is a test, not a graph of correlational data.", "d": "A skewed distribution is an asymmetrical frequency distribution of one variable, with a long tail to the right (positive skew) or left (negative skew); it does not display a correlation."}}, {"q": "Name the test which measures set shifting?", "concept": "The answer is c: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test measures set shifting. Set shifting, or cognitive flexibility, is the executive ability to drop a rule or mental set that no longer works and switch to a new one. David Grant and Esta Berg (1948) built the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) around it. Four stimulus cards are laid out (one red triangle, two green stars, three yellow crosses, four blue circles). The person sorts a pack of response cards under them, matching by colour, form or number, but is never told the rule, only 'right' or 'wrong' after each card. After a run of correct sorts (ten in the standard version), the examiner changes the rule without warning. A flexible person notices the feedback and shifts; a person with poor set shifting keeps sorting by the old rule, making perseverative errors. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal lobe excisions made many such errors, so the WCST became the classic test of frontal executive function, widely used in schizophrenia, head injury and frontal lobe damage. For example, after a road accident Aman keeps sorting by colour for twenty cards after the rule has changed to number, even though he hears 'wrong' each time. Option c is right because the WCST's unannounced rule changes measure set shifting directly. Exam trap: all four options are neuropsychological tests, and digit span is linked to attention, so students may pick it; but digit span measures attention span and working memory, not switching between rules.", "traps": {"a": "The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test (Lauretta Bender, 1938) asks the person to copy nine designs; it assesses visuomotor integration and screens for organic impairment, not set shifting.", "b": "The Nahor-Benson test is a perceptual test in which the person copies simple figures, used in Indian batteries such as the PGI Battery of Brain Dysfunction; it assesses visual perception and visuoconstructive ability, not set shifting.", "d": "Digit span (digits forward and backward, a subtest of the Wechsler scales) measures attention span and working memory, not the ability to shift between sorting rules."}}, {"q": "Which of the following type of hallucination is most common in schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is c: auditory hallucinations are the most common type of hallucination in schizophrenia. A hallucination is a perception without any external stimulus, experienced as real and as coming from outside (in outer, objective space), unlike an illusion, which is a misreading of a real stimulus. In schizophrenia, hearing voices is by far the most frequent kind, reported by well over half of patients in most studies. The voices may speak to the person (second person: 'you are useless'), talk about the person (third person: 'he is going out now'), comment on what the person is doing, or speak the person's thoughts aloud (thought echo). Kurt Schneider (Clinical Psychopathology, English edition 1959) counted three kinds of voices among his first-rank symptoms: audible thoughts, voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and a running commentary. Visual hallucinations occur in schizophrenia too, but much less often; when they are prominent, clinicians first look for an organic cause such as delirium, drug intoxication or Lewy body dementia. Tactile hallucinations such as formication (insects crawling under the skin) suggest cocaine use or alcohol withdrawal, and olfactory ones suggest temporal lobe epilepsy. For example, Sunil, aged 23, hears two men behind the wall discussing him: 'he is useless, he should leave'; no one is there. Option c is right because voices are the commonest and most characteristic hallucinations of schizophrenia. Exam trap: visual hallucinations are the commonest type in organic states such as delirium, so a question about delirium has a different answer; for schizophrenia the answer is auditory.", "traps": {"a": "Tactile (haptic) hallucinations, such as formication, can occur in schizophrenia but are uncommon; they point more to cocaine or amphetamine use and alcohol withdrawal.", "b": "Olfactory hallucinations (smelling burning rubber or rotting flesh) are rare in schizophrenia and suggest temporal lobe epilepsy or another brain lesion.", "d": "Visual hallucinations occur in schizophrenia but far less often than voices; when prominent they point first to organic causes such as delirium, drugs or Lewy body dementia."}}, {"q": "The act in which people help others without any personal interest, motive, reason is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: helping others without any personal interest, motive or expected reward is called altruism. The word was coined by the French philosopher Auguste Comte (1798 to 1857) to mean living for others. In social psychology, altruism is helping driven purely by concern for the other person's welfare, with no expectation of reward and often at some cost to the helper. It sits inside a wider category, prosocial behaviour, which covers any voluntary act that benefits others, such as sharing, donating, comforting or rescuing, whatever the motive behind it. So all altruism is prosocial, but much prosocial behaviour is not altruistic: helping to win praise, to repay a favour or to ease one's own guilt is prosocial but self-serving. Whether pure altruism exists is a long debate. C. Daniel Batson (1981) proposed the empathy-altruism hypothesis: when we feel empathy for someone in need, we help for their sake, even when escaping the situation is easy. Robert Cialdini and colleagues (1987) replied with the negative-state relief model: we help mainly to lift our own sadness. Example: Meera sees an elderly stranger fall on a crowded Kolkata road, takes her to a clinic and leaves without giving her name; she gains nothing and loses time, so her act is altruism. Option a is right because the stem stresses the absence of any personal interest, motive or reason, which is the defining feature of altruism. Exam trap: 'prosocial' is the near-miss, because altruism is one kind of prosocial behaviour; but prosocial is the broad umbrella that also includes helping for selfish reasons, so it does not capture 'without any motive'.", "traps": {"b": "Prosocial behaviour is the broad category of any voluntary act that benefits others, whatever the motive (praise, repayment, relief of guilt); because it includes self-interested helping, it does not specifically mean helping with no motive, as altruism does.", "c": "Nurturance is the tendency to care for and protect the young, weak or helpless; Henry Murray (1938) listed it as a psychological need (n Nurturance), so it is itself a motive, which the stem rules out.", "d": "Social obligation is helping because a norm demands it, such as the social responsibility norm or the norm of reciprocity of Alvin Gouldner (1960); it is helping for a reason (duty), not helping without one."}}, {"q": "Emphasizing what comes to mind first or most readily/quickly is known as", "concept": "The answer is a: judging by what comes to mind first or most readily is a heuristic, specifically the availability heuristic. A heuristic is a mental shortcut, a rule of thumb that gives a quick, usually good enough judgement without weighing all the evidence. Herbert Simon (1955) argued that people with limited time, information and mental capacity cannot weigh every option and settle for choices that are good enough, an idea he later called bounded rationality. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1973) described the availability heuristic: we judge how frequent or likely something is by how easily examples come to mind. In their classic demonstration, people judged that more English words begin with K than have K as the third letter, although the reverse is true, because words starting with K are easier to retrieve. Vivid, recent or heavily reported events are the most available, so people overrate the risk of plane crashes and underrate quieter risks such as diabetes. Example: after watching news of two train accidents, Kabir decides trains are more dangerous than cars and drives to Pune instead, although road travel is far riskier. Option a is right because 'emphasising what comes to mind first or most readily' is the core of heuristic judgement, the availability heuristic. Exam trap: 'intuitive thought' sounds like fast, first-thought judgement, but as a textbook term it is the intuitive substage of preoperational thought described by Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980), at about 4 to 7 years, not the name of this shortcut. Key note: 'intuitive thought' also suggests the fast, automatic System 1 thinking that Daniel Kahneman (2011) describes, and heuristics do belong to System 1; but the stem describes the availability heuristic itself, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Critical thinking is slow, deliberate evaluation of evidence, assumptions and arguments before reaching a conclusion; it is the opposite of relying on whatever comes to mind first.", "c": "Intuitive thought is Jean Piaget's substage of preoperational thinking (about 4 to 7 years), when children reason from immediate perception without logical operations; it is not the term for judging by ease of recall.", "d": "Confirmation bias, shown by Peter Wason (1960) with his 2-4-6 task, is the tendency to seek and favour evidence that supports what one already believes; it concerns which evidence we look for, not what comes to mind most easily."}}, {"q": "Sex chromosome anomaly (XXY) results into the development of:", "concept": "The answer is a: an XXY sex chromosome pattern produces Klinefelter's syndrome. Humans normally have 46 chromosomes: 22 pairs of autosomes and one pair of sex chromosomes, XX in females and XY in males. Klinefelter syndrome, described by the American physician Harry Klinefelter (1942), is a 47,XXY karyotype: a male with an extra X chromosome, usually from nondisjunction (chromosomes failing to separate) when the egg or sperm is formed. Because a Y chromosome is present, the person develops as male, but the extra X lowers testosterone. Typical features are tall stature with long legs, small testes, infertility, sparse body hair, sometimes breast enlargement (gynaecomastia), and often language, reading and learning difficulties, although intelligence is usually in the average range. It is one of the commonest sex chromosome conditions, roughly 1 in 500 to 1,000 male births, and is often found only when an adult is investigated for infertility. Testosterone treatment from puberty helps physical development. Option a is right because 47,XXY is the defining karyotype of Klinefelter syndrome. Exam trap: Turner syndrome is the other famous sex chromosome anomaly and students mix the two up; Turner is 45,X (often written XO), a female with one X missing, the mirror image of an extra X, not XXY.", "traps": {"b": "Turner syndrome (Henry Turner, 1938) is 45,X (XO): a female with one X chromosome missing, short stature, ovarian failure and infertility; it is a missing sex chromosome, not an extra one.", "c": "Down syndrome (John Langdon Down, 1866) is usually trisomy 21, an extra copy of an autosome (chromosome 21), not a sex chromosome; it causes intellectual disability and characteristic facial features.", "d": "Phenylketonuria (Asbjorn Folling, 1934) is an autosomal recessive single-gene disorder in which the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase is deficient; the chromosome count is normal, and a low-phenylalanine diet prevents the intellectual disability."}}, {"q": "Who has explained intelligence in terms of Psychometric Approach?", "concept": "The answer is d: Alfred Binet is the theorist credited with explaining intelligence through the psychometric approach. The psychometric approach treats intelligence as abilities measured with standardised tests, scored against norms and analysed statistically, especially by factor analysis. Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon (1905), built the first practical intelligence test for Paris schools, to pick out children who needed special teaching. Their 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children pass them, giving the idea of mental age: a child who passes the items most 8-year-olds pass has a mental age of 8. William Stern (1912) turned this into a quotient, and Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet (1916) made the IQ famous. Earlier sensory and reaction-time tests in the style of Francis Galton (1880s) had failed to predict school grades, so Binet's scale was the first useful measure of intelligence. Factor-analytic theories, which study the structure of test scores, belong to the same tradition: Charles Spearman's g (1904), Louis Thurstone's primary mental abilities (1938), Raymond Cattell's fluid and crystallised intelligence (1963) and Arthur Jensen's Level I and Level II abilities (1969). By contrast, Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory (1985) explains intelligence through mental processes, an information-processing approach. Example: when Riya's school psychologist reports her mental age and IQ from a standardised test, she is using the approach that began with Binet. Option d is right because Binet built the first working intelligence test and the idea of mental age, the starting point of the psychometric approach to intelligence. Exam trap: Jensen and Cattell are factor-analytic theorists and look like the 'real' psychometricians, which makes the test-builder Binet seem the odd one out; in fact Binet's test is where the measurement of intelligence began. Key note: Jensen and Cattell are psychometric theorists too, so only Sternberg is clearly outside the approach; Binet is keyed as the one who first explained intelligence through measurement (mental age), and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Arthur Jensen (1969) proposed Level I (associative, rote) and Level II (conceptual) abilities and argued about the heritability of IQ; this is work inside the psychometric tradition, but he did not found it.", "b": "Raymond Cattell split general intelligence into fluid and crystallised abilities by factor analysis (later extended with John Horn), which is psychometric work built on Binet's tradition rather than its origin; James McKeen Cattell, who coined 'mental test' in 1890, is a different Cattell.", "c": "Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory (1985) divides intelligence into analytical, creative and practical parts and explains it through mental processes (components), the information-processing approach rather than test-score measurement."}}, {"q": "Paradoxical Intention is based on which of the following technique?", "concept": "The answer is b: paradoxical intention is a technique of logotherapy. Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997), a Viennese psychiatrist, built logotherapy (therapy through meaning) on the idea that the deepest human drive is the will to meaning; his book Man's Search for Meaning (1946) drew on his survival of the Nazi concentration camps. He described paradoxical intention in 1939. Many symptoms are kept alive by anticipatory anxiety: the person fears a symptom, the fear brings it on, and the cycle repeats. In paradoxical intention the client is asked to deliberately wish for, or try to produce, the very thing he fears, often with humour. A man who dreads sweating in public is told to try to sweat as much as he can; trying to produce the symptom removes the anxious struggle against it, and the symptom often fades. Frankl's second technique, dereflection, turns attention away from the symptom and towards meaningful goals. Example: Arjun lies awake every night worrying that he will not sleep; his therapist asks him to try as hard as he can to stay awake, and once the pressure to fall asleep is gone, he drifts off. Paradoxical methods were later borrowed by strategic family therapy and by CBT for insomnia, but they began in logotherapy. Option b is right because paradoxical intention is Frankl's own logotherapeutic technique. Exam trap: logotherapy is a form of existential therapy, so 'existential therapy' is the near-miss; the technique belongs specifically to Frankl's logotherapy, which is the more precise answer.", "traps": {"a": "CBT, built on Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (1960s onward) and Albert Ellis's REBT (1955), changes distorted thoughts and behaviours; CBT for insomnia later borrowed paradoxical intention, but the technique did not originate there.", "c": "Existential therapy (Rollo May, 1958; Irvin Yalom, 1980) is the broad school dealing with freedom, death, isolation and meaning; logotherapy is one branch of it, and paradoxical intention is specific to Frankl's branch, so this answer is too general.", "d": "Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman, 1951) works on here-and-now awareness with methods such as the empty chair; its 'paradoxical theory of change' (Arnold Beisser, 1970) is a different idea, that change comes from accepting what one is, not paradoxical intention."}}, {"q": "Hope, happiness, optimism and flow takes together as", "concept": "The answer is b: hope, happiness, optimism and flow are studied together as positive psychology. Positive psychology is the scientific study of what makes life go well: positive emotions, strengths of character, and the conditions that let people and communities flourish. Martin Seligman launched it in his 1998 presidential address to the American Psychological Association, arguing that psychology had spent decades on illness and damage and too little on well-being; with Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi he set out its aims in a special issue of the American Psychologist (2000). Each word in the stem is one of its signature topics. Hope theory (C. R. Snyder, 1991) sees hope as goal-directed willpower plus waypower, the ability to find paths to goals. Happiness, studied as subjective well-being by Ed Diener (1984), is life satisfaction with frequent positive and rare negative feelings. Learned optimism (Martin Seligman, 1990) is a habit of explaining bad events as temporary and specific rather than permanent and pervasive. Flow (Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi, 1990) is total absorption in a task whose challenge matches one's skill. Example: when Meera loses all sense of time while practising the sitar, she is in flow, a state that positive psychologists measure and try to increase. Option b is right because hope, happiness, optimism and flow form the core research cluster of positive psychology. Exam trap: humanistic psychology (Abraham Maslow, 1954; Carl Rogers, 1951) also stresses growth and the good life and is a forerunner, but it relied mostly on theory and case study; the research movement that grouped these four topics is positive psychology.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive psychology, named by Ulric Neisser (1967), studies mental processes such as attention, memory, language and problem solving; it does not group hope, happiness, optimism and flow as its subject.", "c": "Humanistic psychology (Abraham Maslow, Carl Rogers), the 'third force', stressed self-actualisation and free will and anticipated positive psychology, but the empirical study of hope, happiness, optimism and flow as one cluster is positive psychology.", "d": "Evolutionary psychology (Leda Cosmides and John Tooby, 1992) explains behaviour as adaptations shaped by natural selection, such as mate preferences or fear of snakes; it is not defined by these well-being topics."}}, {"q": "In treating which of the following conditions 'mand' is used as the central principle of behavior modification?", "concept": "The answer is b: the mand is the central principle of language training for children with autism. B. F. Skinner, in Verbal Behavior (1957), treated language as operant behaviour shaped by its consequences and named several verbal operants. The mand (from 'command' and 'demand') is a request controlled by what the speaker wants: the child says 'juice' and is reinforced by getting juice. The tact is a label controlled by something seen or heard ('car' on seeing a car), the echoic repeats another person's words, and the intraverbal answers someone else's words ('What do you drink?' 'Juice'). Many children with autism have little functional speech and communicate through tantrums or by pulling an adult's hand. Applied behaviour analysis (ABA), the best-known behavioural treatment for autism since Ole Ivar Lovaas (1987), includes the verbal behaviour approach of Mark Sundberg and James Partington (1998), which starts with mand training because a request brings an immediate, personally valued reward, so the child learns that words work. Mands can be spoken words, signs or picture cards. Example: Kabir, a four-year-old with autism, is taught to say or sign 'ball' and is handed the ball at once; as his mands grow, his tantrums fall. Option b is right because mand training is the core of language teaching in ABA programmes for autism. Exam trap: ADHD is also treated with behaviour modification (token economies, daily report cards, parent training), so it tempts; but those programmes target attention and conduct, not teaching a child to make requests, which is what the mand does.", "traps": {"a": "Anxiety is treated behaviourally with exposure, systematic desensitisation and relaxation, which work on fear responses; mands, which teach a child to make requests, are not its central principle.", "c": "Anhedonia, the loss of pleasure seen in depression and as a negative symptom of schizophrenia, is targeted with behavioural activation and pleasant-activity scheduling, not with mand training.", "d": "ADHD is managed with operant methods such as token economies, daily report cards and parent management training aimed at attention and impulse control; teaching requests through mands is the hallmark of autism language programmes."}}, {"q": "Bowlby's major contribution to theories of child development grew out of studies of", "concept": "The answer is b: John Bowlby's theory grew out of studies of children separated from their parents. John Bowlby (1907 to 1990), a British psychiatrist and psychoanalyst, first saw the link in Forty-Four Juvenile Thieves (1944): of 44 young thieves at his London clinic, 14 showed 'affectionless psychopathy' (no guilt, no warmth towards others), and 12 of those 14 had suffered long early separations from their mothers. His World Health Organization report Maternal Care and Mental Health (1951) set out the maternal deprivation hypothesis: an infant needs a warm, continuous relationship with a mother or permanent mother substitute, and prolonged breaks in the early years do lasting harm. James Robertson's film A Two-Year-Old Goes to Hospital (1952) showed the sequence of protest, despair and detachment in a separated child. Bowlby then drew on ethology, Konrad Lorenz's imprinting (1935) and Harry Harlow's monkeys (1958), to build attachment theory in Attachment (1969), Separation (1973) and Loss (1980): attachment is an evolved system that keeps the infant close to a caregiver, who becomes a secure base for exploration. Example: when two-year-old Ananya spends a week in hospital without her mother, she cries and searches (protest), then turns listless (despair), then seems indifferent when her mother returns (detachment), just as Bowlby and Robertson described. Option b is right because separation and deprivation studies were the root of Bowlby's work. Exam trap: option d also names a famous child study, but imitation and modelling of aggression is Albert Bandura's Bobo doll research (1961), the basis of social learning theory, not of attachment theory.", "traps": {"a": "Comparisons of fraternal and identical twins, begun by Francis Galton (1875), are the classic behaviour genetics method for separating heredity from environment; Bowlby's work did not grow out of twin studies.", "c": "Recessive gene inheritance goes back to Gregor Mendel's (1866) pea experiments and explains conditions such as PKU; it is genetics, not the basis of attachment theory.", "d": "Imitation and modelling as causes of aggression is the Bobo doll research of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), the root of social learning theory, not of Bowlby's theory."}}, {"q": "A school psychologist wishes to test a second-grade student for possible mental retardation. The most appropriate test to use would probably be the", "concept": "The answer is a: for a second grader being assessed for intellectual disability, the Stanford-Binet is the most suitable of the tests offered. 'Mental retardation' was the DSM-IV name; the current term is intellectual disability: significantly below-average intellectual functioning (an IQ about two standard deviations below the mean, roughly 70 or less), together with deficits in adaptive behaviour, beginning in the developmental period. Diagnosing it needs an individually given, standardised test of general intelligence normed for the child's age, plus an adaptive behaviour measure such as Edgar Doll's Vineland Social Maturity Scale (1935). A second grader is about 7 to 8 years old. The Stanford-Binet, Lewis Terman's (1916) American revision of the Binet-Simon scale, was built for children; its fifth edition (Gale Roid, 2003) covers ages 2 to 85 and over, gives a full-scale IQ with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, and has many easy items at the low end, so it measures low ability well in young children. Option a is right because the Stanford-Binet is an individual intelligence test suited to a young child and widely used to diagnose intellectual disability. Exam trap: the WAIS is also an individual IQ test and looks right, but it is the adult Wechsler scale (16 years and over); the child version, the WISC, is not offered here. Key note: today the WISC (ages 6 to 16) would be just as usual for a second grader, but it is not offered; among these options a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) is a self-report inventory of adult personality and psychopathology (the MMPI-A covers 14 to 18); it measures no intellectual ability and cannot be given to a 7-year-old.", "c": "The WAIS (David Wechsler, 1955; WAIS-IV, 2008) is an individual intelligence test, but it is normed for adults aged 16 and over, so a second grader is far below its age range.", "d": "The Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935) is a projective storytelling test of needs and conflicts; it gives no IQ and cannot diagnose intellectual disability."}}, {"q": "Which of the following models accepts that the more deeply information is processed; the more likely it is to be retained?", "concept": "The answer is a: the levels of processing model holds that the more deeply information is processed, the better it is retained. Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972) argued against the idea that memory depends on which store an item sits in. Instead, retention depends on how deeply the item is analysed when it is encoded. Shallow (structural) processing attends to appearance ('Is the word in capital letters?'); intermediate (phonemic) processing attends to sound ('Does it rhyme with weight?'); deep (semantic) processing attends to meaning ('Does it fit the sentence: He met a ___ in the street?'). Fergus Craik and Endel Tulving (1975) showed that after such questions people remembered far more of the words they had judged for meaning than those judged for letter case, even though they had not been told to memorise anything. Elaboration, linking new material to what you already know or to yourself, deepens processing further. Critics noted that 'depth' is hard to measure except by the recall it is meant to explain, a circularity problem. Example: Riya reads a chapter on reinforcement twice and forgets it, while Arjun explains each idea in his own words with examples from his life; Arjun processes semantically and recalls more. Option a is right because 'deeper processing, better retention' is the central claim of Craik and Lockhart's model. Exam trap: the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) is the most famous memory model and tempts by familiarity, but it explains retention by transfer through stores and rehearsal, not by depth of processing.", "traps": {"b": "'Retrieval cues' is not a model of depth; it refers to hints that help bring back a memory, as in the encoding specificity principle of Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson (1973), which says a cue works best if it was present at encoding.", "c": "The Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968) multi-store model moves information from sensory memory to short-term to long-term memory, with rehearsal as the route into the long-term store; it explains retention by stores and rehearsal, not by depth of analysis.", "d": "Trace decay theory says memory traces fade with time unless they are used or rehearsed; it explains forgetting by the passage of time, not retention by depth of processing."}}, {"q": "The fact that a nearer object may obscure a more distant object from the view is due to:", "concept": "The answer is c: when a nearer object hides part of a more distant one, the depth cue is interposition. Interposition (also called overlap or occlusion) is a monocular depth cue: if one object blocks part of another, the visual system treats the blocking object as nearer. It is one of the pictorial cues that painters have used for centuries to put depth on a flat canvas, along with linear perspective, relative size, texture gradient, aerial perspective, shading and height in the visual field. Because it needs only one eye, it works in photographs and paintings. It gives only relative order (which object is in front), not distance in metres. Binocular cues, by contrast, need both eyes: retinal disparity (each eye receives a slightly different image) and convergence (the eyes turn inwards to fixate a near object). Example: at a cricket match, a spectator's head blocks part of the scoreboard for Arjun, and he knows at once that the head is closer, without judging any distance. Option c is right because the stem describes a nearer object obscuring a farther one, which is the definition of interposition. Exam trap: figure and ground, proximity and closure are also perception terms from the same chapter, but they are Gestalt principles for organising shapes into objects and groups, not cues for depth.", "traps": {"a": "Figure and ground is the Gestalt principle that we split a scene into an object (figure) standing out against a background (ground), shown by the vase-faces picture of Edgar Rubin (1915); it organises a scene but does not signal which object is nearer.", "b": "Proximity is the Gestalt law, set out by Max Wertheimer (1923), that elements close together are grouped as a unit, such as dots spaced in pairs; it concerns grouping, not depth.", "d": "Closure is the Gestalt tendency to fill gaps and see incomplete figures, such as a broken circle, as whole; it concerns completing form, not distance."}}, {"q": "A child's parents only allow her to play video games after she has completed all of her homework for that night. This reward system is consistent with", "concept": "The answer is a: allowing video games only after the homework is done is the Premack principle. David Premack (1959, 1965) proposed that reinforcement is relative: a behaviour a person prefers, that is, one they do often when free to choose (a high-probability behaviour), can reinforce a less preferred, low-probability behaviour. The reinforcer need not be food or praise; it can be an activity. In everyday terms this is 'grandma's rule': first eat your vegetables, then you can have dessert. In the stem, video games are the high-probability behaviour and homework the low-probability one, and access to the first is made contingent on finishing the second, so homework should increase. The order matters: the preferred activity must follow the less preferred one. Premack showed this with children who could either eat sweets or play a pinball machine: for children who preferred pinball, the chance to play reinforced eating sweets, and for those who preferred sweets, the reverse held. Teachers, parents and behaviour therapists use it widely because it needs no tangible reward. Option a is right because a preferred activity (gaming) is used to reinforce a less preferred one (homework), which is exactly the Premack principle. Exam trap: students sometimes reverse the rule; it is the high-probability behaviour that reinforces the low-probability behaviour, not the other way round, so 'homework first, then games' fits, while 'games first, then homework' would not.", "traps": {"b": "The Garcia effect is conditioned taste aversion (John Garcia and Robert Koelling, 1966): a single pairing of a taste with later nausea makes the taste disliked; it is a form of classical conditioning, not an activity used as a reward.", "c": "Observational learning (Albert Bandura, 1961 onward) is learning by watching a model's behaviour and its consequences; the child here is not learning by watching anyone.", "d": "Systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) pairs relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared situations to reduce anxiety; it is a fear treatment, not a reward rule for homework."}}, {"q": "The main difference between the Minnesota Multiphasic personality inventory (MMPI) and the California personality inventory (CPI) is that", "concept": "The answer is a: the CPI is more applicable to normal groups, while the MMPI targets psychopathology. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) was built by empirical criterion keying: a large pool of true-false items was given to psychiatric patient groups (such as depressed, schizophrenic or hypochondriacal patients) and to normal controls, and only items that separated a patient group from the controls were kept for its scale. Its clinical scales therefore carry diagnostic names (Depression, Hysteria, Paranoia, Schizophrenia), and it is used mainly in clinical settings. The California Psychological Inventory (Harrison Gough, 1956) used the same empirical method and borrowed many MMPI items, but it was keyed against normal criteria, such as people rated high or low in leadership or responsibility, to measure 'folk concepts', everyday traits recognised in all cultures: dominance, sociability, responsibility, self-control, tolerance and achievement. It is used in schools, counselling, personnel selection and leadership research, and is often called 'the normal person's MMPI'. Both are objective, self-report inventories with fixed answers scored by key, so neither is projective. Example: a hospital would give the MMPI to Rahul to check for depressive or psychotic features, whereas a company choosing managers would give Sneha the CPI to describe her dominance and sociability. Option a is right because the CPI was designed to describe normal personality. Exam trap: option b simply reverses the two tests; the MMPI is the one keyed against psychiatric patients, so it is the more clinical tool.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses the pair: the MMPI was keyed against psychiatric patient groups and its scales are named after disorders, so it is the clinical instrument, and the CPI is the one aimed at normal people.", "c": "The CPI is an objective, true-false self-report inventory scored with a fixed key; a projective test, such as the Rorschach or TAT, presents ambiguous stimuli and lets the person respond freely.", "d": "The MMPI is also an objective, true-false inventory scored by empirical keys, not a projective test; projective methods use ambiguous material such as inkblots or pictures."}}, {"q": "People are likely to invest less effort in a task when they are working with others. What is this phenomenon called?", "concept": "The answer is b: investing less effort when working with others on a shared task is social loafing. Social loafing is the tendency of individuals to exert less effort when their contributions are pooled into a group product than when they work alone. Its forerunner was the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913), who found that each person pulled a rope less hard as the team grew. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: students asked to clap or shout as loudly as they could made less noise per person in groups, even when they only believed that others were shouting with them, which showed that motivation was lost, not just coordination. Loafing happens because individual effort cannot be identified or evaluated, responsibility is spread across members, and people feel their own effort makes little difference. It shrinks when each person's output is identifiable, the task matters to them, the group is cohesive or each member has a distinct role. Example: in a group project, Kabir does very little because he knows the teacher will mark only the final report, not each member's part. Option b is right because the stem describes reduced individual effort in a group, the definition of social loafing. Exam trap: social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965) is the look-alike, but it runs the other way: the presence of others improves performance on simple, well-learned tasks, especially when each person's effort is visible.", "traps": {"a": "Social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965) is better performance on simple or well-learned tasks, and worse on complex ones, when others are present; it is about arousal from an audience or co-workers, not reduced effort on a pooled task.", "c": "Deindividuation (Leon Festinger and colleagues, 1952; Philip Zimbardo, 1969) is the loss of self-awareness and restraint in an anonymous crowd, which can release impulsive or aggressive acts; it is about losing identity, not slacking on a group task.", "d": "The sleeper effect (Carl Hovland and Walter Weiss, 1951) is the delayed rise in persuasion from a low-credibility source as the source is forgotten; it is an attitude change effect, not a group performance effect."}}, {"q": "Which of the following was most closely associated with the structuralist school of psychology?", "concept": "The answer is a: Edward Titchener is the name most closely tied to the structuralist school. Edward Bradford Titchener (1867 to 1927), an Englishman, trained with Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig and then led the psychology laboratory at Cornell University from 1892. He named the school structural psychology in his paper 'The postulates of a structural psychology' (1898), contrasting it with functional psychology. Structuralism aimed to break conscious experience into its basic elements, sensations, images and feelings (affections), describe their attributes (quality, intensity, duration, clearness) and find the laws by which they combine, much as a chemist analyses compounds into elements. Its method was trained introspection: observers were drilled to report raw sensations and to avoid the 'stimulus error' of naming the object instead. Titchener's laboratory manuals, Experimental Psychology (1901 to 1905), became standard guides. The school faded after his death, because introspective reports could not be checked and laboratories disagreed, and because it ignored children, animals, abnormal behaviour and practical use. Option a is right because Titchener founded, named and led structuralism. Exam trap: Wilhelm Wundt (who opened the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879) is often called the founder of structuralism, but Wundt called his own system voluntarism; the label 'structuralism' and the school itself belong to his student Titchener.", "traps": {"b": "William James (The Principles of Psychology, 1890) is the leading figure of functionalism, which asked what consciousness is for and how it helps us adapt, and he criticised the hunt for mental elements.", "c": "Rene Descartes (1596 to 1650) was the 17th-century French philosopher of mind-body dualism, innate ideas and the reflex; he worked two centuries before any school of psychology existed.", "d": "John B. Watson founded behaviourism (1913), which rejected introspection and consciousness altogether in favour of observable behaviour, the opposite of structuralism."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT categorized as a personality disorder according to the DSM IV?", "concept": "The answer is c: 'impulsive' is not one of the DSM-IV personality disorders. A personality disorder is an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from cultural expectations, is pervasive and inflexible, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, is stable over time and causes distress or impairment. DSM-IV (American Psychiatric Association, 1994) listed ten personality disorders on Axis II in three clusters. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive. A memory aid is 'weird, wild, worried'. Narcissistic, antisocial and borderline are therefore all genuine Cluster B disorders. Impulsivity is a trait and a criterion within borderline and antisocial personality disorder, not a disorder of its own. A separate DSM-IV chapter covered impulse-control disorders (intermittent explosive disorder, kleptomania, pyromania, pathological gambling, trichotillomania), which are not personality disorders. Example: Neha has had unstable relationships, self-harm and sudden mood swings since her teens; her clinician diagnoses borderline personality disorder and records impulsivity as one of its features, never 'impulsive personality disorder'. Option c is right because no DSM-IV personality disorder is called 'impulsive'. Exam trap: the word feels familiar because ICD-10 (World Health Organization, 1992) splits emotionally unstable personality disorder into an impulsive type and a borderline type; that is an ICD-10 subtype, not a DSM-IV disorder. Key note: ICD-10 does have an 'emotionally unstable personality disorder, impulsive type' (F60.30), but DSM-IV has no impulsive personality disorder, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Narcissistic personality disorder is a genuine DSM-IV Cluster B disorder, marked by grandiosity, need for admiration and lack of empathy, so it cannot be the 'not' answer.", "b": "Antisocial personality disorder is a genuine DSM-IV Cluster B disorder: a pervasive disregard for and violation of others' rights since age 15, diagnosed only at 18 or over, with conduct disorder before 15.", "d": "Borderline personality disorder is a genuine DSM-IV Cluster B disorder, marked by unstable relationships, self-image and emotions with marked impulsivity; impulsivity is one of its criteria, not a separate disorder."}}, {"q": "The ________ complex is to girls as the ________ complex is to boys.", "concept": "The answer is a: the Electra complex is to girls as the Oedipus complex is to boys. The stem pairs each blank with a sex: the first blank needs the complex named for girls, the second the complex named for boys. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) placed both in the phallic stage of psychosexual development (about 3 to 6 years), when pleasure centres on the genitals. He introduced the Oedipal idea in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900), after the Greek king Oedipus, who unknowingly killed his father and married his mother. In the Oedipus complex the boy unconsciously desires his mother and sees his father as a rival; fearing punishment (castration anxiety), he resolves the conflict by identifying with his father, which builds his superego and gender role. Carl Jung (1913) proposed the name Electra complex for the girl's version, after Electra, who helped avenge her father's murder on her mother. In this account the girl turns towards her father, partly through penis envy, sees her mother as a rival, and resolves the conflict by identifying with her mother. Freud himself rejected Jung's term and spoke of the female Oedipus attitude, but the Electra label stuck in textbooks. Both ideas are now criticised for lack of evidence and for their view of women; Karen Horney (1926) answered penis envy by pointing to men's envy of motherhood. Option a is right because it places Electra with girls and Oedipus with boys, in that order. Exam trap: option b uses the right two words in the wrong order, and the analogy form makes the reversal easy to miss, so check which blank goes with girls.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses the pair: the Oedipus complex is the boy's version and the Electra complex the girl's, so 'Oedipus is to girls as Electra is to boys' is backwards.", "c": "Oral and phallic are names of Freud's psychosexual stages (birth to about 1 year, and about 3 to 6 years), not complexes; both complexes arise within the phallic stage.", "d": "Phallic and oral are again stage names, here in reverse developmental order; neither is a complex tied to girls or to boys."}}, {"q": "Allport believed that traits could be organized into three levels:", "concept": "The answer is d: Gordon Allport organised traits into cardinal, central and secondary traits. Gordon Allport (1937), in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, treated traits as real neuropsychic structures inside the person that make behaviour consistent across situations. He ranked a person's traits by how pervasive they are, and in Pattern and Growth in Personality (1961) he renamed these individual traits 'personal dispositions'. A cardinal trait dominates almost everything a person does, so much that the person becomes known by it, as in Machiavellian, narcissistic or Christlike; few people have one. Central traits are the 5 to 10 broad qualities that describe most people well, the words you would use in a reference letter: honest, warm, ambitious, anxious. Secondary traits are narrower, less consistent dispositions that appear only in particular situations, such as a food preference or irritability in traffic. For example, Neha's central traits might be friendly, hard-working and talkative, while a secondary trait is that she becomes nervous only when speaking on stage; she has no single cardinal trait. Allport studied individuals in depth (the idiographic approach), for instance in Letters from Jenny (1965). Option d is right because cardinal, central and secondary are Allport's own three levels. Exam trap: 'secondary' also appears in option a, which makes 'primary, secondary, tertiary' look familiar; but Allport's three levels do not include primary or tertiary, and 'primary' belongs to Raymond Cattell's (1949) 16 primary (first-order) factors, a different model.", "traps": {"a": "Primary, secondary and tertiary is a generic ranking (used, for example, for levels of prevention); it is not Allport's terminology, and Raymond Cattell's 'primary' factors belong to a different model.", "b": "Cognitive, emotional and physiological describe the components of an emotional or anxiety response, not levels of personality traits.", "c": "Id, ego and superego are Sigmund Freud's structures of the psyche (The Ego and the Id, 1923), part of psychoanalytic theory, not a trait hierarchy."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five traits?", "concept": "The answer is a: sense of humour is not one of the Big Five traits. The Big Five, or five-factor model, holds that most differences in personality can be summed up by five broad dimensions, remembered as OCEAN: openness to experience (imaginative and curious versus conventional), conscientiousness (organised and dependable versus careless), extraversion (sociable and energetic versus reserved), agreeableness (warm and cooperative versus antagonistic) and neuroticism (anxious and moody versus emotionally stable). The model grew from the lexical hypothesis, the idea that important traits become words in every language. Factor analyses of trait ratings by Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) and Warren Norman (1963) kept finding five factors, and Lewis Goldberg (1981) named them the Big Five, 'big' meaning broad. Paul Costa and Robert McCrae reached the same five through questionnaires and built the NEO Personality Inventory (1985) and its revision, the NEO-PI-R (1992). The five appear across many cultures and are partly heritable. Sense of humour is a real and valued quality, but it is a narrow characteristic that relates partly to extraversion and openness rather than a separate dimension. Example: Kavya is talkative and outgoing (high extraversion), punctual and tidy (high conscientiousness) and very funny; her humour shows up within her extraversion and openness scores, not on a scale of its own. Option a is right because humour is not one of the OCEAN dimensions. Exam trap: examiners slip in a pleasant everyday trait word and rely on students who know only some of the five; a near-miss such as 'emotional stability' is genuine (it is the low pole of neuroticism), but humour is not a factor at all.", "traps": {"b": "Openness to experience (imagination, curiosity, love of new ideas and experiences) is one of the five OCEAN factors, so it is a genuine Big Five trait.", "c": "Conscientiousness (organisation, dependability, self-discipline) is one of the five factors and a strong predictor of school and job performance, so it is a genuine Big Five trait.", "d": "Extraversion (sociability, assertiveness, positive emotion) is a Big Five factor, also found in the model of Hans Eysenck (1947), so it is a genuine trait."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT an example of primary prevention of mental illness?", "concept": "The answer is c: Narcotics Anonymous is not primary prevention, because it serves people who are already addicted. Gerald Caplan (1964), in Principles of Preventive Psychiatry, applied the public health levels of prevention to mental illness. Primary prevention stops a disorder from starting, lowering its incidence (new cases) in the whole population or in at-risk groups, through education, prenatal care, nutrition, stress reduction and genetic counselling. Secondary prevention detects and treats cases early, shortening them and lowering prevalence, as in screening schoolchildren for depression. Tertiary prevention reduces disability and relapse in people who are already ill, through rehabilitation, aftercare and self-help groups. Narcotics Anonymous, founded in 1953 on the twelve-step model of Alcoholics Anonymous, is a self-help fellowship for people already dependent on drugs, helping them stay abstinent and rebuild their lives: relapse prevention, which is tertiary. The other three options act before any disorder exists. DARE (Drug Abuse Resistance Education), begun in Los Angeles schools in 1983, teaches children to resist drugs before they start using them. Prenatal health care protects the foetus from infection, malnutrition, alcohol and birth complications that can cause intellectual disability. Genetic screening with counselling identifies carriers and risks so that a disorder can be avoided. Example: Rohan, recovering from heroin dependence and attending weekly NA meetings, is receiving tertiary prevention; his younger sister, in a DARE class at school, is receiving primary prevention. Option c is right because NA begins only after addiction exists. Exam trap: DARE tempts as the 'not primary' answer because evaluations found it had little effect on later drug use, but effectiveness does not change its level: it still targets children who are not yet using.", "traps": {"a": "DARE (1983) is a school programme that teaches children to refuse drugs before they begin using them, which is primary prevention by design, whatever its later evaluations showed.", "b": "Prenatal health care (nutrition, infection control, avoiding alcohol and other teratogens) stops conditions such as intellectual disability from arising, the classic example of primary prevention.", "d": "Genetic screening, with counselling, identifies carriers and at-risk pregnancies so that a disorder can be avoided before it begins, which is primary prevention in Gerald Caplan's scheme."}}, {"q": "Which of the following hormone is being secreted by adrenal gland:", "concept": "The answer is c: the adrenal gland secretes both cortisol and epinephrine. Each adrenal gland sits on top of a kidney and is really two glands in one. The outer adrenal cortex makes steroid hormones: aldosterone (a mineralocorticoid that keeps salt and water balance), cortisol (a glucocorticoid) and small amounts of sex hormones (androgens). Cortisol is the main hormone of the slower stress system, the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: the hypothalamus releases CRH, the pituitary releases ACTH, and ACTH makes the adrenal cortex release cortisol, which raises blood sugar, dampens inflammation and helps the body sustain a long stress response; its level peaks in the morning. So option a qualifies. The inner adrenal medulla is in effect part of the sympathetic nervous system: when sympathetic nerves fire, it releases epinephrine (adrenaline) and some norepinephrine into the blood within seconds, raising heart rate, blood pressure and blood sugar for the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915). So option b qualifies too. Example: when a dog rushes at Meera, epinephrine from her adrenal medulla sets her heart pounding at once; if her exam stress lasts for weeks, cortisol from her adrenal cortex stays raised. Option c is right because cortisol comes from the adrenal cortex and epinephrine from the adrenal medulla, so both are adrenal hormones. Exam trap: picking a or b alone feels safe because each is correct, but each is incomplete; when both listed hormones are adrenal, the combined option is the answer, and melatonin, the odd one out, is made by the pineal gland.", "traps": {"a": "Cortisol is indeed an adrenal hormone, from the adrenal cortex, but choosing it alone leaves out epinephrine from the adrenal medulla, so it is incomplete.", "b": "Epinephrine is indeed an adrenal hormone, from the adrenal medulla, but choosing it alone leaves out cortisol from the adrenal cortex, so it is incomplete.", "d": "Melatonin is secreted by the pineal gland, mainly at night under the control of the suprachiasmatic nucleus, to regulate sleep and circadian rhythm; it is not an adrenal hormone."}}, {"q": "Despite working fewer hours than her coworkers, Melanie receives a promotion and a raise. Instead of feeling happy, Melanie feels guilty. This is best explained by", "concept": "The answer is c: Melanie's guilt at being over-rewarded is best explained by equity theory. J. Stacy Adams (1963, 1965) proposed that people judge fairness by comparing the ratio of their outcomes (pay, promotion, praise) to their inputs (hours, effort, skill) with the same ratio for others. When the ratios match, the situation feels fair. When they do not, people feel distress: the under-benefited, who get less than they deserve, feel anger or resentment, and the over-benefited, who get more than they deserve, feel guilt. Elaine Walster (later Hatfield), Ellen Berscheid and G. William Walster (1973, 1978) extended the theory to close relationships and stressed that both kinds of inequity push people to restore equity, either actually (working harder, giving something back) or psychologically (persuading themselves that they really did contribute more). In the stem, Melanie's inputs (fewer hours) are lower than her coworkers', yet her outcomes (a promotion and a raise) are higher, so she is over-benefited, and the predicted feeling is guilt rather than pleasure. She might now stay late every evening to bring her inputs up to her outcomes. Option c is right because only equity theory predicts guilt from being over-rewarded. Exam trap: social exchange theory sounds right because it is also about rewards and costs, but it assumes people simply try to maximise rewards and minimise costs, so it would predict that Melanie feels happy; only equity theory says that too much reward is itself distressing.", "traps": {"a": "Social exchange theory (George Homans, 1958; John Thibaut and Harold Kelley, 1959) says people try to maximise rewards and minimise costs in relationships; on that view high rewards for low costs should please Melanie, not make her guilty.", "b": "Gain-loss theory (Elliot Aronson and Darwyn Linder, 1965) says we like people most when their evaluation of us moves from negative to positive; it concerns changing liking for an evaluator, not guilt about unfair reward.", "d": "The foot-in-the-door phenomenon (Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, 1966) is a compliance tactic in which agreeing to a small request makes people likelier to agree to a larger one; it says nothing about feelings after a promotion."}}, {"q": "Which type of memory is being tested when you take a multiple-choice test?", "concept": "The answer is b: a multiple-choice test measures recognition memory. Memory researchers measure retention in three main ways. In recall you must retrieve the information yourself, with no options in front of you: free recall (in any order), serial recall (in the original order) or cued recall (from a hint). In recognition you only have to identify the correct item among alternatives you are shown, as in a multiple-choice or true-false test, or a witness picking out a suspect in an identity parade. In the savings (relearning) method, developed by Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885), you relearn material learned earlier and measure how much less time, or how many fewer trials, it takes the second time. Recognition is usually easier than recall, because the correct answer is presented as a ready-made retrieval cue: you need only judge whether it feels familiar, rather than search memory and produce the item. That is why people often recognise the name of a schoolmate they could not have recalled unaided. Example: in a history exam, Meera cannot write the year of the Battle of Plassey in the essay section, but when the multiple-choice section shows 1757 among four years, she picks it at once. Option b is right because choosing the correct answer from given options is recognition. Exam trap: free recall is the near-miss because it is also a memory test, but recall requires producing the answer with no options, as in an essay or fill-in-the-blank question; once options are shown, the task becomes recognition.", "traps": {"a": "The method of loci, attributed to the Greek poet Simonides of Ceos, is a mnemonic for encoding items by imagining them along a familiar route; it is a memory aid, not a kind of memory that a multiple-choice paper tests.", "c": "The savings method (Hermann Ebbinghaus, 1885) measures how much faster material is relearned the second time; a multiple-choice test involves no relearning.", "d": "Free recall means retrieving items in any order with no cues or options, as in writing down every word you remember from a list; multiple-choice items supply the options, so they test recognition instead."}}, {"q": "A high school teacher walks around the room more and speaks more clearly when being observed by her principal. This is an example of", "concept": "The answer is b: the teacher who works harder only while her principal watches is showing the Hawthorne effect. The Hawthorne effect is a change in behaviour that happens because people know they are being observed or studied, whatever the actual intervention. It takes its name from the Western Electric Company's Hawthorne Works near Chicago, where studies from 1924 to 1932 examined worker productivity. In the illumination experiments, output rose when the lighting was brightened, but it also rose in the control group, and sometimes even when the lights were dimmed, suggesting that the researchers' attention mattered more than the lighting. The later studies were associated with Elton Mayo (1880 to 1949) and reported by Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson in Management and the Worker (1939); Henry Landsberger coined the term 'Hawthorne effect' in 1958. Later reanalyses found the original data weaker than the legend, but the lesson remains central to research design: being observed is itself a variable. Researchers reduce it with unobtrusive measures, longer observation so that people get used to the observer, and control groups that receive the same attention. In the stem, the teacher walks around more and speaks more clearly only when observed, so the change is caused by being watched, not by any training. Option b is right because behaviour that changes through awareness of being observed is the Hawthorne effect. Exam trap: the placebo effect is the closest look-alike because both are expectation effects, but placebo improvement comes from believing one has received a treatment, while the Hawthorne effect comes from knowing one is being watched.", "traps": {"a": "The placebo effect is improvement that follows from believing one has received an active treatment, such as feeling better after a sugar pill; the teacher has received no treatment, she is simply being watched.", "c": "The availability heuristic (Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, 1973) is judging how likely something is by how easily examples come to mind; it is a thinking shortcut, not a change in behaviour under observation.", "d": "A double-blind experiment is a design in which neither participants nor experimenters know who receives which condition, used to control expectancy effects such as placebo and experimenter bias; it is a research procedure, not an effect shown by the teacher."}}, {"q": "A false belief maintained despite contradictory evidence or experience is an", "concept": "The answer is b: a false belief that is held firmly despite contradictory evidence or experience is a delusion. A delusion is a disorder of thought content, not of perception. Karl Jaspers (1913), in General Psychopathology, gave three classic marks: the belief is held with extraordinary conviction (subjective certainty), it is impervious to counter-argument and to contrary experience (incorrigibility), and its content is false or impossible. Modern definitions add that the belief is not explained by the person's culture or religion, so a belief shared by one's community, such as belief in the evil eye, is not a delusion. Common types are persecutory, referential, grandiose, nihilistic, jealous, erotomanic and somatic delusions; they are typical of schizophrenia, delusional disorder and psychotic mood episodes. For example, Arjun is convinced that his neighbours have hidden a camera in his ceiling fan to read his thoughts. An electrician opens the fan in front of him and finds nothing, yet Arjun says the camera was removed the night before. The evidence does not shake the belief, which is exactly what makes it delusional. Option b is right because the stem gives the core definition of a delusion: false, fixed and unaffected by evidence. Exam trap: hallucination is the commonest distractor. A hallucination is a perception without an adequate external stimulus (a voice in an empty room), whereas a delusion is a belief. A patient may have both, and may build a delusion to explain a hallucination, but they are different phenomena. Obsessions are also thoughts, but they are intrusive, and the person recognises them as their own and usually as senseless, so they are not held as fixed truths.", "traps": {"a": "A compulsion is a repetitive act or mental act (handwashing, checking, counting) that a person feels driven to perform to reduce anxiety, as in OCD; it is a behaviour, not a false belief.", "c": "A hallucination is a perception without an adequate external stimulus, such as hearing voices; it is a disorder of perception, whereas the stem describes a disorder of belief.", "d": "An obsession is an intrusive, unwanted thought, image or urge that the person recognises as a product of their own mind and usually resists; insight is kept, so it is not a fixed false belief."}}, {"q": "Single form of the test is administered twice on the same sample with a reasonable time gap is called as?", "concept": "The answer is b: giving a single form of a test twice to the same sample, with a reasonable time gap, measures test-retest reliability. Reliability is the consistency of a test's scores. In classical test theory an observed score is a true score plus random error, and reliability tells us how much of the score variation is true variation. In the test-retest method the same people take the same test on two occasions and the two sets of scores are correlated; the correlation is called the coefficient of stability. The time gap matters. If it is too short, people remember their answers and gain from practice, so the coefficient is inflated. If it is too long, the trait itself may change (children's abilities grow, moods shift), so the coefficient falls for reasons that are not measurement error. A gap of a few weeks is common for ability tests. The method suits stable traits such as intelligence, not passing states such as today's anxiety. For example, Meera gives a vocabulary test to 60 college students in January and the same test to the same students in February; a correlation of 0.88 between the two sessions shows the scores are stable over time. Option b is right because the stem names all three defining features: one form, the same sample and two administrations separated in time. Exam trap: alternate (parallel) form reliability also tests the same people twice, but with two equivalent forms of the test, giving a coefficient of equivalence; the words 'single form' rule it out.", "traps": {"a": "Validity is whether a test measures what it claims to measure (content, criterion-related and construct validity); retesting the same people shows consistency, not accuracy.", "c": "Internal consistency reliability (split-half with the Spearman-Brown formula, the Kuder-Richardson formulas, Lee Cronbach's alpha) needs only one administration and checks whether the items hang together.", "d": "Alternate form reliability gives two equivalent forms of the test to the same people; the stem specifies a single form given twice."}}, {"q": "The therapies that use behaviour modification techniques but also incorporate procedure designed to change maladaptive beliefs are called", "concept": "The answer is b: therapies that use behaviour modification techniques and also add procedures to change maladaptive beliefs are cognitive behaviour therapies. Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) grew from two streams. Behaviour therapy, built on conditioning research and on Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation, changes behaviour directly through exposure, reinforcement, modelling and skills training. The cognitive stream holds that the way we interpret events shapes how we feel and act. Albert Ellis (1955) founded rational therapy, later renamed rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), which disputes irrational beliefs such as 'I must be approved of by everyone'. Aaron Beck developed cognitive therapy in the early 1960s, targeting automatic thoughts and distortions such as catastrophising (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, 1979). Donald Meichenbaum (1977) joined self-instruction training to behavioural practice in cognitive behaviour modification. CBT combines the two streams: thought records and Socratic questioning challenge the beliefs, while behavioural experiments, homework, activity scheduling and graded exposure change what the person does. For example, Kabir avoids speaking in class because he believes 'everyone will think I am stupid'. With his therapist he rates and questions this thought, then gives a two-minute talk to test what really happens. Option b is right because the stem names both ingredients, behaviour techniques plus belief change, which is the definition of CBT. Exam trap: option a is the near-miss. Pure behaviour therapy treats observable behaviour and its learning history and does not set out to change beliefs; once cognitive restructuring is added, the therapy is called cognitive behavioural.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviour therapies (Wolpe's desensitisation, exposure, token economies based on B. F. Skinner's operant principles) change behaviour through learning principles alone; they do not add procedures aimed at maladaptive beliefs.", "c": "Psychoanalytic therapy (Sigmund Freud) seeks insight into unconscious conflict through free association, dream analysis and interpretation of transference, not behaviour modification.", "d": "Dynamic (psychodynamic) therapies are usually shorter, face-to-face descendants of psychoanalysis that still work through insight into unconscious conflict and relationship patterns, not behaviour modification techniques."}}, {"q": "Sympathetic nerves are projected from……", "concept": "The answer is c: sympathetic nerves arise from the thoracic and lumbar sections of the spinal cord. The autonomic nervous system has two main divisions. The sympathetic division prepares the body for action; Walter Cannon (1915) called its emergency role the fight-or-flight response: the heart races, pupils widen, airways open and blood shifts to the muscles. Its preganglionic cell bodies lie in the lateral grey horn of spinal segments T1 to L2 (some books say L3), so it is called the thoracolumbar outflow. Their short axons leave through the ventral roots and synapse mostly in the sympathetic chain, a row of ganglia beside the spine, from which long postganglionic fibres run to the organs. Some fibres go straight to the adrenal medulla, which releases adrenaline into the blood. The parasympathetic division (rest and digest) is the craniosacral outflow: cranial nerves III, VII, IX and X (the vagus carries most of it) plus sacral segments S2 to S4, with ganglia near or inside the organs. For example, when Rohan steps off a kerb and a bus horn blasts beside him, his pounding heart and dry mouth come from sympathetic fibres that leave the upper thoracic segments of his spinal cord. Option c is right because thoracic-lumbar is the textbook location of the sympathetic preganglionic neurons. Exam trap: option b sounds like the parasympathetic pattern but is garbled; the parasympathetic outflow is cranial-sacral (brainstem plus sacral cord), and 'cervical-sacral' matches neither division.", "traps": {"a": "The brain gives rise to the cranial nerves, and the cranial part of the autonomic outflow (nerves III, VII, IX and X) is parasympathetic, not sympathetic.", "b": "Cervical-sacral is not an autonomic outflow; it garbles 'craniosacral', the parasympathetic pattern (brainstem plus S2 to S4). Cervical segments contain no sympathetic preganglionic neurons.", "d": "The sympathetic chain extends up into the neck, but sympathetic preganglionic neurons sit only in segments T1 to L2; cervical and sacral segments have none, so 'all the sections' is wrong."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a standard physiological measure of sleep?", "concept": "The answer is c: the electrocardiogram is not one of the standard physiological measures used to define sleep. Sleep is studied in a laboratory by polysomnography, the overnight recording of several body signals at once. Three signals are needed to score the stages of sleep, as set out in the scoring manual of Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968) and kept in the American Academy of Sleep Medicine rules (2007). The electroencephalogram (EEG) records brain waves from the scalp: alpha in relaxed waking, theta in stage N1, sleep spindles and K-complexes in N2, slow delta waves in N3, and a fast, desynchronised pattern in REM sleep. The electrooculogram (EOG) records eye movements; this is how Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered rapid eye movement sleep. The electromyogram (EMG), usually from the chin, records muscle tone, which falls to near zero during REM (REM atonia). Together they separate the stages: REM, for instance, shows a waking-like EEG, rapid eye movements and a silent EMG. When Ananya spends a night in a sleep clinic, the technician fixes electrodes on her scalp, beside her eyes and under her chin before she sleeps. Option c is right because the ECG measures the heart's electrical activity, which is not used to stage sleep. Exam trap: clinical polysomnography often adds an ECG channel, along with breathing, blood oxygen and leg movements, to detect problems such as sleep apnoea. That extra channel is a monitoring add-on; the three standard measures of sleep itself remain EEG, EOG and EMG.", "traps": {"a": "The electrooculogram records eye movements and is essential for detecting REM sleep (Aserinsky and Kleitman, 1953), so it is a standard measure.", "b": "The electromyogram records muscle tone, usually at the chin; its fall to near zero marks REM sleep, so it is a standard measure.", "d": "The electroencephalogram records brain waves and is the core measure of sleep stages (alpha, theta, spindles, K-complexes, delta), so it is standard."}}, {"q": "Explicit or Declarative memory consist of which of the following types of memories?", "concept": "The answer is d: explicit (declarative) memory includes episodic, semantic and autobiographical memory. Long-term memory is commonly divided into two systems. Neal Cohen and Larry Squire (1980) contrasted 'knowing that' (declarative) with 'knowing how' (procedural), and Squire later built the standard taxonomy. Declarative or explicit memory holds information that can be consciously recalled and put into words; nondeclarative or implicit memory shows itself in performance without conscious recollection (procedural skills, priming, classical conditioning). Endel Tulving (1972) had already divided this kind of memory into two parts. Episodic memory stores personal events tied to a time and place, such as what you ate at your cousin's wedding last May. Semantic memory stores facts and general knowledge, free of the context in which they were learned: that Delhi is the capital of India, or what 'neuron' means. Autobiographical memory is memory for one's own life story. It is built mostly from episodic memories (specific remembered events) plus personal semantic facts (one's birthplace, the name of one's school), and both of these are declarative, so it belongs to explicit memory as well. For example, Riya can describe her first day at college (episodic), state that her college is in Pune (semantic) and narrate how her interest in psychology grew over the years (autobiographical); she can put all three into words. Option d is right because each listed type can be consciously retrieved and stated, which is what defines declarative memory. Exam trap: procedural memory, such as riding a bicycle, is the classic implicit memory; had it appeared in the list, 'all of the above' would fail.", "traps": {"a": "Episodic memory (Tulving, 1972) is declarative, but choosing it alone leaves out semantic and autobiographical memory, which are declarative too.", "b": "Semantic memory is declarative, but choosing it alone leaves out episodic and autobiographical memory, which are also explicit.", "c": "Autobiographical memory is declarative, but it is built from episodic and semantic memory; choosing it alone leaves out those two basic declarative systems."}}, {"q": "Psychotherapy is the treatment, by psychological means, of problems of an emotional nature in which a trained person deliberately establishes a professional relationship with the patient. Definition given by?", "concept": "The answer is b: this definition of psychotherapy was given by Lewis Wolberg. Lewis Wolberg (1954), an American psychiatrist, wrote The Technique of Psychotherapy, a standard training text. His full definition says that psychotherapy is 'the treatment, by psychological means, of problems of an emotional nature in which a trained person deliberately establishes a professional relationship with the patient' with the object of removing, modifying or retarding existing symptoms, mediating disturbed patterns of behaviour and promoting positive personality growth and development. Each phrase does work. 'By psychological means' separates psychotherapy from drugs, surgery or electroconvulsive therapy. 'Problems of an emotional nature' sets the target. 'A trained person' who 'deliberately establishes a professional relationship' separates therapy from friendly advice. The three aims run from relieving symptoms, through changing behaviour patterns, to growth of the whole personality. For example, when Sneha's friend comforts her after a failed exam, that is support, not psychotherapy; when a trained psychologist meets her weekly with agreed goals to reduce her exam panic, Wolberg's definition fits. Option b is right because the quoted words are the opening of Wolberg's well-known definition, which is widely quoted in textbooks. Exam trap: Carl Rogers (1951) is tempting because he placed the therapeutic relationship at the centre of change, but his famous statement is the necessary and sufficient conditions for change (1957), not this definition. David Wechsler (1939) is a test author whose well-known definition is of intelligence, not psychotherapy.", "traps": {"a": "David Wechsler, author of the Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939), defined intelligence as the aggregate or global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment; he did not define psychotherapy.", "c": "Carl Rogers founded client-centred therapy (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951) and named the necessary and sufficient conditions of change (empathy, unconditional positive regard, congruence); the quoted definition is not his.", "d": "Aaron Beck founded cognitive therapy, built on automatic thoughts, schemas and cognitive distortions; he is not the author of this general definition of psychotherapy."}}, {"q": "Self-Efficacy is", "concept": "The answer is d: self-efficacy is a set of beliefs that one can perform adequately in a particular situation. Albert Bandura (1977) introduced the idea in the Psychological Review paper 'Self-efficacy: toward a unifying theory of behavioral change', as part of what became his social cognitive theory. Self-efficacy is not a general opinion of oneself; it is a task-specific expectation: 'Can I do this, here?' A student may have high self-efficacy for mathematics and low self-efficacy for public speaking. Bandura separated efficacy expectations (I can carry out the behaviour) from outcome expectations (the behaviour will bring the result). He named four sources of efficacy beliefs: mastery experiences (past successes, the strongest source), vicarious experience (seeing similar others succeed), verbal persuasion (encouragement) and physiological and emotional states (reading a racing heart as a sign of unreadiness). High self-efficacy predicts more effort, persistence after failure and better progress in therapy, and it is a key ingredient in health behaviour change. For example, Arjun is sure he will pass his driving test because he has driven safely with his father for months, yet he doubts he could cook a meal for ten guests; his self-efficacy differs by task. Option d is right because it captures both features of Bandura's definition: a belief about one's capability, tied to a particular situation. Exam trap: option b describes self-esteem, a global evaluation of one's worth, which is often confused with self-efficacy; a person can have high self-esteem yet low self-efficacy for a specific task.", "traps": {"a": "A mental model of one's abilities and attitudes describes the self-concept or self-schema (Hazel Markus, 1977), a broad cognitive picture of the self, not a situation-specific belief about performing.", "b": "A generalised evaluative attitude toward the self that colours mood and behaviour is self-esteem (measured, for example, by Morris Rosenberg's 1965 scale); it is global, not task-specific.", "c": "The process of coming to know one's own attributes describes self-perception or self-knowledge, as in Daryl Bem's (1967) self-perception theory, where we infer our attitudes from our own behaviour."}}, {"q": "A forensic psychologist would be most likely to study:", "concept": "The answer is a: a forensic psychologist would be most likely to study the accuracy of eyewitness memory. Forensic psychology is the application of psychology to the law and the legal system. Its work includes assessing competence to stand trial and criminal responsibility (the insanity defence), assessing the risk of violence and reoffending, child custody evaluations, police interviewing, jury decision-making and the reliability of witnesses. Hugo Munsterberg (1908), in On the Witness Stand, argued early that memory and testimony should be tested scientifically. The best-known modern work is by Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer (1974). Students watched films of car accidents and were asked how fast the cars were going when they 'smashed', 'collided', 'bumped', 'hit' or 'contacted' each other. The stronger the verb, the higher the speed estimate, and a week later people asked with 'smashed' were more likely to falsely remember broken glass. Leading questions can reshape memory, which matters in every courtroom. For example, Vikram, a witness to a chain-snatching, is asked by police, 'Was the thief wearing the red jacket?', and later 'remembers' a red jacket that was never there. Option a is right because eyewitness accuracy is a question that directly serves the legal system, the defining territory of forensic psychology. Exam trap: options b, c and d are all real research topics, but each belongs to another applied or basic field (consumer, biological and educational psychology); only option a connects to the law.", "traps": {"b": "The impact of advertising on shopping behaviour is consumer psychology, a branch of applied and marketing psychology, not psychology applied to the law.", "c": "The effect of hormones on decision making is biological psychology (behavioural endocrinology); it studies body and brain, not the legal system.", "d": "Gender differences in learning styles belong to educational psychology and the psychology of gender; they have no direct legal application."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a defence mechanism?", "concept": "The answer is c: ingratiation is not a defence mechanism. Defence mechanisms, described by Sigmund Freud and systematised by Anna Freud (1936) in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence, are unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety by distorting or blocking awareness of unacceptable impulses or painful facts. Projection attributes one's own unacceptable feelings to someone else, as when a man who resents his colleague believes the colleague hates him. Regression is a retreat under stress to behaviour typical of an earlier stage, such as a seven-year-old who starts thumb-sucking and bedwetting after a sibling is born. Sublimation channels unacceptable impulses into socially valued activity, as when aggressive urges are poured into competitive sport; it is usually seen as the healthiest defence, and George Vaillant (1977) ranked it among the mature defences. Ingratiation, by contrast, is a social psychology term. Edward E. Jones (1964) analysed it as a deliberate impression-management tactic for winning someone's liking: flattery, agreeing with their opinions, doing favours. For example, Rahul praises his manager's dull presentation and agrees with every view she states because appraisals are next week. He knows exactly what he is doing; nothing is unconscious and no inner anxiety is being warded off. Option c is right because ingratiation is a conscious influence strategy aimed at other people, while the other three are unconscious ego defences. Exam trap: ingratiation sounds like introjection, which is a genuine defence (taking in another person's values or traits as one's own), so read the word carefully.", "traps": {"a": "Projection is a classic Freudian defence: one's own unacceptable feelings or wishes are attributed to someone else.", "b": "Regression is a Freudian defence: under stress the person returns to behaviour of an earlier developmental stage, such as thumb-sucking or tantrums.", "d": "Sublimation is a Freudian defence and one of the mature ones: unacceptable impulses are channelled into socially valued activities such as art, sport or work."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the third stage of Selye's general adaptation syndrome?", "concept": "The answer is b: exhaustion is the third stage of Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome. Hans Selye (1936), an endocrinologist working at McGill University in Montreal, exposed rats to many different noxious agents (cold, injury, toxic drugs, tissue extracts) and found the same triad every time: enlarged adrenal glands, shrunken immune tissue (thymus and lymph nodes) and stomach ulcers. He concluded that the body makes a nonspecific response to any demand, which he later called stress (The Stress of Life, 1956). The general adaptation syndrome (GAS) has three stages in a fixed order. In the alarm reaction the body first meets the stressor (a brief shock phase) and then mobilises: sympathetic nerves and the adrenal medulla release adrenaline, and the adrenal cortex begins to pour out cortisol. In the stage of resistance the body adapts to the continuing stressor; cortisol stays high and the person seems to cope, but resources are being spent. In the stage of exhaustion, if the stressor goes on, those resources run out, resistance collapses and illness follows, which Selye called diseases of adaptation (ulcers, high blood pressure, lowered immunity). For example, Pooja copes with three months of night shifts at a call centre (resistance), but in the fourth month she falls ill with one infection after another (exhaustion). Option b is right because exhaustion comes after alarm and resistance. Exam trap: resistance is the second stage, and the usual slip is swapping its order with exhaustion. 'Mobilization' is a plausible word because mobilising energy is part of the alarm reaction, but it is not a separate stage.", "traps": {"a": "Alarm (the alarm reaction) is the first stage of the GAS: the initial shock followed by sympathetic and adrenal mobilisation.", "c": "Resistance is the second stage of the GAS: the body adapts to a continuing stressor with sustained hormonal arousal.", "d": "Mobilization is not one of Selye's three stages; mobilising the body's resources happens within the alarm reaction."}}, {"q": "Visual cliff experiment demonstrated that", "concept": "The answer is b: the visual cliff experiment was taken to show that depth perception is most likely innate. Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960) built a visual cliff: a sheet of thick glass over a table. On one side a checkerboard pattern lay just under the glass (the shallow side); on the other the same pattern lay far below (the deep side), so the glass looked like a drop. A centre board ran between them. They tested 36 infants aged 6 to 14 months. Of the 27 who moved off the centre board, all crawled onto the shallow side when their mothers called, but only three crept onto the deep side; many peered down, cried or backed away even though they could feel the solid glass. Young animals that move about at or soon after birth, such as chicks, lambs and kid goats, also refused the deep side. Gibson and Walk therefore concluded that depth perception is present as soon as a creature can move about and is largely inborn. For example, baby Aarav, newly crawling and never having fallen off anything, stops at the edge of the deep side while his mother calls. Option b is right because the classic conclusion of the study is that depth perception is innate rather than learned from falls. Exam trap: option a reverses the conclusion, and options c and d swap in shape and size perception, which the cliff did not test. Key note: Joseph Campos and colleagues (1970) found that 2-month-olds placed on the deep side showed a slowed heart rate (interest, not fear), and later work tied fear of heights to crawling experience; since crawlers already have months of visual experience, the cliff cannot fully separate nature from learning. The standard conclusion of the original study is still b, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Saying depth perception is acquired reverses Gibson and Walk's conclusion; infants and newborn animals avoided the drop without having learned from falls, which is why the study is cited against the purely empiricist view.", "c": "Shape perception was not tested on the visual cliff; infant form perception was studied with looking-time methods such as Robert Fantz's preference tests, while the cliff manipulated only apparent depth.", "d": "Size perception (size constancy) was not tested on the visual cliff; the apparatus varied only the apparent drop beneath the glass."}}, {"q": "The structuralist intended to identify the building blocks of", "concept": "The answer is a: the structuralists aimed to identify the building blocks of consciousness. Structuralism was named by Edward Titchener (1898), an Englishman who trained with Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig and taught at Cornell University from 1892. He wanted to do for the mind what chemistry had done for matter: break conscious experience down into its basic elements, discover how they combine, and describe the structure that results. He named three elements: sensations (the elements of perception), images (the elements of ideas) and affections (feelings of pleasantness or unpleasantness). Each element had attributes such as quality, intensity, duration and clearness. The method was trained introspection: observers, after long practice, reported the raw elements of an experience while avoiding the 'stimulus error', that is, naming the object ('an apple') instead of describing the sensations ('red, round, smooth'). For example, asked to introspect on biting a mango, Neha would have to report sweetness, softness, coldness and a mild pleasantness, not simply 'a mango'. Because the method needed a trained, conscious observer, structuralism ignored the unconscious, children, animals and disturbed minds, and it faded after Titchener's death in 1927. Option a is right because consciousness, analysed into its elements, was the whole subject matter of structuralism. Exam trap: 'subconscious' and 'unconscious' belong to Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (from about 1900), which studied exactly what introspection could not reach; structuralism stands at the opposite pole.", "traps": {"b": "'Subconsciousness' is a psychoanalytic and popular term for mental activity just below awareness; structuralists studied only what could be introspected, so it lay outside their aim.", "c": "The unconscious was the subject of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis; Titchener's introspection could not reach it, and he did not try to.", "d": "None of the above fails because consciousness, option a, is exactly what the structuralists set out to analyse into elements."}}, {"q": "The smallest change in the stimulus that a person is able to detect:", "concept": "The answer is a: the smallest change in a stimulus that a person can detect is the DL, the difference limen. Psychophysics, founded by Gustav Fechner (1860) in Elements of Psychophysics, studies how physical stimuli relate to sensations, using thresholds (limens). The absolute limen (AL, absolute threshold) is the smallest intensity of a stimulus that can be detected at all, defined as detection on 50 percent of trials. The difference limen (DL, difference threshold) is the smallest change in intensity that can be detected, again on 50 percent of trials; the change itself is called the just noticeable difference (JND). Ernst Weber (1834) found that the DL is not a fixed amount but a constant fraction of the starting stimulus: delta I / I = k, now called Weber's law. For lifted weights the fraction is roughly 2 percent, so a 100 g weight must change by about 2 g to be noticed, but a 1,000 g weight by about 20 g. For example, Sana can feel the difference between a 100 g and a 102 g packet in her hand, but holding a 5 kg bag of rice she cannot feel an extra 2 g; it would take about 100 g. Option a is right because the DL is defined as the smallest detectable change in a stimulus. Exam trap: AL is the near-miss; it is the smallest detectable stimulus, not the smallest detectable change. Key note: Weber's law is about the DL (it states how the DL grows with the size of the stimulus), so it is related, but a law is not a threshold, and the AL is not about change at all; 'All of the above' therefore fails, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "AL, the absolute limen or absolute threshold, is the smallest intensity of a stimulus detected at all (50 percent of the time), not the smallest change in a stimulus.", "c": "Weber's law (Ernst Weber, 1834) is the rule that the DL is a constant proportion of the starting stimulus (delta I / I = k); it describes the DL but is not itself the threshold.", "d": "All of the above fails because the AL concerns detecting a stimulus, not a change, and Weber's law is a law about the DL rather than a measure of it."}}, {"q": "Rorschach ink blot test is based on which types of projective technique:", "concept": "The answer is c: the Rorschach inkblot test is an associative projective technique. Projective techniques present an ambiguous stimulus and assume that people project their own needs, conflicts and ways of thinking onto it. Gardner Lindzey (1959) sorted them by the kind of response they demand into five families. In association techniques the person reports the first word, image or percept the stimulus calls up: the inkblots of Hermann Rorschach (1921, Psychodiagnostik) and word association tests such as the Kent-Rosanoff list (Grace Kent and Aaron Rosanoff, 1910). In construction techniques the person builds a product, usually a story: the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935). In completion techniques the person finishes an incomplete stimulus: sentence completion blanks and the Rosenzweig Picture-Frustration Study. In choice or ordering techniques the person picks or arranges items, as in the Szondi test. In expressive techniques the person creates freely: Draw-a-Person, House-Tree-Person, finger painting, play and psychodrama. In the Rorschach the examiner shows ten symmetrical blots one by one and asks, 'What might this be?', and the person says what the blot brings to mind. For example, Tara looks at Card I and says 'a bat', then 'two dancers holding hands': she is associating to the blot, not building a story or finishing a sentence. Option c is right because the Rorschach response is an immediate association to an ambiguous blot, the defining feature of Lindzey's association category. Exam trap: construction (option a) is the home of the TAT, the other famous projective test, and examiners swap the two; remember 'Rorschach associates, TAT constructs'.", "traps": {"a": "Construction techniques ask the person to build a product, usually a story, as in the TAT (Morgan and Murray, 1935); the Rorschach asks what the blot looks like, not for a story.", "b": "Completion techniques ask the person to finish an incomplete stimulus, as in sentence completion tests and the Rosenzweig Picture-Frustration Study; a Rorschach blot is not an unfinished item to be completed.", "d": "Expressive techniques let the person create freely, as in Draw-a-Person, House-Tree-Person, play and psychodrama; in the Rorschach the person interprets a given blot rather than producing one."}}, {"q": "In Rorschach, Card no. 1,4,5,6,7 are representative of:", "concept": "The answer is b: Rorschach cards I, IV, V, VI and VII are achromatic. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published his inkblot test in Psychodiagnostik and died the following year, aged 37. The test has ten bilaterally symmetrical inkblots, always shown in the same order from Card I to Card X, and they fall into three colour groups. Five are achromatic, printed only in black and shades of grey: Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII. Two are black with bright red areas: Cards II and III, called partly chromatic or semi-chromatic in some Indian books. Three are fully chromatic, in pastel colours: Cards VIII, IX and X. So the deck splits into five achromatic and five coloured cards. Colour matters in scoring: responses that use colour (FC, CF, C) are read as signs of how a person handles emotion, responses that use the grey shading are linked to anxiety and the need for affection, and a sudden delay or rejection on the coloured cards was traditionally called colour shock. Card V is the one most often seen as a bat or butterfly. As a memory aid, Rahul remembers the grey cards as 'Card I, then the run from IV to VII', with the red cards sitting between them. Option b is right because Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII contain no colour, only black and grey. Exam trap: semi-chromatic (option c) is the label for Cards II and III, which carry red patches, and chromatic (option a) fits Cards VIII to X; mixing up the three groups is the usual error.", "traps": {"a": "Chromatic describes the fully coloured Cards VIII, IX and X (and, loosely, all five cards with colour); Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII have no colour.", "c": "Semi-chromatic (partly chromatic) is the label some books give to Cards II and III, which are black with red areas, not to the grey cards.", "d": "None of the above fails because the five listed cards are exactly the achromatic set."}}, {"q": "Computer provided a new way to conceptualise mental processes and to develop detail theories which is known as", "concept": "The answer is d: the approach in which the computer gave a new way to conceptualise mental processes and to build detailed theories is the information-processing approach. During the cognitive revolution of the 1950s psychologists saw that a computer takes in input, codes it, stores it, transforms it by rules and produces output, and that the mind might be described in the same terms. George Miller (1956) showed that short-term memory holds about seven, plus or minus two, chunks. Donald Broadbent (1958) drew one of the first flowcharts of the mind, with attention as a limited-capacity channel guarded by a filter. Allen Newell and Herbert Simon wrote computer programs, the Logic Theorist (1956) and the General Problem Solver (from 1957), that solved problems in humanlike steps. Ulric Neisser (1967) gathered the field in Cognitive Psychology. The approach yields box-and-arrow models with stages and capacity limits, such as the multi-store model of memory of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968): sensory register, short-term store, long-term store. For example, when Isha looks up a phone number and repeats it until she dials it, an information-processing account says the digits enter a sensory register, are encoded into short-term memory and are kept alive by rehearsal. Option d is right because the stem names the computer analogy itself, the defining idea of information processing. Exam trap: behaviourism (option b) refused to theorise about inner mental processes at all. Key note: information processing is the core framework of the wider cognitive approach, so option a is close; but the stem describes the computer as the model of the mind, which is the information-processing approach in particular, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The cognitive approach is the broad perspective that studies perception, memory, thinking and language; it contains information processing, but it is the wider family rather than the specific computer-analogy approach the stem describes.", "b": "The behavioural approach (John B. Watson, 1913; B. F. Skinner) studied only observable stimuli and responses and rejected theories of inner mental processes, the opposite of modelling the mind on a computer.", "c": "The Gestalt approach (Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Kohler, Kurt Koffka, from 1912) studied how perception is organised into wholes; it came before computers and does not use them as a model."}}, {"q": "A person who is impatient, compulsive, and competitive is likely to be diagnosed as having", "concept": "The answer is b: a person who is impatient, compulsive and competitive is best described as having a Type A personality. The idea came not from psychologists but from two San Francisco cardiologists, Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman (1959), who noticed that many of their heart patients were driven, hurried people. They described the Type A behaviour pattern: intense competitive achievement striving, a chronic sense of time urgency (doing several things at once, impatience with any delay), easily roused hostility and aggressiveness, and over-commitment to work. Type B people are relaxed, patient and easy-going. In their Western Collaborative Group Study of about 3,000 working men, Type A men developed coronary heart disease about twice as often as Type B men; later research found hostility, especially cynical hostility, to be the most harmful component. They popularised the idea in Type A Behavior and Your Heart (1974). For example, Vikram honks the moment a light turns green, eats lunch while answering emails, and turns a friendly game of carrom into a contest he must win; he fits the Type A pattern. Option b is right because impatience (time urgency), competitiveness and a driven, compulsive work style are the textbook marks of Type A. Exam trap: the word 'compulsive' points towards obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, but OCPD is about perfectionism, orderliness and control, not time urgency and competitiveness. Key note: Type A is a behaviour pattern, not a DSM or ICD diagnosis, so 'diagnosed' is loose wording; among these options it fits the description best, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder is a DSM Cluster C disorder of perfectionism, orderliness, rigidity and need for control; it is not defined by impatience and competitiveness.", "c": "Type B is Friedman and Rosenman's contrasting pattern: relaxed, patient, easy-going and free of time urgency, the opposite of the stem.", "d": "Machiavellianism (Richard Christie and Florence Geis, 1970) is a manipulative, cynical, exploitative style, one of the Dark Triad; it is not defined by impatience or time urgency."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT true about Ex Post Facto Research?", "concept": "The answer is a: of these statements, 'the dependent variable has already occurred' is the one that does not define ex post facto research. Ex post facto means 'after the fact'. Fred Kerlinger, in Foundations of Behavioral Research (first edition 1964), defined it as systematic empirical inquiry in which the scientist does not have direct control of independent variables because their manifestations have already occurred or because they are inherently not manipulable. So the defining features all concern the independent variable: it has already happened (b), the investigator cannot control or manipulate it (c), and the study is carried out after the event (d). The researcher compares groups that already differ on the IV, or traces an outcome back to possible causes, which is why the design is also called causal-comparative research. Smokers versus non-smokers for lung disease is the classic example. With no manipulation or random assignment, causal claims are weak; self-selection and the post hoc fallacy ('after this, therefore because of this') are its risks. For example, Neha compares the exam anxiety of students who did and did not attend coaching classes last year; she cannot assign anyone to coaching, so attendance is an ex post facto IV. Option a is right because the defining clause of ex post facto research is about the independent variable, not the dependent variable. Exam trap: b, c and d all sound like 'after the fact' and are true; the item tests which variable the 'already occurred' clause belongs to. Key note: Kerlinger notes that the researcher often starts from an observed dependent variable and looks back for causes, so a is not flatly false; but the defining feature is an IV that has already occurred and cannot be manipulated, and a is the statement the paper treats as not defining, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The independent variable having already occurred is the core of Kerlinger's definition, so this statement is true and cannot be the exception.", "c": "Lack of control over the independent variable (it has already happened or cannot be manipulated) is a defining feature, so this statement is true.", "d": "Ex post facto literally means 'after the fact': the study is done after the event has occurred, so this statement is true."}}, {"q": "How many clinical scales are there in Luria?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Luria-Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery has 11 clinical scales (Form I). Alexander Luria (1902 to 1977), the Russian neuropsychologist, assessed brain-injured patients qualitatively, watching exactly where and how a function broke down rather than using norms. Anne-Lise Christensen (1975) compiled his procedures into a set of test items, and Charles Golden, Thomas Hammeke and Arnold Purisch (1978; manual 1980) standardised them at the University of Nebraska into a scored battery with American norms. Form I has 269 items, each scored 0, 1 or 2, grouped into 11 clinical scales: motor functions, rhythm, tactile functions, visual functions, receptive speech, expressive speech, writing, reading, arithmetic, memory and intellectual processes. Scores are converted to T scores, and higher scores mean more impairment. Beyond the clinical scales there are summary scales (such as the pathognomonic, left hemisphere and right hemisphere scales) and localisation scales. Like the Halstead-Reitan, it is a fixed battery: every patient gets the same tests. A simple way to remember the eleven: Farhan groups them as body (motor, rhythm, tactile, visual), language (receptive speech, expressive speech, writing, reading) and thinking (arithmetic, memory, intellectual processes), 4 + 4 + 3 = 11. Option b is right because Form I, the standard version taught in textbooks, has 11 clinical scales. Exam trap: the MMPI has 10 clinical scales, which makes option a tempting; do not carry that number across to the Luria-Nebraska. Key note: Form II adds a twelfth clinical scale, intermediate memory, so 12 is defensible for that form, but the standard answer for 'the Luria' is Form I's 11, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "10 is the number of MMPI clinical scales (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley); Luria-Nebraska Form I has 11.", "c": "12 clinical scales belong to Form II of the Luria-Nebraska, which adds intermediate memory; the standard Form I battery has 11.", "d": "13 matches no version of the Luria-Nebraska: Form I has 11 clinical scales and Form II has 12."}}, {"q": "Which of the following methods is generally concerned with causal relationship?", "concept": "The answer is d: the laboratory experiment is the method generally concerned with causal relationships. To show that X causes Y, a researcher must show that X comes before Y, that the two vary together, and that nothing else explains the link. An experiment does this by manipulating the independent variable, randomly assigning participants to conditions and holding other variables constant, then measuring the dependent variable. The laboratory gives the tightest control over extraneous variables, so it has the highest internal validity, the confidence that the IV really caused the change in the DV. Fred Kerlinger (1964) set out four kinds of research: laboratory experiments, field experiments, field studies and survey research. A field experiment manipulates an IV in a real-life setting, as when Irving Piliavin and colleagues (1969) staged a collapse on New York subway trains to see who helped; it gains realism but loses control. A field study manipulates nothing and studies relations as they exist in a factory, school or community. A field survey uses questionnaires or interviews to describe how variables are distributed in a population. For example, Aditi tests whether caffeine speeds reaction time: she randomly gives half her participants coffee and half decaffeinated coffee in a quiet laboratory, keeps the room, time and instructions identical, and measures reaction time, so any difference can be traced to the caffeine. Option d is right because the laboratory experiment combines manipulation with maximum control, the conditions needed to infer cause. Exam trap: the word 'field' in three options hides the real distinction, which is manipulation plus control. Key note: a field experiment also manipulates the IV and can test causal relations, but with weaker control over extraneous variables, so the method generally concerned with causation is the laboratory experiment, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "A field experiment manipulates the IV in a natural setting (Piliavin's subway study), so it can test causes, but it has less control over extraneous variables than the laboratory, so causal inference is weaker.", "b": "A field study (Kerlinger's ex post facto field study) observes relations as they exist in real settings without manipulating any variable, so it shows association, not cause.", "c": "A field survey describes the distribution of attitudes or behaviours in a population through questionnaires or interviews; it neither manipulates nor controls variables."}}, {"q": "Which One of the following scales takes measure from true zero?", "concept": "The answer is d: the ratio scale is the scale whose measurements start from a true zero. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), in 'On the theory of scales of measurement' in Science, described four levels of measurement, each adding one property. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, blood group, jersey numbers); the numbers carry no order. An ordinal scale adds order (class rank, ratings of agreement), but the distances between ranks are not equal. An interval scale adds equal intervals, so differences are meaningful, but its zero is arbitrary: 0 degrees Celsius is just the freezing point of water, not an absence of temperature, so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20 degrees. IQ and most psychological test scores are treated as interval. A ratio scale adds a true (absolute) zero, where zero means none of the quantity: height, weight, reaction time, number of errors, temperature in kelvin. Only here do ratios make sense. For example, if Kiran answers in 400 milliseconds and Dev in 800 milliseconds, Dev took twice as long, a statement that is valid because 0 milliseconds means no time at all. The memory hook is NOIR: nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio, in rising order of information. Option d is right because a true zero is the defining feature of the ratio scale. Exam trap: the interval scale (option c) also has equal units and is often confused with ratio; the difference is that the interval zero is arbitrary, while the ratio zero is real.", "traps": {"a": "A nominal scale only labels categories (gender, blood group); it has no order, no equal units and no zero point.", "b": "An ordinal scale ranks cases (class rank, Likert-type agreement) but has unequal intervals and no true zero.", "c": "An interval scale has equal intervals but an arbitrary zero (Celsius, IQ scores), so 'twice as much' statements are not meaningful."}}]});
